div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1250956#post1250956\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">OOC: Hoo boy. So that went places I was absolutely not anticipating. I'm not normally someone who sets the penalty to failing a climbing roll above a lethal fall to be &quot;you fall&quot;, but Aed's player had run the numbers some time ago and figured that he could actually survive an impact at terminal velocity and actually wanted to see his character do it. So he turned the failure into a dramatic failure and things spiralled like crazy from there. I've not seen a session dictated quite so much by dice in a while, whether it be the failures (like Aaron and Aed both failing rolls to try and get Aaron's leap into darkness to land anywhere near where Aed was) or the successes (like Aed succeeding on an against-all-odds Intimidation check to turn the confrontation in the Chamber of Echos from something way beyond his ability to deal with into something almost laughably easy). I do seriously wish I'd found a way around splitting the party though, ending up in a position where Regan and Orla practically had to sit the session out and only Aed got to see any cool action was a real drag.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd be interested to see what some of those rolls were, for comparison.<br /> <br /> As to &quot;split the party&quot;, I suppose there wasn't time to give them their own &quot;side-quest&quot; to handle, like the escaped air spirits, or fending off the police investigation?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1250956#post1250956\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Full credit to <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/38-cinder\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar38_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"38\" data-vbnamecard=\"38\">Cinder</a> for the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/815580-suthilu-the-bat-host\">Suthilu</a>, by the way. Those things are awesome. After reading up on them I basically rewrote pretty much everything host-related that I had for the setting to change the focus much more towards them instead of the originally intended Beshilu. Which all took place either the week before or the week after the campaign actually started, so that was a... a lot of work. Worth it though. It does lose out on a little bit of dark mirroring when your Hunter In Darkness is a priest fighting a Beshilu Minister, but I've gone with a kind of Suthilu prophet to retain a bit of that particular fun. And on the plus side, the Suthilu are perfectly suited to both the geography of Belfast (so many mountains filled with caves for the Colony) and the times the players are dealing with, with the added fun of being something unexpected. Father Aaron's player was all but nodding along and saying &quot;Beshilu. Yep. Definitely Beshilu&quot; throughout this entire hunt and it was nice to throw a bit of a curveball.</div> </div> </div> </div>Curve-balls are always fun. And the Bat-Host are a blast.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1250956#post1250956\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Finally, I'm off on a holiday for the next few weeks, so it'll be a bit over a month before we get to play another session. I'm counting the days <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Have fun!<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-09-03T23:14:36-07:00","id":1251284,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cinder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1251064#post1251064\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hooray, my babies! I was already enjoying this Actual Play, but now I'm personally involved <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Seriously though, glad you liked them and thanks for giving them a place in this cool game</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you for writing them! I'm really hoping I can do them justice. They haven't quite had a chance to flex their wings yet, so to speak, though ramifications for what the Herald managed to get up to while wearing Ciara should start to come through over the next few in-game days.<br /> <br /> Mind you, the pace of the game is such that it could still take 5-6 more sessions for that to come up... Since a hunt has just concluded and provided the players don't see the need to immediately follow up with Eaven and the Whispers we might be able to take a breather on the tight pacing, at least once Christmas has come and gone.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1251076#post1251076\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Dun, dun, DUN!</div> </div> </div> </div><img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1251076#post1251076\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Aww, that's actually kinda endearing.</div> </div> </div> </div>Tell me about it. Aed's in character ramble at this near-inert spirit went on for a while. Between that and his reaction to his entourage being discorporated by his fall and it was quite the session for showing Aed's soft side towards spirits, particularly weak ones, and his general fondness for animism.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1251076#post1251076\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh, come on! It doesn't seem like Aaron actually did/does anything to protect them. Aed and Regan actually were the ones who bothered (Dramatic Failure aside). I'd expect a Hunter In Darkness to have at least a little bit of an intelligence network in the area.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well Aaron was in the middle of a sermon at the time and kind of had to rely on them as proxies. But he doe shave the intelligence network you mentioned. Gloria, the local gossip, springs to mind as someone Aaron was able to bring in to help prepare for this hunt.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1251076#post1251076\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd be interested to see what some of those rolls were, for comparison.</div> </div> </div> </div>What do you mean? Like the number of dice rolled, or just what I was asking them to roll in terms of attributes/skills?<br /> <br /> From memory the Intimidation check was under quite a number of penalties, so Aed was rolling a single dice (not a chance dice) on the Presence + Intimidation attempt.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1251076#post1251076\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As to &quot;split the party&quot;, I suppose there wasn't time to give them their own &quot;side-quest&quot; to handle, like the escaped air spirits, or fending off the police investigation?</div> </div> </div> </div>That would have just exacerbated the problem in my view. If I try to balance one player going off on his own by giving each player something to do on their own, that just extends the amount of time the players spend playing without each other. Does that make sense? I'd rather just wrap up the individual events quickly and get back to group play.<br /> <br /> It's a tough thing to reconcile short of just expecting my players to stick together, since I do aim for a sandbox vibe rather than any kind of story path to keep the players moving as one. Luckily the players themselves are usually pretty good at it, when they're not trying to see how far they can fall and live.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1251076#post1251076\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Have fun!</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-10-11T07:30:10-07:00","id":1260127,"title":"","content":"So now that the Storyteller's Vault is open to CofD, any chance you would write up this chronicle as an SAS once you finish playing it?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><a href=\"https://tinyurl.com/2p8ss75f\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Chris H</b></a>/<b>EndDaysEngine</b> | <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/EndDaysEngine\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Patreon</b></a></span>| <b>He/His </b>| <b>Currently Writing</b>: <i>[Redacted], [Redacted]</i><br /> <br /> <b>CofD booklists: </b><a href=\"https://goo.gl/DRdB7h\" target=\"_blank\">Beast</a> I <a href=\"https://goo.gl/x1FPpM\" target=\"_blank\">Changeling</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/ZSCx5U\" target=\"_blank\">Demon</a> | <a href=\"http://shorturl.at/syDK9\" target=\"_blank\">Deviant</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/Mh5M6m\" target=\"_blank\">Geist</a> l <a href=\"https://goo.gl/gSjfm5\" target=\"_blank\">Hunter</a> l <a href=\"https://goo.gl/qj5aX9\" target=\"_blank\">Mage</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/2HCpjg\" target=\"_blank\">Mummy</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/68yRHY\" target=\"_blank\">Promethean</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/a4mjqA\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/hrULtN\" target=\"_blank\">Werewolf</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4433-second-chances","name":"Second Chances","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4202"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-10-11T18:05:44-07:00","id":1260232,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Second Chances</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1260127#post1260127\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So now that the Storyteller's Vault is open to CofD, any chance you would write up this chronicle as an SAS once you finish playing it?</div> </div> </div> </div>I wouldn't know where to begin... I've barely looked at existing SAS, all my exposure to them is basically limited to the sample stories occasionally tucked away in the back of some first edition blue books. Aren't they normally linear sample adventures broken down as a sequence of consecutive scenes? Given that I mostly write campaigns as just a stack of environments and attendant NPCs, with the latter all having objectives, and then as the players explore their world I push everything into the players' faces with all the enthusiasm of a kid bashing toys together. I'm not sure the format carries over based on my very limited understanding of SAS.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-10-14T18:36:57-07:00","id":1260598,"title":"","content":"Got back from holidays on Thursday, and of course one of the first things I did after getting off the plane was making sure everyone was ready for a game over the weekend. And with that we have...<br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Session 9</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">With their duties discharged and Christmas upon them, each of the Harbingers spent their Christmas Eve busying themselves in their own way. Regan wanted to do something nice for her aunt, so she spent the day baking. Orla had a present in mind for her new pack, so she sealed herself away in her room with a bucket of dead rats, a coat hanger, and an imprisoned wind spirit. Aaron had a morning mass to give, which he delivered in a half-hearted fashion. And Aed, well, Aed mostly just followed Aaron around all day, not having anything better to do with his time.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">The last hunt's events were weighing on Aaron, so he resolved after mass to confront the Whispers. Aed went with him against his wishes, but the Cahalith's presence proved worthwhile when they reached the Whispers' territory and were accosted by members of the constabulary. The two wolves had been lurking in the alley where Aed had previously contacted the Whispers, waiting for the other pack to show up, when they were spotted. The suspicious behaviour coupled with Aaron's obviously Catholic attire drew attention, but as the conversation went on the two Harbingers became increasingly aware that two of the officers they were speaking to were conspicuously keeping to the elongated shadows of the nearby buildings. Indeed, Blackstaff's spiritual afflictions had not been healed by the dispersal of the <i>Suthilu</i>, rather to Aed's disappointment.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Aed kept the attention of the officers largely by talking about himself until Jamie, the young wait who appeared to lead the Whispers, showed up. Since they'd claimed they were only in the area to meet friends, the arrival of those friends convinced the constables that everything was in order... although Aaron did notice that the two who kept out of the sunlight didn't retreat very far. They went back into the alley with Jamie and found the wolf called Greyson lurking there, but no sign of the larger man, Dorian, Aed had met before.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Throwing himself fully into the Cahalith role, Aed relayed the story of their hunt in Blackstaff. He left nothing out, beginning with tracking Ciara to the creature called We-Who-Eat-In-Darkness, and all the way to his single-handed triumph over the Bat Hosts. Jamie's attention was fixed from the moment he mentioned the <i>magath</i>, and when he finished she made him go over every detail of that encounter. Looking somewhat ashamed, she mentioned that the creature he described had once been the Whispers' Totem. Someone or something had convinced the spirit of gluttony that it should turn its appetites towards essence of a darker, shadowy nature, leading to its corruption. When the Whispers realised what had happened they excised the totem from the pack, but hadn't been able to locate it since. They thanked the Harbingers for alerting them to the <i>Suthilu</i>, but it was clear from the orders Jamie gave Greyson that the Whispers intended to go straight after We-Who-Eat-In-Darkness as soon as they could.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">At this point Aaron interrupted, warning the Whispers that intrusions into the Harbingers' territory wouldn't be tolerated any longer. Jamie looked at him flatly and declared that she rather thought he should be happy that another pack had chosen to take on the Pure problem on the Harbingers' behalf. She left without offering any particular commitment to keeping clear of Andersonstown, Greyson racing ahead of her.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">The two Harbingers returned to their territory. When Aaron reached St Huberts and sorted through his mail for the day, he found a plain envelope with a short message inside. &quot;9pm, 26/12/81, 28 Longwood Avenue. The Hunters make their final run.&quot; While an opportunity to hunt with his fellow Hunters in Darkness was something he was looking forward to, Longwood Avenue lay in the territory of the Balelight Howl. He knew their leader, Mercy, personally as she'd been present for his own initiation into the Hunters, and he knew the Howl as a pack with no fewer than three Hunters as part of their membership. But the territory they claimed ran through Shankill, practically the birthplace of the UVF.<br /> <br /> That was all still two days away, however. Aed spent his afternoon trying to track down a Christmas party to crash, before finally giving up and going to Regan's place with a bottle of wine. Regan and her Auntie Steph were there, deep in a conversation about Regan's father and why he hadn't taken Regan in when her mother died. Steph had been trying to delicately explain that, with a wife and several daughters already, it hadn't really been possible for him to accept Regan. Regan had been halfway through asking Steph how she could contact her father when there was a heavy knock on the door. And with that Regan found herself having to explain that her new involvement with the church had led to her making friends with, among others, a B-grade movie star.<br /> <br /> Once the initial surprise had worn off, Aed had introduced himself to Steph, and the wine had been broken open, Regan found herself noting with some dismay that Aed and Steph were really begin to hit it off. She ducked off to her room and grabbed a mirror to invoke her Messenger Rite and contact Aaron, begging him to come over as a chaperone, and then started contacting anyone else she could think of on short notice by more mundane means. She spoke on the phone briefly with Jo Austin, asking her and her father to come over for a surprise Christmas dinner, and to bring anyone else they knew with no other plans. <br /> <br /> Before long, the small household had become quite cramped. The Austins, hearing that Aed was there, had brought their copies of Diamond Man 2 and their VCR and had the movie playing in the living room. Aed was alternating between telling stories about his movie career and acting as an impromptu bar tender, while Orla eventually tracked her packmates down and started awkwardly flirting with a couple of the miners while their wives were distracted. The wives, for their matter, were deep in conversation with Father Aaron as they asked for advice on how to deal with the sudden loss of income to their households following on from the mine closure. Regan kept trying to minimise how much one on one time Aed and Steph spent together, but then Jo wanted to thank her for organising this party and taking her father's mind off losing his job. By the time Regan extricated herself from that conversation she found an intoxicated Steph complaining to Aed about how Regan's own father considered himself so superior, how he'd always looked down his nose at their family. Rather than interrupt, Regan just lurked where she could listen in until the subject changed back to Aed.<br /> <br /> The party began to wrap up a little bit before midnight. One by one the humans began to trickle out of the household and returning to their beds. Aed lingered, but when it became clear that Aaron wouldn't let him be the last one to leave he reluctantly walked Orla back to his home. Aaron excused himself and went out on patrol, while Regan helped Steph to bed.<br /> <br /> Christmas day dawned with Steph swearing at the mess she had to clean up, and conscripting Regan to help her. Orla presented Aed with her gift, a bizarre parody of a wind chime made of wire and dried rat, with the Wisp inside compelled to clean away the scents of those who come near. Aed accepted it, frowning, and had a good long talk with her about how they had a responsibility to keep the denizens of the Shadow on their side as much as possible. Still, the Fetish would come in useful. Aed figured planting it somewhere out of the way and unconnected to the members of the pack would give them the ability to foil would-be trackers by erasing their trails, but he had no clear ideas yet of what a good location would be.<br /> <br /> Aaron was busy with his congregation, Regan with her one family member and chores, but Orla and Aed still had some time to kill and they were curious to see what the Shadow would be like on this day. They pushed their way through Hope's Locus, passing through the looking glass and into the ruins of burning Belfast. And there they found war. The cacophony of battle was everywhere, hulking, furred beasts grappled with ephemeral creatures of wind and air on every side. At a quick glance Aed could already spot several skirmishes being fought on his street, while larger spirits could be glimpsed moving among the ash in the near distance. The two of them slipped away rather than get caught up in the maelstrom, escaping back across the Gauntlet as fast as they could.<br /> <br /> On Christmas night, each of the wolves spotted lights flash on the slopes of the Old Man. Aaron, out on patrol, Orla and Aed, drinking on the porch as Orla smoked, and Regan staring out of her bedroom window, each saw a pulse of silver light on the mountain. It came again, a few seconds later. And then a third flash, red and raw and burning, leaving ugly afterimages in their eyes. Each of them set off, with Regan slipping out of the house quietly to avoid waking Steph. Aaron was the first to arrive and quickly located the source of the lights. The ground was churned while tangled, overgrown hedges looked blackened and burned. And there was blood, a great deal of it, splattered across the ground. The rest of the Harbingers arrived within minutes, shifting back to <i>Hishu</i> as they examined the scene. They were all pretty sure that the Whispers and Cleareyes had fought, and after a brief discussion they agreed that Orla and Aaron should taste some of the blood. Aaron gained a sense of a prey heading south at a steady trot, while Orla's impression was of someone moving north and fast. The Whispers lacked permission to be on Black Mountain, and they'd been warned, so as one the pack shifted into <i>Urhan</i> and raced north.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately, Orla was the slowest of the group and the one with a bead on their target. Racing ahead, Aed and Aaron tried to pick up the trail on their own, but doing so slowed them down and they all gathered again on the edge of town without having caught their prey red-handed. Aaron was spitting furious and tried to push forward into Blackstaff to run down the Whispers, but Aed tackled him in wolf form and kept him pinned until he agreed to wait for the others to arrive. At last, with the pack assembled, they decided that this conflict in their territory couldn't continue. They agreed to send Orla and Regan after the Whispers to ask them to meet with the Cleareyes, with the Harbingers as mediators, while Aed and Aaron went south to deliver the same message to the Fire-Touched.<br /> <br /> Of course, technically, the Cleareyes hadn't broken any law or promise. Aed mollified Aaron as they set off by pointing out that, should the Whispers accept, they would regret putting themselves at the mercy of the Harbingers.</span></span></span><br /> </span><br /> <br /> </span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-10-21T17:58:02-07:00","id":1262029,"title":"","content":"Session 10<br /> <br /> In the last few hours of Christmas night, the Harbingers ventured beyond their borders in the north and south. Regan, Orla, and Hope followed the trail of blood to a groundskeeper's shack on the grounds of a local school. While they were still at a safe distance, Regan invoked both her Cloak of Fear and Totemic Empowerment Rites to garb herself in hissing shadows and give herself the ability to manipulate the target's weaknesses. She approached, moving quietly, and saw a large, injured man rummaging around in a trunk. She walked out and greeted him, scaring the ever-living crap out of him, and turned real waspish at his panicked questions. After seeing how angry he was getting at her snarky answers she finally introduced herself, and they were able to have a proper conversation. The injured wolf was Dorian, and he gave her a quick rundown on what had happened. The three Whispers had been roving south, intending to raid the borders of the Cleareyes' territory again, when they ran into a Pure who burned like fire on the slopes of Black Mountain. They fought, but the Pure did <i>something</i> awful to Greyson that caused his flesh to bubble and boil and sent him screaming worse than anything Dorian had ever heard before, then subdued Jamie and dragged her off while Dorian ran. Regan asked Dorian to come with her, telling him that they intended to bring the Pure in for a meeting to try and end the war peacefully, and she convinced him only thanks to the New Moon's shadowy gift allowing her to slip inside his guard.<br /> <br /> Down south, Aaron and Aed had crossed into Cleareyes territory. They crossed through the fields beyond Belfast in <i>Urhan</i> form, finally shifting back to <i>Dalu</i> to stalk through a small village only a short distance outside the city. The blood they were following led them unerringly through the village, to the start of a narrow dirt road winding away from town and into a forested thicket. It was at the start of that trail that they spotted a man waiting in the lee of a building, small spirits of hedge and nature clambering across his body and poking and prodding him towards the two <i>Uratha</i>. The man introduced himself as Peadar, a Wolf-Blooded of the Cleareyes pack, and after they said they needed to speak to Eaven he agreed to lead them to her. They followed the dirt road on foot for a good half hour before the thicket parted and they saw the Cleareyes' compound, four large buildings facing out onto a central square, a rust-coloured monolith at its heart. Peadar led them straight to the largest building, and up close the Harbingers noted that while the buildings looked new, the foundations at their base were very old.<br /> <br /> Aed entered first, and found himself in a room with four other <i>Uratha</i>. Eaven was reclining on the lounge, holding a china doll to her chest and her head in the lap of another young woman with a discoloured patch of skin running down her throat. This new woman was cleaning Eaven's many wounds carefully, even keeping them from scabbing over in order to rise out the injuries. The two others, a man and a woman, both got to their feet as he entered. The man was of indeterminate age, emaciated, flannel clothing hanging like loose sails from his frame, his face covered by a thick beard. The woman looked to be in her fifties, her clothes homespun and adorned in iron medallions, and she was already shifting to <i>Dalu</i> in response to the intrusion. Aed's senses were keen enough to pick up Eaven saying, &quot;No sunlight. No food either, until-&quot; but all conversation ceased as he went in.<br /> <br /> After a few moments of staring down the room, Eaven said, &quot;I don't recall giving permission for you to return the favour. What brings you two into my home?&quot;<br /> <br /> Aed explained that they'd grown tired of their territory being used as a battleground, and that he hoped to end the war. At that, Eaven grinned. The war was over, she told them. She'd met the Whispers and broken them. One, Jamie, was in her keeping, one had been disposed of, and the third had run like a whipped cur. No need for negotiations, there was no one left to fight.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately, Aed <i>really</i> didn't like the idea of the Cleareyes keeping one of the Whispers. He insisted that Eaven attend the negotiations, and that she bring Jamie with her. Eaven was both a little incredulous and beginning to get angry. She had absolutely no reason to talk things over with the last Whisper, if he even had the courage to show up. She certainly had no reason to risk giving up her prize. She warned Aed that the Harbingers would find themselves quite isolated if their fellow Forsaken found out that they'd been dealing with the Cleareyes, and told him not to press this any further. Aed told her that he was confident he'd be able to talk the other packs around and that he wasn't scared, which finally prompted her to get off the couch.<br /> <br /> At around this point, Aed noticed the eyes of the china doll were looking at him. They were eyes he recognised, the compound, refracted glare of an angry wasp. Eaven noticed him looking and mentioned that while she'd been hunting the Whispers, this spirit had come to her seeking protection. It had promised to serve her in a fetish in return for being taken away and kept safe, and she had used it to help her deal with the Whispers. Aaron snarled at the doll, remembering that they'd ordered Mazdarix to hop across the Gauntlet rather than stay in the Flesh, but Aed turned his attention back to the confrontation with Eaven. He offered to represent her fairly at the negotiation with the Whispers, make sure that there was no retaliation, and allow her to remain in her compound so long as she allowed Jamie to come with him. The audaciousness of such a one-sided deal took Eaven off-guard and she wavered between laughing and ordering him to get out, ultimately settling on the latter.<br /> <br /> The Harbingers reconvened back on the slopes of Black Mountain with Dorian in tow. They worked out where they stood, and decided that if they wanted to go up against the Cleareyes and get Jamie back then they had a few things they needed to do. The Fetish made from Mazdarix was clearly something they had to get out of the Pure's hands, so Regan would need to steal it away before any confrontation. They would then try and mount a distraction while Regan rescued Jamie. But first, Aed wanted to see if they could track down Greyson. If they could get another Blackstaff wolf back on his feet it would probably help steel the spine of the very shaken Dorian and help to balance out the Cleareyes' numbers. They asked Dorian to identify which patch of blood-sodden ground was where Greyson had been hit, and then used that to allow Regan to track his trail.<br /> <br /> They moved quickly, the pack spreading out as they pushed to reach Greyson as fast as they could in case he was clinging to life and in need of medical attention. Regan raced far ahead of the others and reached the source of the trail. Greyson lay slumped against a rotten trunk, his body ravaged in sores and gaping wounds. He was in <i>Urshul</i>, his massive body covered in crushed fragments of mushrooms and splintered wood. Regan approached cautiously, listening to his liquid, laboured breathing. She watched as a small, juvenile wasp crawled out from a wound just beneath his ear and flexed its wings. She crouched, baring her teeth and ready to attack the small creature, when Greyson lurched to his feet. The sores across his body moved and shifted, larvae and carapace-covered bodies shifting within his flesh. He lunged at Regan, tearing a terrible wound open across her side and forcing her into Garou to survive. As her flesh knit back together, she grappled with Greyson and tried to pin him. Stronger than him in her larger form, she tried to call on Hope's powers to strengthen his mind and allow him to overthrow the influence of the creatures possessing him... but instead she got the sense that his mind was far away and too distant for her influences to effect.<br /> <br /> Luckily, the others reached her before the Death Rage began to overwhelm her. Aed pinned Greyson beneath him and rendered him immobile, giving Regan the time to conduct a basic first aid examination. She tried pulling some of the larvae from his wounds, crushing them as they emerged. Dorian squatted down to watch, and they could clearly see that as Greyson's body tried to knit itself back together it was repairing itself <i>with wasp larvae already in the regenerated flesh</i>. Whatever curse Eaven had unleashed on him with Mazdarix's help, it would require a Rite to repair. Even though he was a Bone Shadow, Dorian couldn't think of any local spirits who might know such a Rite.<br /> <br /> By this point, the sun was starting to creep up over the horizon. Regan needed to be home soon, before Steph was up and about. They realised that the only time Greyson showed hostility towards them was when they tried to harm the wasps, and otherwise he seemed &quot;content&quot; to lie on the ground and allow the larvae to eat away at him from the inside. With Regan having tasted his blood, they decided that they would break for the day, work out a solution, and track him down again once they had some idea of how to help him.<br /> <br /> Dorian returned to Blackstaff, saying he had a shotgun with silver rounds that he would make ready. The Harbingers returned to their respective homes. Regan was approaching her front door when she was accosted by a man stinking of sweat and stale alcohol. His hands pawed desperately at her jacket as he begged her to please, please, please undo what she had done. Regan was baffled and backed up, bracing herself against the door, and she demanded to know who he was. For the briefest moment they made eye contact and she saw his left eye, with a milky white pupil and an iris the colour of blood. Her essence revolted at the sight, feeling like a full body vomit, and the man ducked his head again to stare at the floor, ashamed. He muttered that his name was Arthur Cosgrave, he was an officer with the Ulster Royal Constabulary, and the last thing he remembered before the world went mad was standing guard at a crime scene and making eye contact with her. He begged her again to fix whatever it was that she'd done to him.<br /> <br /> Regan remembered using her Gifts to inflict Lunacy at a glance, filling the constable's flesh with essence so that Hope could possess him and let the pack easily access the scene at Ciara's home after their initial investigation. She realised that in the throws of Lunacy, Arthur had come to Luna's attention. She had adopted him as Wolf-Blooded. And with that Regan realised that she had no idea how to help.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-10-21T19:00:28-07:00","id":1262031,"title":"","content":"So you guys know how the first page of this thread has <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar7212_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"7212\" data-vbnamecard=\"7212\">Vent0</a> saying at least twice that &quot;X will have consequences&quot;? Yesterday was the day they were proven right <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1059,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","user":"Azahul","date":". 10-21-2018, 10:03 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-10-22T01:05:22-07:00","id":1262082,"title":"","content":"The trainwreck... It's beautiful!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>~<span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u266b</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7593-teatime","name":"Teatime","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7593_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"750"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-10-22T18:01:56-07:00","id":1262258,"title":"","content":"It does turn out though that one downside to introducing an NPC in Session 1 and then trying for a shock reveal in Session 10 is that the players maaaaay not exactly remember the NPC in question <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> &quot;And you realise you have seen those eyes before, the eyes of the spirit Mazdarix.&quot;<br /> *a table of blank stares*<br /> &quot;Who?&quot;<br /> &quot;I think he means the flying messenger spirit Eaven used before.&quot;<br /> &quot;Oh come on guys, they were the first NPC you ever met!&quot;<br /> <br /> I guess that's to be expected. Mazdarix becoming a Fetish probably happened behind the scenes sometime around Session 4 and while I've spent the two months or so since then eagerly awaiting a chance to spring this on them they have had no reason to wonder about that wasp they pissed off back in the beginning.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-10-23T01:20:13-07:00","id":1262315,"title":"","content":"To be fair, the term &quot;parasite wasp Spirit&quot; is easier to remember than &quot;Mazdarix&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>~<span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u266b</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7593-teatime","name":"Teatime","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7593_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"750"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-10-23T05:00:20-07:00","id":1262330,"title":"","content":"Oh, no doubt. I did mention insectoid, compound eyes, but I guess too much time had passed.<br /> <br /> Looking back, it also appears that I neglected to mention the original encounter with Arthur Cosgrave. I'm pretty sure it happened at the start of Session 7, it was how the Pack gained access to the crime scene at Ciara's home in order to pick up her trail, but if I mentioned it elsewhere please let me know. Basically, as a distraction, Regan used her Gaze of the Moon gift on the constable on duty to inflict Lunacy, specifically the Reception Condition. That allowed Hope to possess the constable while they scoured the area for Ciara's trail.<br /> <br /> ...I think. This was obviously all close to two months back at this point, so if the events actually are written up somewhere in previous write-ups please let me know.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-10-23T15:16:50-07:00","id":1262420,"title":"","content":"And so, the Pack continues their sacred quest to screw themselves and their territory over, and forget about all those whose wrath they've earned.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-10-23T15:41:26-07:00","id":1262425,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1262420#post1262420\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And so, the Pack continues their sacred quest to screw themselves and their territory over, and forget about all those whose wrath they've earned.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's the flip-flopping that amuses me <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Help one side of a conflict long enough for it to essentially win the fight, and then decide that they actually prefer the other side. Way to make things harder for yourself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-10-28T18:18:05-07:00","id":1263289,"title":"","content":"Session 11<br /> <br /> As the desperate, newly Wolf-Blooded constable grasped and pleaded at Regan to take back what she'd done, she carefully located and stole the handgun he had tucked into the back of his jeans. Better safe than sorry. Feeling a bit more comfortable now, she explained what she could at short notice to Arthur. He was Wolf-Blooded, something she didn't believe there was any cure to. When he asked if that's what she was too, she laughed. The earnestness of her next words cut short Arthur's attempt to reject what she was saying, but she explained that she was a werewolf and that there were others like her. Then, realising that she'd been about to enter her home with her face still daubed in blood and clay from the rituals she'd been performing, she asked Arthur for his handkerchief. At that he noticed the fact his gun was missing and went very pale and frantic. He didn't notice that Regan had pocketed it, and instead asked if they could meet again to talk about this more. He'd lost something very important and needed to try and find out where he'd dropped it. Regan agreed easily, told him to go to St Hubert's when he was ready and ask for Father Aaron, and let him go. She crept inside, made sure her aunt still slept, and washed her face.<br /> <br /> A few hours later, the pack convened at St Hubert\u2019s in order to discuss strategy for taking on the Cleareyes. Aed laid out a range of options, but made sure the pack understood their priorities. They needed to get Mazdarix away from Eaven and rescue Jamie. Aed had a few ideas on how to achieve these objectives, ranging from the comparatively bloodless to an attempt to slaughter the entire Pure pack. He started to talk about how they need to find out a cure for Greyson first, to up their numbers, and that their best bet from there would be to take the Cleareyes unaware. Aaron objected to this. He demanded that the pack make sure the Pure would know they were coming, and know that it was as a response to profaning Black Mountain with their conflict. They needed to know their doom approached, and why.<br /> <br /> Aed suggested a few ideas for leading the Pure into traps, but ultimately everyone agreed that they needed to find a cure for Greyson first. They debated a few different beings they could ask for help in learning a purification rite, and settled on the Old Man. They entered the Shadow together and found it a wasteland, the discorporating corpses of spirits everywhere and the streets silent and bereft. Aed mentioned the battle he and Orla had seen taking place yesterday and figured that this must be the aftermath.<br /> <br /> The group continued on to Black Mountain, where they found the entrance to the Old Man\u2019s throne room closed. Hope figured that the entry must only open when the Old Man held court, and that they needed to find another way. Aed suggested tracking down the Other, the only powerful spirit he could think of, but given the peace and healing they needed Aaron broached the subject of the nearby Glade he\u2019d been carefully tending in his free time.<br /> <br /> The Glade took the form of a small garden, reflecting a stretch of wild growth in the real world just beyond the edge of Andersonstown\u2019s streets. Aaron had spent the past few months nurturing its growth, and within its rows the group found the first spirits they\u2019d seen since entering the Hisil. Aed sought to identify the strongest spirit within, a <i>Hursah</i> who took the form of a gauzy shroud wrapped around the scars of an old tree spirit in the midst of the garden. The <i>Hursah</i>, introducing herself as Silkenspun, listened to Aed\u2019s description of Greyson\u2019s affliction and sadly pronounced that she had not the knowledge to help. At the offer of gathra she told the pack that the only spirit of the right temperament and age that she could think of was the being known as Cairnbreath. But\u2026 Silkenspun warned them that Cairnbreath had been sealed beneath the earth long ago by the Wolf In Golden Chains, and surely would hunger fiercely. There would certainly be safer options than that.<br /> <br /> Aed declared that they didn\u2019t have time to seek out safer alternatives. A good gift for a hungry death spirit would surely be another, weaker death spirit, and Belfast was full of those.<br /> <br /> While all this had been going on, Orla and Aaron were on the verge of coming to blows. Orla had been picking flowers and braiding grass from the gardens, and Aaron saw her ruining his good work. They shouted and yelled at one another over it, and Aaron was still fuming as he led the group from the Glade. In his distraction, he failed to notice the greater threat. The violence spirits who followed Aed around, the gremlin-like beings he called his Entourage, had followed their champion into the Glade. While Aed was able to shoo them out, the sight of violence literally manifesting in the place he\u2019d sworn to preserve from that shook Aaron to his core. He could feel the Flesh begin to pull on him all the stronger.<br /> <br /> Unbalanced, frustrated, and angry, Aaron slammed into Orla from behind once they were clear of the Glade. She whirled on him, not really understanding why they were fighting but determined to defend herself. She struck him once, twice, but her blows were light compared to the rage driving Aaron. His second blow almost sent her to her knees, but with a supreme effort she was able to rise. The sudden strike to his temple staggered Aaron, a second blow to his stomach doubled him over, and a boot to his knee sent him crashing to the ground. He tried to lift himself but his reserves were spent. Six days without sleep, his body fuelled only by his connection to Hope, his constant exertion in warding his territory since then, all came crashing down and he found his arms unwilling to lift him off the ground.<br /> <br /> Aed ordered the two of them to cool off, and Aaron stomped back to his glade. He tried to repair some of the damage done by the visit, but he couldn\u2019t feel the connection he normally did amidst the peace and quiet of the garden. His Harmony had pulled him ever further from this precious place, and his usual connection was clouded.<br /> <br /> The rest of the group sought out the site of the Weeping Tree, remembering that it had been born from the sorrow at a massacre. The street was like a sodden marsh, even the asphalt yielding to their feet like a swamp\u2019s surface and filling with brackish, salty water. They scoured the area for death spirits, but it was Orla, her attention wandering up towards the sky, who spotted them. Far to the north, beyond the reach of their territory, circled \u201cV\u201d shaped creatures like vultures. She recognised them as Soul Snatchers, spirits of sudden and violent death born from shootings and bomb blasts.<br /> <br /> They couldn't waste time seeking permission from other packs to cross their territory and besides, the Whispers would understand. This was all for Greyson after all. So the three Harbingers made their way north, shifting to <i>Urhan</i> for speed. Only the outskirts of Blackstaff's Shadow had been touched by the battle for Andersonstown, and they found the spirit population there to be a lot more present. The leech-like darkness spirits they'd seen underground were everywhere here, shielding the entire region from Helios's light. In fact, within a few blocks the wolves found that all light was gone. They had to rely on other senses as they negotiated their way through the tangled streets. Unlike the underground versions many of the darkness spirits here were enormously overgrown, more like anacondas in size than leeches. They were coiled around the lamppost reflections, ringed maws suckered over the lightbulbs, their skin distended and bloated with essence. Remembering his conversation with the exiled darkness spirit underground, Aed realised that these must be the spirits who had allied with the <i>Suthilu</i>. The Whispers had clearly made little progress in cleansing their territory in the past few days which was... perhaps not surprising given recent events.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately, the clouded skies prevented them from spotting any Soul Snatchers in Blackstaff. It wasn't until they reached the northern border of the Whispers' territory, further than any of them could remember travelling in the <i>Hisil</i>, that the shadows began to ebb and they managed to catch sight of a flock of the spirits.<br /> <br /> Between them, however, stood an immense wall of steel and granite, its surface giving off a freezing aura and its top covered in barbed wire. Aed briefly attempted to scale the side of a nearby building, but immediately noticed that the wall was growing as he did so. He stepped off and walked up to the wall and attempted to push through it. He felt it draw out his essence and begin to yield before him, but something in his spirit rebelled against it and he was pushed back. Regan and Orla each attempted to push through the wall in turn, and found that it opened easily to them. The experience was not unlike passing through the Gauntlet, and they emerged stumbling into clear sunlight. Immediately in front of them loomed an immense rotted structure, its concrete facade pitted and eaten through with holes. Soul Snatchers were feeding on some unfortunate lesser spirits in front of them, but began to startle and take flight. The spirits reacted faster than the wolves, it was only the blessing of Regan's auspice that allowed her to spring forward and pin one of the spirits before it could escape. Wounds opened up across her flanks with a sound like gunfire as the other spirits winged away and abandoned their trapped companion, while Orla rushed up and opened her spirit bottle. Seeing the spirit's essence begin to flow away from her, Regan relaxed. In that moment the Soul Snatcher's teeth tore into her side in a vicious blow that sent her instinctively into <i>Garou</i>, teetering on the edge of death rage.<br /> <br /> She managed to regain control, and together with Orla passed back through the wall. This time the peace line appeared to recognise them and let them pass without effort or a payment in Essence. Taking a mirror from Aed, Regan sent a message to Aaron to rejoin them on the southern side of their territory. They made their way back through Blackstaff and Andersonstown without incident, and with the pack united they slipped quickly through Cleareyes territory and even further south and west, reaching the site of an old henge. Standing stones dotted the landscape like ancient monoliths, and between them they could see the tunnel entrance to the catacombs Silkenspun had described to them. Taking a deep breath, they descended.<br /> <br /> Air moved strangely in the dark, cold tunnels. It came up from below in waves, smelling faintly of rot. It was Hope who voiced the uneasy impression they had all begun to form. &quot;The dead are breathing.&quot; They passed empty stone sarcophagi, each of them shattered or pulled apart by something with incredible strength, and remembered what Silkenspun had told them about Cairnbreath's hunger.<br /> <br /> On edge as they were, the pack was prepared when they were attacked. Four ancient, mummified bodies came stumbling out of alcoves in a clumsy attempt at an ambush. Aaron shifted to <i>Dalu</i> and raced to meet them, but his blows were off-kilter and he only managed the most superficial of damage. Regan harried their attackers from the side in <i>Urhan</i>, even as the bodies began to shift as well. Dry tendons and muscles simply snapped and pulled apart as bones lengthened and muzzles formed, each of the corpses shifting into withered mockeries of familiar forms. Two of the creatures took <i>Dalu</i>-like forms and attacked Regan and Aaron, while a third shifted into a desiccated <i>Urshul</i> and assaulted Orla. The fourth didn't have time to shift. Aed tackled it in his wolf form, bearing it down to the ground and systematically tore it into little pieces until it stopped twitching.<br /> <br /> Aaron and his opponent traded blows back and forth, their claws rending each other's flesh. His own swings seemed largely ineffectual, but his Rahu-blessed endurance made him confident that he would outlast his opponent. Somewhere in the background he glimpsed Orla, still in <i>Urhan</i>, biting through the neck of her <i>Urshul</i> formed attacker and throwing it away. The distraction was enough for his attacker to close the distance and drive its clawed fingers deep into his stomach. He felt something vital tear, deep inside, and gasped in pain. The sound drew Orla's attention, and a second later her body slammed into the undead werewolf from behind. Bones shattered, skin tore, and the creature came apart at the seams. Aaron snarled at shame of being saved by the Bone Shadow, and threw himself back into the fray.<br /> <br /> The pack quickly finished off Regan's opponent and paused to take stock. Regan was quite badly wounded, still suffering from the devastating injuries inflicted by the Soul Snatcher, and they decided it would be best if she hid herself among the sarcophagi until they came back. The other three pressed onwards, following a set of winding stairs further underground.<br /> <br /> They reached a circular chamber with no clear exit, its walls adorned with murals depicting a war between two spirit courts and lit by an unearthly blue light that seemed to be more a quality of the air itself. The Harbingers could make out scenes that seemed to depict spirits of death and rot clashing with spirits of the forest. A being like a tidal wave of skulls and bones, cloaked in miasma and rooted in stone, led one court against another being resembling a titan of thorns and wood. In the background, obscured by other images and only visible when they took the time to really examine the scenes closely, lurked an abstracted depiction of a werewolf. It didn't fight in the war, but it was there for every battle just... watching.<br /> <br /> Aed knocked on one of the panels at random, trying to determine if there was some sort of secret switch or pressure plate, and heard a clear echo telling him there was a passage behind it. He went to put pressure on the mural, to see if he could find a way through, when Orla launched herself at it. A furry cannonball of muscle collided with the fragile surface and shattered the mural apart, sending both her and Aed tumbling through to the other side. A stretch of corridor led away from them, as far as the eye could see, empty alcoves on either side making it appear like they had entered a ransacked ossuary.<br /> <br /> The three Harbingers collected themselves and began to walk, Hope following in their footsteps. They had been walking for some time when they realised they were no longer along. A shrivelled being, all skull and withered skin, walked with them. They could feel its clawed fingers running down their back, caressing their chins, even though it still lurked well outside of arm's reach. Clothed in a robe of grave dirt that trailed behind him like the train of a bridal dress, the ancient spirit considered the pack with a careful eye.<br /> <br /> &quot;Too soon.&quot; The voice was a gasp, like the last expulsion of air from a cadaver. &quot;Come back. When you start to rot.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1059,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","user":"Azahul","date":". 10-29-2018, 12:38 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-11-04T18:11:31-08:00","id":1264666,"title":"","content":"Session 12<br /> <br /> Aed approached the ancient spirit slowly, carefully. It was clear based on the murals that they'd seen that this was Cairnbreath, and that the spirit was much diminished from his former status by its imprisonment. Keeping his attention for long was difficult, he kept fixating on the walls of his tomb and licking them with his long, grey tongue in order to scrape off even the barest dregs of Essence. That lasted until Aed produced the jar containing the Soul Snatcher. Cairnbreath accepted it eagerly, his mouth unhinging like a snake's and covering the jar's opening while unseen hands delicately removed the lid. A shudder rippled through the spirit's body as he devoured the Soul Snatcher.<br /> <br /> &quot;A little sugary,&quot; he murmured, but the feeding seemed to bring new life to the creature (paradoxically). He listened in turn to Aed's plea in return for the <i>gathra</i> paid, but proclaimed that ancient laws bound him and prevented him from offering the knowledge they sought. Aed asked for an explanation of the laws, and the two of them negotiated carefully over the next few hours. They had to delicately unpick their way through byzantine clauses and loopholes, finding ways in which Cairnbreath would be able to help. Piece by piece, knowledge of the Rite began to form.<br /> <br /> Father Aaron, however, was beginning to feel the pressure of time weighing upon him. He needed to leave while discussions were still in progress to make it to the Hunters' run in time. He collected Regan from her hiding spot in the upper levels and left the tomb, but on Cairnbreath's urging he did use his claws to mar the chains decorating the entrance. His acquiescence endeared Cairnbreath greatly, to the point where he actively helped Aed find the key to allowing him to help the pack.<br /> <br /> Aaron and Regan retraced their steps through the Shadow. While they had made no effort at stealth crossing the Cleareyes' territory before, merely putting on as much speed as they could muster, this time they felt it would be safest to proceed slowly and carefully. Unfortunately, it seemed like the land itself was against them. Lesser spirits whispered at first, then began to call out at their passing. They heard howls in the distance and broke into a sprint, but the Pure were upon them quickly. Eaven emerged, her fur aflame in <i>Urhan</i>. She raced alongside them, naming them hypocrites in the First Tongue, and tore and harried them. Regan raced ahead, knowing they weren't far from the edge of the Harbingers' territory. Once she had crossed it she covered her tracks and began to circle back.<br /> <br /> Eaven finally appeared to break away from Aaron, but it wasn't long before he smelled the scent of smoke between him and his territory's borders. She tackled him, jaws tearing at his flesh, the fire coursing across her in place of fur burning him at such close quarters. She shifted into <i>Dalu</i> as they grappled, and it was only by calling on the powers of his auspice and burning the last of his Essence that Aaron was able to heave her off and make a break for it. His vision was dimming, his fur still smouldering. Eaven chased him to the very edge of her territory and then stopped, a pointed declaration that she, at least, would honour their borders for now. But as a parting gift she raised her hands, and the smouldering fur on Aaron's body rose up in flames. When Regan found him he had assumed Gauru in blind pain, rolling back and forth as he struggled to put out the fire. Even when he shifted back down to a more human form, some of the burns were so deep they refused to heal.<br /> <br /> Aed and Orla, meanwhile, had completed their negotiations with Cainbreath. He whispered into their minds, describing to them the place of death within the cycle of life. Rot and decay might be seen by some as foul or evil, but from decay would spring new life. They could use this knowledge to harrow the flesh of Greyson and drive out the parasites, and if he was lucky the scars from the process would one day heal.<br /> <br /> Then, rite bestowed, Cairnbreath left the tomb. Where he went, the wolves did not see, but it seemed clear that by damaging the chains Aaron had freed the spirit from his captivity. They could feel Luna's gaze on them though, and knew with certainty that the Lunes were watching. For uncovering this lost knowledge, the two of them had earned new Renown.<br /> <br /> Aed and Orla, knowing that they were not suited for quiet travel, headed north to cut around the Cleareyes' territory. They knew they had Forsaken neighbours to their west, who held most of the streets east of Glen Road. Aed paid <i>gathra</i> to one of the violent little gremlins of his entourage to send it with a message for the Silent Sons. Aed explained that he had no choice, time was of the essence to spare a dying wolf, but he would meet with them and ask their forgiveness for the transgression at a later date. The spirit received the message, eyes wide and awed by the attention from its star, before darting away. Aed and Orla moved on, encountering no resistance. They did notice that the streets here were barred in places by lines of barbed wire, and there were places where still-living spirits had been staked as markers or warnings, but they couldn't see the owner of such dire symbols. They reached their own territory without incident and crossed back over through the Locus.<br /> <br /> That was where they met Regan, who had been hanging out in Aed's home and feeding off the Locus to replenish some of her own badly depleted Essence stores. They found out that Aaron had continued on into the night in <i>Urhan</i> form, leaving Regan behind as he raced off to attend his run. Aed asked Regan to take him to Greyson immediately, and thanks to Greyson's blood they were able to locate the ruin of the wolf before long. The pack worked together to restrain him as Aed performed the rite, brutalising Greyson's body until he was on the point of expiration. His wounds tried to knit back together, but the injection of essence appeared to be consumed by the wasps instead. Eggs hatched and the larvae within matured into wasps in the blink of an eye, gorged on the essence poured into them by Greyson's body. The woods became filled with the buzzing swarm, and each of the wolves could feel the parasites trying to take root in their own flesh. With Hope's blessings upon them however, they were utterly impervious. Aed reached into Greyson's chest, claws parting layers of flesh, until he could grasp at a writhing ball of eggs and larvae buried deep within the wolf's ribcage. Cairnbreath's blessings flowed through his claws, rotting the infection away to nothing, and he pulled the dying mass free. A spasm shook Greyson's body, but when it passed they could see his wounds closing and dying insects crawling from his body to expire on the ground.<br /> <br /> Greyson continued to breath, shallow but easier than before, but they noted with concern that his eyes still stared blankly at the sky. He appeared to be in a coma, as far as the others could tell. They gathered up his body and carried him back to Aed's home, barely avoiding detection by the few humans still out and about at this time of night. They snuck him past a sleeping Lizzie, curled up on Aed's couch, and stashed the body in Aed's basement. Aed tried to write a note that would dissuade Lizzie from investigating, but a premonition told him that the note would only make her curious. He tore it up, locked the basement door, and just hoped she wouldn't feel the need to use the gym in the next few days.<br /> <br /> Finally, gathering together with the others, he urged them all to get a good night's sleep. &quot;Tomorrow, we'll take the war to the Cleareyes.&quot;<br /> <br /> Far to the north, Aaron had wound his way through the Unionist streets of Blackstaff and Shankill without detection. He reached the address he'd been asked to seek out, discovering a crowd of werewolves waiting in the street. He shifted back to <i>Hishu</i> to greet them. Some he recognised from his initiation, others he did not. Three members of the Balelight Howl, including one he recognised as the pack's leader, a woman named Mercy, were standing in front of an old dilapidated house. Mercy greeted him as he approached, and explained that they were performing a Rite that could not be enacted on any other night. They'd even purchased the house, which... had apparently been quite cheap thanks to what happened to the previous occupants. She didn't elaborate, turning her attention back to Rite her packmates were performing.<br /> <br /> As he waited, Aaron overheard two other Hunters discussing those who were missing. Apparently Joseph, a name he also recognised, wouldn't be attending tonight. As undoubtedly the most powerful Hunter in the city, that appeared to be a bit of a blow to the prestige of the night's run. It was to be expected though, as his pack, the <i>Kallakalat</i>, were &quot;back on the front lines&quot;. Aaron also interjected when he heard some speculating where Greyson had ended up, telling the gathering that he'd been injured but the Harbingers were working on healing him.<br /> <br /> He felt a presence at his side, and turned to see a slight, blonde woman. She greeted him in a voice that sounded slightly hoarse, and introduced herself as Iule of the Silent Sons. She was interrupted by the Rite reaching its conclusion, but told him that after the run she needed to speak to him urgently. Her pack and his were neighbours in Andersonstown, and they needed to discuss Silver City.<br /> <br /> At last, Mercy declared the Rite complete and invited all the Hunters to enter the house and begin the run. One by one, Hunters passed across the threshold and vanished. Aaron shifted to <i>Urhan</i> moments before padding through the door. He immediately found himself in a darkened living room. The doorway behind him had vanished. There was shattered glass, broken picture frames, turned over furniture. He paced around the room, which was when a radio in the corner crackled to life.<br /> <br /> &quot;This story is true,&quot; it said, in a voice like that of one of Mercy's packmates. It told the story of the Whispers and the Balelight Howl working together to clean up their territories' Shadows after a large riot a few months previously. The horde of lesser anger and violence spirits spawned by the event had been corralled and shepherded into a great vessel prepared by the packs, and sealed away before they could distort the Shadow unduly. Aaron listened until the story ended, and then left the room.<br /> <br /> He found himself at the base of a flight of stairs. Ahead of him stretched a long, dark corridor. Behind him stood a door to the living room. There was another Hunter in <i>Dalu</i> leaning against a wall, bleeding from one side. A second later and a door to his right burst open, and a second wolf in <i>Urshul</i> came skidding into the room. It shifted into <i>Hishu</i> to reveal a worried looking Iule, breathing hard. The wolf in Dalu snarled out that whatever the Balelight Howl had organised for this run, it was hunting <i>them</i>. Aaron persuaded the two of them to slow down and describe what they had seen. It seemed Iule had been in the house for hours, the other wolf, Art, for about forty minutes. Aaron was sure no more than five minutes could have passed. Iule described glimpsing a man made of faces stalking her, and Art claimed he'd been tracked for a good twenty minutes without seeing anything but empty corridors before he received a knife in the back. Aaron gathered them together and they all shifted into <i>Urhan</i>. Water began to run down the stairs, pouring from a room on the second level, but they decided to proceed down the hallway first.<br /> <br /> They'd been walking in silence for several minutes, long past the point where they should have left the house entirely, when Aaron spied an open door. Inside was the living room he had started in, this time in daylight. The furniture had all been replaced, and he specifically noted that the television couldn't be more than a year old. A young couple was sitting on the lounge inside, but in a corner of the room behind them stood a creature that looked like a small, hunched over man wearing clothes stitched together from different patches of skin. Many of the patches had human features in them. As Aaron watched, the woman's hand began creeping towards a knife lying on an empty dinner plate beside her. Aaron was frozen with indecision, as the woman seized the knife and jumped atop the man, stabbing in a frenzy. Aaron burst into the room, hearing an exclamation from the wolves behind him suddenly cut off, and found that the door he passed through had vanished as soon as he stepped through. Separated from the others, he squared off against the creature in front of him... until movement from a portrait to his right ended in a flash of pain in his side. He turned and glimpsed the creature fleeing through a new door behind him and out of sight.<br /> <br /> Aaron tried to follow, leaving the struggling humans behind him, and found himself back in the darkened corridors. There was no sign of the others. He wandered here for hours. He glimpsed other sights of the house from different points in time. The oldest appeared to be of a small family, a couple and a child of eight or nine years old, eating dinner together. He recognised them from the portraits in the room he had first appeared in. He was ambushed a second time by the patch-skinned man, wounded badly, and shifted to <i>Garou</i> to fight back. He felt something tear like paper beneath his claws and the spirit fled again, forcing him to shift back.<br /> <br /> His exploration continued. He saw a badly scarred man lying in bed alone, and after a moment realised it was the man he'd seen being stabbed by his partner. Sheets and curtains in the room were slowly winding their way around his limbs, and Aaron fled just in time to avoid becoming entangled himself. He heard at times voices in the distance, singing stories about the triumphs of the different packs of Belfast. One gave thanks to the sun for its help, allowing the Symphony Cascade to drive back darkness spirits encroaching from across the border with Blackstaff. They had endured Helios's scorn and condescension in return for the aid of Hellions to wipe all shadows from their territory for a day, allowing them to drive a Shadow-Claimed <i>duguthim</i> to its death. They added this act to the litany of thanks the wolves of Belfast owed Helios, including acts like &quot;the burning of Mulrook&quot;, &quot;the forging of the alpha's golden chains&quot;, &quot;for the vigilance of Hai-el&quot;, and so on.<br /> <br /> Aaron finally made his way upstairs, where he glimpsed a masked man drowning a boy in a sink. The man was sobbing, begging the child to be quiet, calling the young boy by the name Callum. The child pushed his head clear long enough to scream at Aaron, deafening and bruising him. Through twists and turns he found himself to the flooded bathroom in daylight, where a voice from the open window sung the story of Eloith Sinseeker, aging alpha of the Shorerunners. He listened as Eloith sought Luna's intercession with the ocean, to use her pull with the tides to dredge forth the water's deepest secrets so that the Shorerunners might have something to remember their alpha by when she was gone. She had stood in the bitter waters, among waves tipped with ice, as a darkened orb washed up on the shore. With it, the Shorerunners would be able to spy on every corner of their territory, making up for the loss of their keenest tracker. For the sea did not give up something for nothing, and Eloith walked further into the waves that night and was never seen again.<br /> <br /> At last, Aaron was confronted by a scene of torture. Three masked men had a fourth tied to a chair, a bloodied ruin of a human being. They used a drill to bore through one of the man's kneecaps, but though he tried to scream he seemed unable to make much of a noise. Aaron saw one of the masked men look away, as if uncomfortable, which was when he spotted Callum watching from the doorway. The boy ran, the man gave chase, and Aaron followed. They reached the bathroom for the third time, and Aaron seized the man's arm to stop him from killing the boy. That was when he felt the patch-faced man's knife drive into his side. He tried to twist away, keeping his hold on the man, trying to free the boy from the man's grasp. He saw the knife coming again, arcing towards his eye.<br /> <br /> After that, he saw only darkness.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-11-05T07:19:29-08:00","id":1264737,"title":"","content":"Yeah, let's release the ancient death spirit someone went through the trouble of sealing. What could go wrong?<br /> <br /> What's that? Should we piss off the obviously stronger Pure Pack we already burned our bridges with? Why not.<br /> <br /> Poor Aaron. Is he just unlucky, or are there poor choices in play here?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-11-05T14:52:31-08:00","id":1264802,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1264737#post1264737\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, let's release the ancient death spirit someone went through the trouble of sealing. What could go wrong?</div> </div> </div> </div>Haha, I was interested to see where the pack would go on that one. They actually felt sorry for Cairnbreath, in spite of all the (un)dead Werewolves in and around the tomb. He is a bit of a wretched figure, reduced in rank from his former heights and utterly starved of essence. And as he demonstrated to Aed, he kind of sees himself as a kind of nature spirit, a shepherd of new things as much as the destruction of the old. Aaron didn't it as necessary to keep the spirit imprisoned when it had done nothing wrong as far as he could see, and the decision gave Aed an enormous bonus on the following social maneuvering roll.<br /> <br /> Admittedly a little bold to assume that he had all the information on something that happened thousands of years previously, but still.<br /> <br /> Incidentally, I really do love how epic social maneuvering with a high rank ephemeral being is. Aed had to break through 12 doors in 9 rolls, with each roll taking an hour (Excellent impression after the <i>gathra</i> and Voice of Glory). He managed it in 7. Loads of exceptional successes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1264737#post1264737\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What's that? Should we piss off the obviously stronger Pure Pack we already burned our bridges with? Why not.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, that was a bit unfortunate. They had wanted their attack to be a surprise, but the Cleareyes most likely suspect the Harbingers are up to something if they're stepping onto Cleareyes turf without so much as a by your leave.<br /> <br /> They're in a bit of an unfortunate position now. Both Aaron and Regan were suffering aggravated damage even before Aaron went off to join the Hunters' run. They were both tapped for essence by the end of the confrontation with Eaven.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1264737#post1264737\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Poor Aaron. Is he just unlucky, or are there poor choices in play here?</div> </div> </div> </div>Bad luck was certainly involved. He failed his stealth roll crossing Cleareyes turf, which was why he'd brought Regan with him. Her Pack Stalks The Prey Facet should have allowed her to correct his failure into a success... except that she failed her stealth roll too. You know you're unlucky when your Irraka is failing at stealth.<br /> <br /> Some of the poor choices are intentional. Aaron is guilty and determined to do better, which is why he's been burning the candle at both ends. He's going on six days without sleep at this point. The Totem's benefits allow him to ignore about 4 points worth of those exhaustion penalties, so he's basically on his feet solely because of the intercession of Hope. His player is trying to push him to a breaking point (a metaphorical one, although a mechanical one towards the spirit would be appreciated now that he's Harmony 8) and see how the character evolves from there, whether he learns the lesson about some things being outside of his control.<br /> <br /> The final fight of the session had Aaron make the choice to try and save the boy rather than fight back, so he had to endure several rounds of being stabbed without fighting back. The ephemeral being he was being attacked by wasn't enormously strong, but it had softened him up with those multiple hit-and-run attacks and he'd already used his one <i>Garou</i> use for the scene healing some of the damage he'd taken.<br /> <br /> Plus side, I'm sure that qualifies him for some Purity Renown if he survives the experience.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-11-11T18:19:40-08:00","id":1266558,"title":"","content":"Session 13<br /> <br /> Aaron regained consciousness slowly. He could smell diesel, and there was a steady vibration running through his bones that told him he was in a moving vehicle. He tried to open his eyes... and then the pain hit, and he realised why he couldn't. He called out, and heard a voice swear and the car pull over. The engine shut off, and in the silence he heard the voice ask nervously how he was feeling.<br /> <br /> He recognised the speaker's voice this time as Iule, the Silent Sons Hunter he'd met earlier. Once he was settled and composed, she laid out what had happened. The Balelight Howl hadn't bothered to share this information with any of the Catholic wolves until <i>after</i> the run, of course, but it turned out they'd been trying to cleanse a Wound. The Rite they'd performed dislocated the house on 28 Longwood Avenue from time, allowing them to reach back to the moment of the Wound's creation and snuff it out as it was being born. It was the Howl who'd found Aaron in the grip of Patches, some kind of wound-born spirit who had been intently trying to carve Aaron's face off in order to add to his collection. They'd driven the spirit off quickly and stabilised him, and once the run was complete they'd turned him over to Iule to drive home since she was heading that way anyway. Unfortunately, they hadn't been fast enough to save Aaron's eyes, but they'd grow back eventually. He just needed to stay out of sight of mortals until the missing organs and skin grew back and he should be right as rain.<br /> <br /> Iule ended up driving Aaron home. At his insistence, they spoke about the Silver City problem she'd mentioned before the Run. The Sons had been monitoring Silver City's activities for months, and were perturbed by the fact that they seemed to be moving a lot of their operations into the Black Mountain Quarry. She wanted eyes (&quot;Shit, shit, I am so sorry!&quot;) on the situation there, because mortal hunters couldn't be allowed to operate freely or else they'd become a danger to both packs. She wanted to meet with the whole pack in order to coordinate the effort, so when they reached the rectory Aaron tried to call Aed. The phone was answered by Lizzie, who basically just swore down the telephone line at Aaron for a few seconds for disturbing her sleep at 3am and then hung up on him. Aaron promised Iule that he'd share the message with the rest of the pack in the morning, and that they'd be in touch. After she leaves, Aaron makes a second phone call to his Wolf-blooded deacon and asks him to take over services for the next couple of months. He should tell the congregation that Aaron had been injured in a spate of violence.<br /> <br /> The next morning, Aed tried to put the pack together. He and Orla picked up Regan from her home and tried to get in contact with Aaron, but Aaron wasn't answering his phone. They drove around to the rectory and, eventually, Aaron answered the door. The sight of him made Regan and Aed lose their breakfast. He ushered them inside quickly, it was a Sunday morning and there were plenty of mortals nearby attending the church service, and explained the story. Aed lost his temper, accusing Aaron of acting irresponsibly when they were so close to war with the Cleareyes. They couldn't afford to be down a packmate, let alone their Rahu.<br /> <br /> After the two of them cooled off, Aed shared his preparations with the other. He'd been quizzing Regan a lot about Eaven's abilities, and had stocked up on protective gear. He had several lengths of chains, a fire blanket, and a fire extinguisher ready for when they went toe to toe with her. He also wants to track down Scarred Beast Burning again, so that Orla can forge a Fetish weapon to help them in the fight.<br /> <br /> The pack declares a hunt against Scarred Beast Burning, and Orla uses Impossible Spoor to trace the spirit. Aaron insists on coming, shifting to <i>Urhan</i> so that his other senses can better compensate for his lost sight. They trace the rogue spirit to a warren it had carved to itself in a hill on the far side of Black Mountain. There, Aed confronts the beast. It declared that it wasn't scared of the pack any longer, that the Court of Bloody Communion had driven back the Union from Black Mountain and it now had the backing of its fellows. Aed didn't bat an eyelid. He'd just treated with a god of death, this little mongrel wasn't going to be a challenge. He declared that Scarred Beast Burning's only hope of staying this side of the Gauntlet was to help the Harbingers, beginning by serving them as a Fetish. It looked for a moment like the spirit might resist him, but then its will broke and it agreed. Orla bound the spirit to a single bullet, creating a shapeshifting bullet wish gnashing teeth for Regan to use in her gun.<br /> <br /> With the preparations almost complete, Regan used her Message Rite to summon Dorian to meet with them at Aed's home. Aed asked Lizzie to give them some space, and the Harbingers performed the <i>Siskur-Dah</i> against the Cleareyes while they waited. Aed then retreated to his bedroom, sensing that several members of the Cleareyes were currently asleep. He intended to hunt them through their dreams.<br /> <br /> Dorian arrived, and the others took him down to see the healing Greyson. Barring the immense wound in his chest, the dire wolf appeared to be largely recovered from his harrowing experience. The only problem was that he was still unresponsive. Dorian mentioned that Greyson had the capacity to send his mind elsewhere, to possess wild creatures and ride them instead, and that he could be doing so in order to avoid the pain afflicting his real body. They just needed to find a way to tell Greyson it was safe to return. Regan suggested her Message Rite, and hunched over her little hand mirror as she tried to bridge a connection to Greyson's mind.<br /> <br /> The Rite worked. Greyson stirred to life, breathing out a shuddering thank you in the First Tongue. At around this point, a shaken-looking Aed entered the room. He was pleased to see Greyson up and talking, but had some good news and bad news to share. He'd been hunting in the dreams of the Cleareyes, and discovered a few things. First he encountered the emaciated man he and Aaron had seen when they visited the Cleareyes. That man was sleeping in one of the buildings in the compound, the smallest one right next to the rust-coloured stone monolith that sat at the centre of the Pure compound. His dreamscape had been barren and, strangely, entirely defenceless. The only thing in the room with him was a trapdoor, which Aed was confident would be where the Pure were keeping Jamie.<br /> <br /> The bad news, however, was that he'd then entered the dreams of an unknown Pure. She'd taken the form of a withered, ancient crone. He'd decided to force her awake, stalking her and driving her to exhaustion in her dreams and making sleep itself foreign to her. It had been a struggle, as if the material of her dreams resisted him in a way he wasn't prepared for, but it had worked. He'd then slipped into the dreamscape of the the third Cleareyes, a second Pure he didn't recognise. And it was at this point he put two and two together. It was known that there were six Cleareyes, but two hadn't been seen in some time. He'd seen four of them when he and Aaron visited the compound, and now he'd seen another two. With a sinking feeling he'd used the final sleeping Cleareyes' dreams to scry out her surroundings. He'd seen something resembling an infirmary, another one of the buildings in the Pure compound. Two of the beds were occupied, one by the sleeping girl whose dreams he was in, the other by the ancient crone he'd just forced awake. The other Cleareyes were gathered around the bedside of the old woman, and though words were difficult to hear precisely through the fog of dreams he got the impression of repeated variations on the theme of, &quot;It's a miracle!&quot;<br /> <br /> So, basically, they now had five Cleareyes to deal with instead of four. Aaron, somewhat bitterly, sniped that Aed could hardly accuse him of recklessness now. The two packs tried to work out a plan for getting Jamie back from there. They could just try to steal her away from the Cleareyes, but that would no doubt just invite retaliation. Aed asked Greyson what the Whispers knew about the Cleareyes' totem. The Whispers hadn't ever encountered the totem during their raids of Cleareyes territory, but they did know that the Cleareyes compound surrounded a Locus, the rust-coloured monolith, that shared a resonance of earth and violence.<br /> <br /> The problem now was that they were outnumbered. Greyson was still healing from his experience with <i>Mazdarix</i>, and Aaron was in no shape to fight. Everyone promptly ignored Aaron's insistence that he still intended to join them. They were now up against five Pure.<br /> <br /> Finally, Aed turned to the Whispers and asked them why they were fighting the Cleareyes in the first place. The story, as it transpired, was that the Blackstaff wolves had no Locus in their territory. After they lost their Totem, their own essence reserves ended up largely depleted. They couldn't take a new totem, no spirit would accept them until they dealt with the spirit that had become We-Who-Eat-In-Darkness and in doing so sever their old ties. But without essence there was little they could do as their territory spiralled more and more out of control. The closest locus that they knew of at the time (and here Dorian cast an eye towards the mirror in Aed's living room, the locus where Hope made her home) was on Cleareyes turf, so they began raiding Cleareyes territory for essence. When pressed, they denied being responsible for two of the Cleareyes being put into enchanted sleep.<br /> <br /> After hearing the story, Aed announced that he had a solution. He'd tried to resolve things with Eaven amicably, but he'd lacked anything she wanted as much as a new convert. Now, possibly, he had something. The Whispers agreed to a non-violent solution so long as they got Jamie back and the Harbingers helped deal with We-Who-Eat-In-Darkness.<br /> <br /> Aed had Regan send another Message to Eaven, asking her to meet the Whispers and Harbingers at their old meeting spot. &quot;And give my regards to sleeping beauty.&quot;<br /> <br /> The message certainly piqued her interest. Eaven showed up shortly after the other two packs arrived. She came alone, and stood on her side of the grove with her feet rooted in her territory. After a momentary diversion caused by Eaven's shock at the state of Aaron's face, Aed convinced her that he was responsible for waking up her sleeping packmate, and that he was willing to wake the other one so long as she agreed to turn over Jamie. Eaven agreed, hashing out some additional terms. The Whispers were to return to Blackstaff. If they ever set foot in Andersonstown, or on Cleareyes turf, then the Harbingers and Cleareyes would work together to destroy them. And Aed would wake up the sixth Cleareyes before they handed over Jamie.<br /> <br /> Bearing in mind Eaven's Honour, her reputation of having never told a lie, the Harbingers agreed to the terms. He dropped himself into a supernaturally induced sleep, and entered the dreams of the sleeping girl once again. Just as with the old woman, he had to struggle to impose his will on the dreamscape of the sleeping girl, but he had an advantage over the force that opposed him. His affinity for dreams, Auspice-given and Luna-blessed, allowed him to navigate the dreamscape as if it were his own. He drove and harried the avatar of the girl until she, too, was forced back to consciousness.<br /> <br /> Eaven had Aaron and Aed accompany her back to the compound to retrieve Jamie. To Aed's surprise, Jamie turned out to not be in the building with the trapdoor, but in the fourth building he had yet to see inside of. There, they found a battered Jamie sitting in a room bereft of floorboards. The bare earth had been carved with savage, angular runes, and watching over her was a horrifying man-shaped being of raw, pink flesh. Eaven called the creature Num-Num, and he appeared to be immediately taken with Aed. He couldn't speak properly, just making happy gurgling noises at the sight of the newcomers, before swapping to a pained whine when Eaven released Jamie. Aaron pieces together enough of what was going on to realise they were in the presence of a <i>duguthim</i>, and accused Eaven of being a monster. She got defensive. The creature had been irrevocably fused with its host when she found it, and it was better that it be locked away here than to have a Torture Claimed wandering around loose.<br /> <br /> They left Num-Num waving sadly at the departing Aed, and escorted Jamie back to the border. It was later that night that each of them felt Luna's approval for their actions. For treating with their enemies and finding a diplomatic solution between three packs on the brink of war, each of them received new brands of Honour.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-11-11T18:25:58-08:00","id":1266561,"title":"","content":"It turns out sometimes, just sometimes, a player goofing around with a new power and randomly interacting with things in their environment can be a good thing for all concerned <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I can't really make it clear how stunned Aed's sequence of dreamhunting left me. That was an entirely separate story thread to the events that were playing out, the players' only contact with it were the rumours about the missing Pure. And then he went and accidentally resolved it because he was just trying out the different effects of his previously unused Gift.<br /> <br /> Didn't get the aspiration for that though. Definitely not weakening.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-11-11T18:59:01-08:00","id":1266573,"title":"","content":"&quot;Unfortunately, they hadn't been fast enough to save Aaron's eyes, but they'd grow back eventually.&quot;<br /> Classic.<br /> <br /> Aed to Scarred Beast Burning: &quot;B#&amp;$h, you do what I say!&quot;. Seriously, that PC's Social is a deadly weapon.<br /> <br /> &quot;Orla bound the spirit to a single bullet, &quot; <br /> ...What? A <i>single</i> bullet?<br /> <br /> Yay, some good thinking led to happy outcomes.<br /> <br /> What is with Aed? Presence 5+ or something?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":7212,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","user":"Vent0","date":". 11-11-2018, 10:08 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-11-11T19:33:13-08:00","id":1266581,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1266573#post1266573\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">&quot;Unfortunately, they hadn't been fast enough to save Aaron's eyes, but they'd grow back eventually.&quot;<br /> Classic.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Specifically, Aaron is currently on 8 Aggravated damage. His first eyeball grows back once he heals 4 of that, the second eyeball heals after the other 4.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1266573#post1266573\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">&quot;Orla bound the spirit to a single bullet, &quot;<br /> ...What? A <i>single</i> bullet?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, just the one. Given Scarred Beast Burning's nature, I was discussing with Orla and Regan's players what sort of Fetishes they could make. The spirit is basically an animalistic fear creature, so we settled on two different options. If bound to the gun, it would provide an enormous bonus to intimidation checks if fired to make a point rather than to wound (i.e. up in the air or something). If bound to a bullet, they could create a bullet with teeth that would tear into victims from the inside. The bullet is particularly durable and can be retrieved from the flesh of a victim with an extended medicine roll, and reforms itself to fit the barrel of any gun it's loaded into.<br /> <br /> Main problem with it is it's a bit nippy. Tries to take your fingers off as you're loading it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1266573#post1266573\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yay, some good thinking led to happy outcomes.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, first it was the RPG equivalent of random button mashing. &quot;Let's see, new gift. Let's do this effect. And this effect. And... whoops. Uh, about that second effect...? And why was I rolling a Clash of Wills for that?&quot;<br /> <br /> But yes, good thinking then followed and turned a bad thing into a very good thing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1266573#post1266573\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Aed to Scarred Beast Burning: &quot;B#&amp;$h, you do what I say!&quot;. Seriously, that PC's Social is a deadly weapon.<br /> <br /> \u200bWhat is with Aed? Presence 5+ or something?</div> </div> </div> </div>I want to say Presence 4, but I don't know for sure off-hand. His main advantage is, well, partly it's a player who knows the Social Maneuvering rules like the back of his hand. He's got a very high Socialise, high Persuasion and Intimidation, and then a selection of merits like Striking Looks, Fame, and Sympathetic. The latter is why he's Leveraged/Swooning with half the NPCs he's ever met, although a lot of those Conditions were resolved this session. Obviously, not all of those are useful when dealing with spirits, but he's had a good eye for <i>gathra</i> so far and with the Scarred Beast Burning he had no qualms about just brute forcing the roll.<br /> <br /> Oh, the other thing in his favour is the Voice of Glory Gift and a Socialize specialty in &quot;Strangers&quot;. So he makes a <i>really</i> good First Impression, and then leverages that to allow him to complete what would ordinarily be a lengthy social maneuvering sequence in a convenient time frame. He often uses Voice of Glory twice a session, I honestly don't know if we've had one where he didn't use it at least once.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-11-12T07:18:51-08:00","id":1266688,"title":"","content":"To keep track, Aed has Social-Fu'd:<br /> \u2022 Sent a full Suthilu cave into retreat<br /> \u2022 Talked a Death-god into granting them power<br /> \u2022 Made a Spirit <s>his bi$#h</s> a Fetish<br /> \u2022 Brought peace to warring Forsaken and Pure Packs, when the Pure were clearly winning<br /> \u2022 ...And failed to impress a human girl.<br /> <br /> Oh, and random Darkness and Claimed fawn over him.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-11-13T04:21:57-08:00","id":1266916,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1266688#post1266688\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To keep track, Aed has Social-Fu'd:<br /> \u2022 Sent a full Suthilu cave into retreat<br /> \u2022 Talked a Death-god into granting them power<br /> \u2022 Made a Spirit <s>his bi$#h</s> a Fetish<br /> \u2022 Brought peace to warring Forsaken and Pure Packs, when the Pure were clearly winning<br /> \u2022 ...And failed to impress a human girl.<br /> <br /> Oh, and random Darkness and Claimed fawn over him.</div> </div> </div> </div>A part of me finds it endlessly fascinating that my players have been more willing to talk their way through problems in this game, where they are probably as powerful as they have ever been short of Mummy, compared to any of the other games I've run.<br /> <br /> Aed is certainly an impressive social-fu artist though. Voice of Glory really can't be understated I think. After he defeated the Suthilu, his Glory rose up to 3. Activating it for the cost of an Essence point is like spending Willpower on every Persuasion roll. Except he can spend Willpower on top and, if he really needs it, use his Cahalith auspice ability to get +9 to a single roll. When you can leverage that you can get by with even mediocre social skills, and mediocre is not how I'd describe Aed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-11-18T17:59:47-08:00","id":1268673,"title":"","content":"Session 14<br /> <br /> Without the worry of war with the Cleareyes, the Harbingers returned to their homes and began to relax. They spend the next few days on their own affairs. Aed began to organise a New Year's Eve party, splashing out with some of his film earnings and using his affinity for local politics to arrange the local school's hall as the set for a big public gathering. He convinced Andersonstown's pubs to supply the alcohol, and Lizzie and her band to be the entertainment. He had Regan help him put up flyers throughout their territory, and even took out an ad in the local paper.<br /> <br /> During a break among these activities, Aaron had his pack escort him through the Shadow so that he could recuperate in the Glade and try to restore his connection to the place. He asked Aed to escort him, and Aed asked Regan and Orla to come along to keep his entourage of minor violence spirits entertained while he was settling Aaron in safely. He told the two women that the little spirits liked being told stories, the bloodier the better, so Regan had the bright idea of just describing action scenes from Aed's movies to the spirits. They listened with rapt attention, almost too entranced to squabble among themselves. Orla, for her part, seemed just as entranced by the vivid descriptions of broken noses and shattered bones, sitting in the midst of the crowd of gremlins with an almost identical expression on her face.<br /> <br /> Inside the Glade, Aed propped Aaron against a tree and left him there. He asked the trees of the Glade to watch his friend, and to thank Silkenspun for the information that had led them to Cairnbreath. The trees whispered their acknowledgement, and he left promising Aaron that he'd be back to pick him up the next day.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately, the next day saw Aed get invited out to the pub by Lizzie, where she introduced him to the rest of the band. The afternoon's fun ran into the evening, and when Aed made his way home he had quite forgotten about Aaron. He only remembered early the next morning. He leapt out of bed and raced through the Shadow to the Glade, pulling a shirt over his head as he ran, and found a frustrated Aaron largely unaware of how much time had passed. Aed managed to play it cool and pretend he was on time, and he helped Aaron back to the Locus as Aaron bemoaned the fact that he'd been unable to restore his connection to the Glade.<br /> <br /> On New Year's Eve, the day of the party and the day the Harbingers were due to return to the Old Man's court and determine whether they had successfully resolved the problem of the Broken and the quarry, Aaron found himself waking to a heavy pressure on his chest and a hot breath on his face. A voice snarled at him, a voice he recalled from the tunnels beneath Blackstaff, as the wolf spirit he'd allowed to possess him named him Chainbreaker and accused him of striking at the very legend upon which Belfast's packs had been founded. Freeing Cairnbreath was a betrayal of the ancient packs, the founders who had fought to tame the Spirit Wilds around the city. Aaron kept his cool and asked why Cairnbreath had been imprisoned in the first place, for he'd seen nothing to indicate why the ancient god had been sealed away. That, the wolf refused to answer. When Aaron then asked who the wolf spirit was, he was interrupted by a knock on the door. A moment later, the pressure on his chest disappeared.<br /> <br /> Aaron found that Peter, his Wolf-Blooded deacon, had come to get him because a police officer was asking to speak to him. Regan had explained to him just the day before what had happened with Constable Arthur Cosgrave, so Aaron asked Peter to show the man in. Arthur was a little more calm than he'd been when Regan had spoke to him, even confronted by an oracle-like blind man claiming to be a Werewolf and, on a spiritual level, a cousin of sorts to him. Aaron explained a few of the basics of Wolf-Blooded and their relationship to the <i>Uratha</i>, and asked Arthur how he was coping. Arthur confessed that he was worried his position at his job would be in jeopardy soon, and that he was hearing whispers. Several times during this conversation he broke off to talk to himself. At last, Aaron asked Arthur to describe what was happening with his eye. Arthur talked about how he could sometimes feel something pushing through his eye and into anyone he made eye contact with. The sensation was impossible to truly describe, but Arthur found it deeply unpleasant. When Aaron suggested he might have developed the Evil Eye due to his Wolf-Blooded nature, Arthur finally hit his limit. He babbled some excuses and left, swearing to himself the whole way.<br /> <br /> In the aftermath, Aaron received a phone call from Iule. She was worried, she'd been trying to get in contact with him for the last day, but he hadn't been answering. Aaron explained that he'd been in the Shadow, and they arranged to meet on the border of their territory in around an hour. Aaron called Aed, interrupting him during a final party planning meeting with Lizzie, Regan, and Orla. Aed agreed to come and meet with Iule and to bring the rest of the pack, and told Lizzie she was in charge of the decoration budget. They didn't have a lot of cash and only a few hours to sort something out, but he wanted the night to have a rough Hollywood movie theme in his own honour.<br /> <br /> OOC: We called the session around then as a couple of players needed to leave. It was a nice breather given the drama and tension of the last session, but the little character moments that we spent all of this session on don't translate terribly well to how I do these write-ups sadly. Still, there's some stuff building in the background there and next session we should finally get to see some of the ongoing ramifications of the players' first <i>Siskur-Dah</i>, with both spying on the army occupation of the quarry and visiting the Old Man's court for a final judgement on the matter.<br /> <br /> And, amidst it all, a party!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-12-02T21:56:10-08:00","id":1272537,"title":"","content":"So after missing a session because I was out of town playing other games, we returned to the Harbingers for:<br /> <br /> Session 15<br /> <br /> With Lizzie and her crew busy preparing for the New Year's Eve party, the Harbingers collected Aaron and prepared for their meeting with the Silent Sons. Make-up and fashion advice from Aed and Regan respectively conspired to make Aaron quite difficult to recognise, in case he ran into any parishioners while wandering the streets in his eyeless condition. They made their way to the border of their territory with the Silent Sons then, to the prearranged meeting spot on Glen Road. Iule was there, and Aaron was able to introduce her to the others. The other Silent Sons were a bald, heavily tattooed man that Regan vaguely knew by reputation as a local criminal named Bull, an older man with long greying hair named Gilroy, and a voice Aaron vaguely recognised from his school days long ago as Sammie Johanesson. Those of the Harbingers with eyes could also see the reflection of pale eyes in the window of the closest building, watching them, and Iule introduced her pack's totem, Farraige the Silence in the Mist.<br /> <br /> The two packs moved on from introductions to discuss why they had gathered. The Silent Sons had been hunting, and been hunted in return, by a unit of soldiers operating out of the military fort in Andersonstown. The fort, code named Silver City, had recently started shifting large numbers of men and material to the Black Mountain quarry. The Sons wanted to work alongside the Harbingers to set up a watch on the fort. Nothing overt yet, they just wanted to figure out what Silver City was up to and monitor the situation. They warned the Harbingers to be on the lookout for heavily armed soldiers packing silver bullets, many of them veterans of enough fights with <i>Uratha</i> to have become scarred and resistant to Lunacy.<br /> <br /> The Harbingers were happy to help, though Aed warned that there were few members of the pack that were good enough at stealth to keep watch. Iule pointed out that, with herself as the exception, every member of the Silent Sons was an Irraka. They would be able to help the Harbingers in an emergency. She asked if the others wanted to go and take a look at the quarry now. The Harbingers unanimously agreed to allow it, but Aed said he had an errand he needed to run. The others, even Aaron, set off towards Black Mountain in the hopes of getting high enough on the slopes to see into the quarry and scope it out.<br /> <br /> Aed, for his part, headed across to the Cleareyes territory. His car turned into the compound in time to see all six of the Cleareyes spilling out of the different buildings, many of them shifting to <i>Dalu</i> in preparation for a fight. Eaven held them back from attacking when she saw who it was, but she was a little more hostile towards Aed than he expected. He'd come to invite the Cleareyes to the Andersonstown party, and was a little surprised to hear that the Pure might consider that a violation of their borders. To him, territories only existed for what he called &quot;Werewolf business&quot;, and more mundane matters shouldn't be impacted. Nevertheless, he was able to convince Eaven to bring her most eager packmate, Aine, along to the party.<br /> <br /> Back on Black Mountain, Regan hoped to impress her fellow Irraka. Like them, she melted into the shadows, though the fumbling efforts of her packmates drove her to distraction and made her worry that it would reflect poorly on her. She used Pack Stalks the Prey to quiet Orla's thunderous passage through the undergrowth, and guided Aaron up the slope to the lookout point.<br /> <br /> They found a spot where they could all look down into the quarry. Orla and Regan were the most observant of the group, pointing out to Iule and her compatriots that there was a small tunnel in the mountainside that seemed to be the hub of activity. Stretchers and body bags lay outside it, though none appeared to be occupied. Regan also noted that barbed wire had been used liberally even inside the quarry's perimeter, as if the soldiers had prepared multiple fallback points in case their outer defences were breached.<br /> <br /> After a short few minutes, the two packs agreed to leave Gilroy of the Silent Sons behind on watch. The remaining members of the pack were invited to the New Year's Eve party, which they happily accepted. The Harbingers split, with Regan taking Aaron home and then heading to the corner shop where Steph worked to chat. Aunt and niece debated for a while what sort of Hollywood-themed costumes they could dig up at short notice, eventually settling on Han and Leia from the new Star Wars sequel as the best bet since they could cobble together approximately the right outfit just from what they had in their cupboards.<br /> <br /> Orla, for her part, wrapped herself in a white blanket and put a discarded Christmas wreath on her head and declared herself Julius Caesar.<br /> <br /> All of the Harbingers, save Aaron, reunited at Regan's school hall. The party was already getting started without them, with Lizzie and her band, the Broken Commitments, playing loudly at the head of the hall. Many of the more conservative members of the community were visibly put out by the music choice but the beer was flowing liberally enough that no one seemed too unhappy. Steph ran into Aed, who was wearing a Diamondman outfit taken from the set of his last movie, and they struck up a conversation complimenting each other on their outfits. Regan slipped away, spotting Orla surrounded by a group of similarly heavy drinkers and making a determined effort to get through all the kegs supplied by the local bars. Roger and Jo Austin spotted Aed's Diamondman costume and pushed their way over to him, bloating his ego considerably by exclaiming their excitement over it.<br /> <br /> Hours in, each of the wolves noticed a heavy thump on the roof. Independently, they all managed to sneak away from their conversations without notice or being followed. They convened outside and decided to boost Orla up to check out the roof. She found toeholds where there shouldn't have been any, hauling herself up the side of the hall with a few immense thrusts from her powerful thighs, and reached the lip in time to come nose-to-snout with something huge, winged, and covered in fur.<br /> <br /> Orla reacted by instinct, seizing the creature by the snout. It reared back, hauling her up onto the roof, momentarily out of sight from Regan and Aed. Before the alarm could truly register they saw the creature lift up from the roof, grappling with Orla, until it wrenched itself free far above the ground. Orla twisted in the air, barely grabbing the edge of the roof before she fell to the ground.<br /> <br /> Conscious of the fact that they were barely out of sight of the party, Regan and Aed circled around towards the school's oval trying to draw the creature away. They managed to get a better view of it as it circled towards them. Han and Diamondman squared off on a white field against the beast out of darkness. It had once been human, there was no doubt of that, but its nose was broken and folded up into its face by a protruding maw, and its body was covered in patches of thick, rank fur. Membranous wings connected too-thin arms down to its waist and covered a wing span almost half a dozen metres in width. It lashed at Regan with its claws, and her body shifted reflexively into <i>Garou</i> to keep herself conscious as those claws raked open her chest. Aed moved to flank the creature but it moved faster than either wolf could believe, dodging attacks from both wolves and slicing open Regan's gut in return. Her flesh knit together almost as quickly, but then the beast was leaping onto Aed and lifting off with him clutched in its claws.<br /> <br /> As they fought, the two wolves could hear the beast talking constantly. It moved its head back and forth, shaking its ears with irritation as the punk rock blasting from the hall a short distance away seemed to interfere with its hearing. The constant babble had a few clear fragments.<br /> <br /> &quot;The Herald must be raised up, uplifted.&quot;<br /> &quot;Blood and fire will rain from the sky. Birth a beautiful wound. Beautiful.&quot;<br /> &quot;Stupid wolves. Fear the Scream of Slaughter. I will drain you, drink you whole.&quot;<br /> <br /> Back on the rooftop, Orla could see that her pack was in trouble. Even as she moved to assist, however, she stumbled across a duffel bag left behind by the Scream. The sound of ticking reached her ears, and she pulled it open to see a rudimentary bomb. Luckily, she was the only member of the pack that would have had any idea what to do. Vague memories of similar devices let her check quickly to see how much time she had time left, and she set to work pulling wires and working the detonator free. If she failed, she knew in the back of her mind that she would have just enough time to try and cross the gauntlet with the explosive and let it detonate there away from the people below.<br /> <br /> Aed slammed into the snow-covered fields and rolled onto his back with a grunt. The Scream circled and dove again, but the music from the hall only seemed to be getting louder. At the last moment the immense <i>Suthilu</i> veered away, screaming its frustration, and Aed had a glimpse of its blind, milky eyes. The noise was interfering with its ability to see. Cursing them loudly, the hybrid took off into the darkness, vanishing swiftly from sight.<br /> <br /> When the others reached the entrance to the hall, they found a happy Orla dropping to the ground beside them with a duffel bag in her hand, pleased that she'd disarmed it. Regan did a quick circle of the hall, looking for signs of any other bombs, but couldn't find any. Aed sent Orla back to his home with the bomb, hoping that it wouldn't be recognised, and then he and Regan came up with an excuse for their dishevelled state. When they went back inside, they claimed they'd been using Aed's knowledge of make-up and special effects to give Regan's clothes a blood stained affect, as if she'd just been attacked by one of the more monstrous aliens from those movies. Jo, in particular, proved hugely impressed by this.<br /> <br /> Aed circled around the party as the last minutes before he needed to leave ticked away. He spent some time talking to Roger, and began to understand the full scope of the hardship the Harbingers had inflicted on the community by closing down the quarry. To make it up to Roger, Aed tried to call over Martin Coveney, the local journalist, to take a picture of them together in their matching Diamondman outfits, but the man he thought was Martin turned out to be a confused man with a slight English accent with roughly the same build. Aed apologised, not recognising the man at all, and then spotted Martin a bit further away. Then, finally, he spotted Eaven leaning against a wall, watching her packmate Aine dance with some of the younger men. Aed pushed through to Aine and moved close enough to strike up a conversation. Aine's guard dropped quickly in the face of Aed's literally supernatural magnetism, and he started to ply her for information about Eaven. Aine revealed that Eaven didn't have a job, the Pure all allowed their Wolfblooded to provide for them, but she did let slip that Eaven was a big fan of dogs. They didn't keep any on the compound because of the landmines, obviously, but there was a farm run by a Wolfblooded vassal of theirs that Eaven went to in order to relax.<br /> <br /> Armed with this knowledge, Aed departed to flirt with Eaven with the little time he had left. He failed to notice the starstruck look Aine cast after him as he left. Eaven was a little less impressed than her packmate, but even her guard started to lower before Aed realised that he needed to leave to make the scheduled meeting with Old Man Mountain to discuss the closure of the quarry.<br /> <br /> He was on his way to the exit when the Silent Sons confronted him about the presence of Pure at this party. Aed persuaded them, barely, that it was an effort to keep the peace. War didn't benefit either side at the moment, and the Pure would be leaving soon anyway. The Sons seemed to buy this, but it took Aed pulling rank as as a Blood Talon to persuade them that he knew best.<br /> <br /> A short time later, with the party still raging, Aed, Regan, Orla, and Aaron passed into the Shadow and began their track up the slopes of Black Mountain. It was time to stand before the Old Man and see if their first ever hunt was deemed as much a success as they felt it was.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-12-03T05:25:20-08:00","id":1272586,"title":"","content":"That... didn't seem to end in nor lead to disaster this time. I'm impressed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-12-04T14:57:30-08:00","id":1272893,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1272586#post1272586\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That... didn't seem to end in nor lead to disaster this time. I'm impressed.</div> </div> </div> </div>More importantly, it featured Aed dressed as a Superman knock-off squaring off against a bat-man across an icy field on New Year's Eve with punk rock blasting in the background.<br /> <br /> Sometimes you get a single scene and just know you're onto a winner with a campaign.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-12-04T16:07:32-08:00","id":1272902,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1272893#post1272893\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">More importantly, it featured Aed dressed as a Superman knock-off squaring off against a bat-man across an icy field on New Year's Eve with punk rock blasting in the background.<br /> <br /> Sometimes you get a single scene and just know you're onto a winner with a campaign.</div> </div> </div> </div>That, I did not know, because I wasn't up on the context.<br /> <br /> Which just makes that session <i>all the cooler</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-12-04T16:23:02-08:00","id":1272904,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1272902#post1272902\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That, I did not know, because I wasn't up on the context.<br /> <br /> Which just makes that session <i>all the cooler</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Also featured: Han Solo helping out the Superman knock-off, and Julius Caesar the bomb disposal expert.<br /> <br /> It was pretty awesome. I did try to work those elements into the fight description but I might have neglected to specifically call out how darn cool the actual fight scene was and just left it to inference.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-12-17T18:37:48-08:00","id":1274993,"title":"","content":"Session 16<br /> <br /> The Harbingers climbed the slopes leading to the Old Man's court. In the <i>Hisil</i>, they could see the party lighting up the school hall in a brilliant display of light. The creeping darkness and despair they'd been accustomed to in the Shadow appeared absent for once, and they could see Luna's shifting children dancing in the skies above the Old Man's peak. When they crested the summit, minutes before midnight, they could see the entrance to the court was open once again. They passed through the darkness and its shifting cavern of chains to step out into the crater sheltering the court.<br /> <br /> The changes in the court were few and subtle, but the Harbingers did notice that there were fewer Broken huddled beneath the eaves of the Willow Wardens. Those that did sit there in shadows did not wail or cry out as they watched the <i>Uratha</i> pass. They arrived before the Old Man's throne, with Aed keeping a sharp eye out for Clifford. The human did not appear to be anywhere in sight.<br /> <br /> Regan raised her hand in greeting, and the Old Man bowed his head in acknowledgement. His gaze swept across the pack, examining each wolf in turn, but he paused when he reached Father Aaron. A moment later and he was on his feet. Never before had any of the Harbingers seen the Old Man rise from his throne, and as he stepped closer he began to shift. His jaw jutted forward, distending into a muzzle, and the mane of moss he had in place of hair flowed out across his neck and shoulders. He looked like a Werewolf shifting from <i>Dalu</i> to <i>Garou</i>, and he closed the distance between himself and Aaron in two shift strides.<br /> <br /> &quot;I took upon this duty for you, at your own request, and yet you go behind my back! Why? And how has he broken free? The chains have told me nothing!&quot;<br /> <br /> Aed reacted the fastest, asking the Old Man if this was something to do with Cairnbreath. The ancient mountain spirit took a few moments to reply, the expression on his face moving from anger to confusion. &quot;No. What has happened to that old creature?&quot;<br /> <br /> Moving right along before that avenue of questioning could be pursued, Aaron thought of the wolf spirit he'd encountered far underground. He described it to the Old Man, who grew increasingly concerned and more than a little baffled. A moment later, the Harbingers felt the ground give out beneath them. The sensation of falling was unpleasantly familiar to Aaron and Aed, but they found themselves safely on their feet somewhere new. They were underground, the Old Man still standing before them, in a cavern the likes of which they had never seen even in all their underground delving. It stretched out into the darkness, the shadows illuminated by four torches taller than a man placed in a diamond up ahead. Each torch stood at a cardinal point, and though the fire in them remained lit they looked to be ancient constructions. Bands of bronze wrapped around crude wooden shafts, pitted and corroded with age. And in the midst of this diamond, at its centre, lay the wolf.<br /> <br /> He was clearly male, an <i>Uratha</i> in his <i>Garou</i> form. He was big, larger than either Aaron or Aed in their war forms, but perhaps a little smaller than Orla. In spite of that his presence seemed to distort the entire chamber, as though he were the centre of his own gravity well. The stink of him assailed their noses, nearly overwhelming the blind Aaron in particular, who pleaded with the others to tell him what was happening. They could hear his heavy breathing, though he didn't stir at their presence. His fur was pure white, and around his limbs and torso were bound lengths of shining gold chain.<br /> <br /> Aed took a wild stab at what was happening, and asked the Old Man to confirm his theory. An old wolf had gone into the Death Rage, and the Old Man had taken him here to keep him from hurting anyone. If he woke up, his rage would continue where it left off. But hearing this, the Old Man shook his head. He had begun to calm upon seeing that his prisoner was still bound, in spite of his fears. Aed's incorrect guess confused the spirit though. He asked the Harbingers why they did not know who this was, when they had been the ones to ask him to act as warden. It took a little bit of probing, but eventually they worked out that the Old Man had a pretty poor grasp of Werewolf lifespans and was confusing them with other wolves from centuries, maybe even millennia, prior. The Old Man sobered at that, and told them that this story was not his to tell. They should seek out a loremaster of their own kind, one who would know the tale.<br /> <br /> And then they were all back in the Old Man's court, as suddenly as they had left. The Old Man's form shifted away from its wolfish aspect as he returned to his throne, and with a wave of his hand a fissure in the earth opened and spat out a frozen-looking Clifford. Without mention of what had happened, the Old Man asked Clifford whether he considered himself satisfied at the closure of the quarry. Clifford announced that he was, and thanked the Harbingers for doing what they had to help the spirits of the mountain. Aed, however, wasn't satisfied. He urged Clifford to turn himself in to the constabulary for the murder of the mine's foreman. At least bring closure to the man's family. This was not a suggestion that Clifford was particularly open to, and in spite of Aed's best work he couldn't convince him to do more than make an anonymous tip-off to the police so that they could locate the dead man's body. Aed wasn't helped by overly aggressive packmates, who tried to harangue and intimidate the human into following through while Aed was trying for a more emotive angle. At last Clifford thanked them one more time for what they had done, and told the Old Man that their business was concluded. Another Shadow Gateway was torn open through the earth and sent the human back to the flesh.<br /> <br /> The Harbingers returned via their own locus, and held a quick meeting. They needed to give Aaron time to recover from his wounds before braving any conflicts, but Aed would do what he could to dig up information on the wolf in chains buried beneath Black Mountain. Aaron was insistent that their next hunt would be to track down the Scream of Slaughter, but he acknowledged the need to give his eyes time to regenerate.<br /> <br /> Aed's best opportunity came over the next week, when he was meeting with Iule to discuss the quarry's sentry roster for the next few days. She told him that Laurence of the Symphony Cascade was the oldest Cahalith she knew in the city, but he wasn't the only other loremaster. Reynard of the <i>Kallakalat</i>, a Wolf Blooded, was renowned for his knowledge. Aed took this information back to Regan, and had her perform the Message Rite to send word to Reynard. They offered an invitation for him to visit the Harbingers on their own ground, and gave Aed's address.<br /> <br /> School had resumed by Regan by this point, but Reynard's arrival fell upon that Saturday when the pack was already gathered at Aed's home. Reynard turned out to be an old man with tobacco-stained teeth and poor posture, but he greeted the Harbingers warmly. His attention was particularly fixed on Regan, and she saw his Adam's Apple bob nervously before he approached her. Back as stiff as it would get, Reynard held out one hand and introduced himself as Regan's grandfather. His son was Joseph Parnell, a name both Regan and Aaron immediately recognised. As they shook hands, Aaron started laughing softly to himself before exclaiming that Joseph had been the one in charge of Aaron's initiation rite into the Hunters in Darkness. Regan, for her part, struggled to process what she was hearing. She retreated into herself, backing away after mumbling a greeting in return, and the anger inside her built slowly in the background while Aed turned matters back to why they had summoned Reynard.<br /> <br /> First, the Wolf Blooded made it clear that Joseph expected something in return for this favour. In return for Reynard's help, the Harbingers would be called upon in the future to offer something of equivalent value. That was a promise Aed felt comfortable making, and they settled down around Aed's kitchen table. Aed described the wolf they had seen, bound in chains of gold, and Reynard didn't take long to work out which myth they were talking about. He said the spirit Sliverbug had told him this tale, and began his story.<br /> <br /> There was a time after <i>Urfarah</i>'s fall, when the People were hunted across the Earth. The Firstborn had not yet formed the tribes, and spirits hunted the People in retribution for the Great Wolf's death. This was when a great hunter rose in Ireland, a wolf born beneath a blood-red moon. He slaughtered his way across the <i>Hisil</i>, binding and dominating spirit after spirit, until even the primal forces of nature had been forced to boy before him. His pack grew and grew, absorbing all others he came across, until he had unified every pack in Ireland beneath his banner. There were whispers that he was akin to a new Firstborn, and this mighty alpha heard these whispers and pondered.<br /> <br /> The Celestines had grown increasingly concerned with this wolf's actions, as he and his followers scoured the <i>Hisil</i> bare without regard to balance. Yet Luna could not bring herself to act directly against one who had won such renown in her name. Her maddening songs of praise in his name called up the tides, raised Leviathans and mighty beasts from the ocean's depths, but the alpha slew them in turn and cast their broken bodies upon the rocky shore. Serpents shattered the cliffs and were sundered in turn. The alpha was winning even this war, against the ocean's relentless might, and the adoration of his followers turned inexorably to worship.<br /> <br /> Helios watched this, and knew that something must be done. His Hellions sought out those dissenters who yet remained among the alpha's pack, and forged for them chains of gold and sunlight that could restrain even the mightiest of the <i>Uratha</i>. This secret brotherhood hatched a plan, to seal this champion beyond Luna's sight somewhere far beneath the earth. Their moment came at the height of the war, when the ocean spat forth its greatest champion. The stories of what happened that day are muddled, confused, and the fate of the conspirators and the ocean's champion are unknown. The ocean receded, leaving only bloated bodies in its wake, and the great pack fragmented without its leader to guide it. What is clear, however, is that the alpha was never seen again after that day, and the story of the alpha's golden chains is one that still resonates with the spirits of Belfast.<br /> <br /> His story finished, Reynard made to leave. He told Regan that he hoped to see her again, and got only a surly grunt in reply. The mood in the room after his departure was somber. Reynard had that the <i>Kallakalat</i> would keep the actual, verified existence of the Wolf in Golden Chains a secret. For the Harbingers, they were grappling with the knowledge that their territory was sitting atop, as Aed put it, a living weapon of mass destruction.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-12-18T02:05:05-08:00","id":1275048,"title":"","content":"I enjoyed reading this session, but what gets me is that we were on the egde of an important reveal, and then everybody got distracted.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>~<span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u266b</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7593-teatime","name":"Teatime","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7593_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"750"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-12-18T05:32:10-08:00","id":1275064,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Teatime</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1275048#post1275048\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I enjoyed reading this session, but what gets me is that we were on the egde of an important reveal, and then everybody got distracted.</div> </div> </div> </div>Which reveal was that?<br /> <br /> Looking back I did miss a couple of potentially relevant details. Namely:<br /> <br /> -Aed did take the opportunity to ask Reynard who Ur-Baal was, it was a name he'd heard a few times in the Shadow. According to Reynard the name refers to one of the Court of the Union's generals, a war spirit rumoured to have been born at the dawn of the 20th Century by the conflicts in Ireland.<br /> -The characters did suss out that, based on the murals in his tomb, Cairnbreath (and the enemy spirit he was depicted fighting) were the &quot;primal forces&quot; mentioned as the first significant victory of the Wolf's rise to power.<br /> -In case it wasn't explicit, the players did put two and two together and assumed that the wolf spirit that Aaron agreed to let possess him was likely a manifestation of the Wolf in Golden Chains of some kind. Hence the Old Man smelling the Wolf on him.<br /> <br /> Also, just in case anyone needs the reminders, we've got chains as a recurring motif in Belfast (the Wolf aside, Aaron broke the chains in Cairnbreath's tomb and the entrance to the Old Man's court is decorated with a series of chains) and the Cahalith Laurence that they opted not to contact belongs to the Symphony Cascade, which is a pack Aaron heard of during the Hunter's Run in the house on Longwood Avenue (the story sung during the Run was that they had allied with Hellions and braved Helios's scorn to drive all shadow from their territory so that they could hunt a Shadow Claimed that had crossed over from Blackstaff, and the litany of thanks they gave to Helios at the end was probably the first explicit reference to the &quot;Alpha's Golden Chains&quot;).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-12-18T07:15:06-08:00","id":1275072,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1275064#post1275064\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In case it wasn't explicit, the players did put two and two together and assumed that the wolf spirit that Aaron agreed to let possess him was likely a manifestation of the Wolf in Golden Chains of some kind. Hence the Old Man smelling the Wolf on him.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah... that's the bit I missed. I assumed neither Cairnbreath nor Wolf in Golden Chains were what the Old Man was speaking about.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>~<span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u266b</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7593-teatime","name":"Teatime","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7593_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"750"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-12-18T18:09:55-08:00","id":1275215,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Teatime</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1275072#post1275072\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ah... that's the bit I missed. I assumed neither Cairnbreath nor Wolf in Golden Chains were what the Old Man was speaking about.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, it isn't confirmed that it was why the Old Man became so riled up. The other theory was that the wolf spirit might just be an agent of the Wolf in Golden Chains. But they're pretty confident that the wolf spirit must have some connection because there aren't too many other things that Aaron has been exposed to that the rest of the pack hasn't, and the odds that the wolf that lived underground being the one linked to the werewolf kept prisoner underground seems high <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Edit: Rereading the bit in the Old Man's court again, I can see how you got a bit confused. To break down the scene a little further, the Old Man was accusing the Harbingers of going behind his back and making deals with the Wolf in Golden Chains, which confused him because he didn't entirely understand that the Harbingers had no connection to those ancient conspirators who persuaded him to lock the Wolf away and act as warden for all these intervening centuries. That left the Old Man confused, because he'd had no sign that the Wolf's prison had been disturbed, and betrayed because he thought his old allies were going behind his back. The players eventually managed to persuade him that they were way too young to have been involved in events thousands of years ago. So yeah, that initial accusation of betrayal levelled at Aaron was all about the Wolf to begin with, the players deduced it must have something to do with the wolf spirit during that conversation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1059,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","user":"Azahul","date":". 12-18-2018, 11:28 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-01-15T20:34:39-08:00","id":1281035,"title":"","content":"Ok, so we're back from holidays with a nice, simple session as the Harbingers followed through with one of their outstanding obligations.<br /> <br /> Also Orla's player is going to be absent for a while, not sure how long, for personal reasons. In-universe we've decided that she's gone off on some kind of spirit quest in the meantime, although that could just be code for following a band on tour around the country.<br /> <br /> Session 17<br /> <br /> Aaron's injuries healed slowly over the week following the New Year's events. His left eye regenerated first, slowly weaving itself whole again from the ruin the wound-bound spirit had made of his face. By the time he could see again, marvelling at how colours had returned all looking slightly different in some indefinable way, he was champing at the bit to return to the hunt. Though more and more bats seemed to be moving into Andersonstown, a worrying coincidence after their fight with the Bat Host, Aaron was reluctantly persuaded by his packmates that their first duty was to discharge an existing obligation. They had promised the Whispers as part of the peace treaty that they would lend their help in running down the <i>Magath</i> that had once been their pack totem. We-who-eat-in-darkness was still out there, causing trouble in Blackstaff and bringing more darkness spirits across the Gauntlet with each passing day, but with no Loci to give them essence and no Totem to allow them to perform the <i>Siskur-Dah</i>, the Whispers were unable to take action themselves.<br /> <br /> The three wolves still in Belfast headed north in Aaron's car. Aaron himself now felt comfortable going about in public, though his new eye was a little small and thoroughly bloodshot. By keeping his head bandaged he could sell to anyone that knew him that he had &quot;nearly lost an eye&quot; in the attack. It was evening on a Thursday, after Regan had been let out from school, and Aed took it as a point of pride that it was a Gibbous Moon that hung in the air for their first hunt of the new year.<br /> <br /> They reached the alleyway where they knew the Whispers had wards and waited for the other pack to arrive. The shadows in Blackstaff were as long and unnatural as ever, stretching out like it was late afternoon even during the middle of the day, and at night they seemed darker and utterly impenetrable.<br /> <br /> When the Whispers arrived, Jamie, Dorian, and Greyson all together, the two packs went straight down to business. Aaron tried to work out where the Gauntlet would be weakest, making it easy for We-who-eat-in-darkness to open gateways for her allies, while Jamie suggested they try to track her from where they had last seen her. That location was actually inside the Blackstaff River, running beneath their feet. The Harbingers followed the Whispers to their Den, somewhere quiet and out of the way where they could invoke the <i>Siskur-Dah</i> to have away to actually end the <i>Magath</i> permanently. The den looked like a simple, modest home from the outside, not far from the school where Regan had located Dorian previously, but its floorboards had been mostly ripped up and the places beneath filled with all kinds of traps. The living room was devoid of furniture, floor stained by past rituals, and it was here the Harbingers invoked the Rite.<br /> <br /> When the two packs left to track We-who-eat-in-darkness, the Whispers led them all to something that seemed remarkably similar to a sewer access point. They descended into the darkness.<br /> <br /> From their previous encounters with the <i>Magath</i>, the Harbingers were aware of that her Ban required her to follow the instructions of anyone who presented her with a great feast. Jamie added that, using her Gifts of the New Moon, she had determined that We-who-eat-in-darkness's Bane had changed as well from when she had been the Whispers' Totem. Growing stronger and warping in nature as she devoured more and more darkness essence, We-who-eat-in-darkness could no longer stand the light of the sun and moon conjoined. When the two celestials appeared in the sky together, she would burn and whither away into nothing.<br /> <br /> Aaron took point as they descended into the river, all of them shifting to join Greyson in <i>Urhan</i>, and began to track. The spirit's trail headed upstream. They were underground here, in a concrete tunnel through which waters flowed without ever seeing the light of day. The wolves had to swim and wade their way against the current, and after half an hour of this Aed was already feeling exhaustion creep in. The trail brought the two packs to a split in the tunnel, where a cavern appeared to have been bored through the concrete and into the earth beyond.<br /> <br /> The tunnel inside spiralled into a maze of rock formations, half-filled with water, but they had only gone a short distance before they heard voices in the distance babbling in the First Tongue. Aaron invoked his Hunter's Aspect, letting loose a shattering howl in the darkness. The hearts of the spirits ahead were filled with bravado, convincing them to Materialise and fight the wolves with tooth and claw. Moments later, the wave of darkness was upon them.<br /> <br /> At first, in the lightless depths, it was impossible to discern their attackers from the shadows all around. Aaron and Aed felt their flesh turn to rot, sloughing off as pulsating ulcers formed in their flesh. Regan turned towards them, and found herself with a pallid creature with bloated, white flesh. She recognised those wide, staring eyes from her encounter with them in the <i>Hisil</i>. Drowners. Long, pale fingers wrapped around her throat before she could react, and began to choke.<br /> <br /> Jamie saw the first Drowner appear, then a second move towards Aaron, and cried out, &quot;Drink the water!&quot; She bent down to do so and had managed to swallow a mouthful, when the water came alive. Aed recognised the leech-like Shadow spirits he had seen throughout the underground, swarming up and over the werewolves as they fought. One flung itself onto Aaron's back, another two latched onto Jamie. They could see Dorian battling something unseen in the darkness, and then, in the midst of the chaos, We-who-eat-in-darkness materialised among them.<br /> <br /> Greyson saw his former totem and seemed to forget the plan, which required the Harbingers to be the one to strike the killing blow. He shifted up into <i>Urshul</i>, gaining bulk and muscle, and threw himself at the creature. His teeth rent large holes in the <i>Magath</i>'s corpus as he bore it to the ground, but it seemed content to ignore him as it struggled back to its feet, wounds already closing. Aaron felt the rot in his body take greater hold, and only retained consciousness by flinging himself into the <i>Garou</i> form. Raging, he tore at the spirits clinging to his back, but the weaklings actually put up a fight. For a moment he felt his vision going dark, knowing instinctively that the rot had taken such a strong hold that it would take days to regenerate, before he finally sank his claws into the little wisps of shadow clinging to his body and rent them asunder.<br /> <br /> Regan was in a similarly dire position. The hands around her neck were choking, crushing her windpipe and stifling her cries. Aed went to help of his teammate, even as Jamie won her way through of the shadow leeches. It took three Werewolves to bring the first Drowner down, and it discorporated into little more than brackish water at the last. Aed and Regan immediately followed Jamie's instructions, drinking deep from the water around them.<br /> <br /> By this point, only Aaron hadn't had a moment to drink, and it was clear that he was the focus of the surviving spirits. The onslaught of decay continued to ruin his body even as he turned his attention towards We-who-eat-in-darkness. He struggled to harm the creature through its rolls of fat, but at last his teeth sank deep into her neck and struck something that made her squeal. Her injuries were closing rapidly, but not enough to keep up with the onslaught as Regan and Aed joined the attack. Each Harbinger had their teeth into a different part of the <i>Magath</i>'s body, and when Aed pulled one final time her body came apart in every direction.<br /> <br /> Aed feasted, absorbing the last essence from the spirit. She had been near-drained by the fight, and was left with nothing she could use to reform her body. The <i>Magath</i>, former totem of the Whispers, was dead. She would not be coming back.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-01-20T18:24:00-08:00","id":1281853,"title":"","content":"Session 18<br /> <br /> The two packs left the gently discorporating bodies of the slain spirits, and the ruined cadaver of the <i>Magath</i>, behind them as they ventured back out into the tunnels. Only Dorian paused, spitting on the body of his pack's former totem. They followed the current in the dark until they reached the ladder they had used to enter the underground river system. That was when a figured stepped out of the darkness, illuminated by the faintly glowing lightbulbs near the entrance. A voice boomed, accusing them all of trespass. The figure was thin, a man with a scraggly growth of hair around his chin, each bone in his trembling hands standing out as he pointed at them in accusation. He smelled like the river itself, but his words made it clear he was one of the People.<br /> <br /> The Whispers, for their part, seemed more bemused than concerned. This was their territory, after all. But the man insisted, he had dominion over all the underground places of Belfast. Dorian shook his head and just shoved the man out of the way. The push sent him staggering, and the newcomer made no effort to retaliate or prevent the Whispers as they passed him and left the tunnels.<br /> <br /> The Harbingers struck a more conciliatory tone. Aed asked the strange wolf what his name was. At first the man just grunted the word &quot;Wade&quot;. A beat later and all the Harbingers were laughing, looking pointedly at the knee-high water they were all standing in. The stranger swore and added that his first name was Eddy. The Harbingers each introduced themselves, and Aed tried to work out where Wade's allegiances lay. His hackles went up at the suggestion, and he swore he had no connection to either the Forsaken or the Pure. He was a Ghost Wolf, one who'd been driven underground by the predations of Werewolves of all stripes. He claimed the Balelight Howl had killed his family as part of a ploy to force him into a Forsaken pack, and that near every <i>Uratha</i> he'd ever met had tried to beat him, bite him, or drive him into the arms of a new pack. He'd claimed Belfast's underground, all of it, because for the last twenty-seven years there had been no place for him on the surface world at all.<br /> <br /> Aed mentioned that they'd be in touch, and offered the Harbingers' help if Wade ever needed extra hands policing his immense territory. Regan noted that he struck a balance between arrogant and pathetic, unable to meet Aed's eyes without his hands trembling, but proud enough to dismiss the offer for help out of hand. The Harbingers bid him farewell and made their way back to the surface and, under cover of darkness, travelled south to return to their territory.<br /> <br /> Their victory allowed them all to head home for an early night. With We-Who-Eat-In-Darkness destroyed, the Whispers should be able to seek out and take a new totem and begin the long process of restoring balance to their territory. Aed was feeling exhausted, so he dropped Aaron off at the rectory and then headed straight home, making Regan walk the last few blocks to her own house. He headed straight to bed and collapsed face-first into it.<br /> <br /> Regan was at the front door of her house when the lamplight above flared out into a brilliant silver sphere of light, large enough to encompass her and the front of her house. Blinking, she witnessed a Lune of a kind she had never seen before. Brilliant silver armour, pure white wings, and a voice like chiming bells that made her ears ache and bleed, it praised her for her triumph over the twisted <i>Magath</i>. Regan felt the blood running down the left side of her neck begin to hiss and sizzle, branding in her first Purity Renown. Across town, Aed too woke momentarily from his slumber to receive the same gift, as the hooked blades of the visiting Lune bit into his chest and began to extend his existing brands.<br /> <br /> Aed woke unusually early the next morning, thanks to the early night, and decided to make his way to Regan's home. It was a Friday, so Regan was busy getting ready for school and failing badly in dodging Steph's question about the odd silver light that had come in through the windows the night before. She was saved by Aed's knock on the door, though when he and Steph started making eyes at one another she quickly wished her previous predicament would return. She made it clear to Aed that she wanted to get the pack together after school and slipped away while they were making breakfast plans. Aed took Steph out to the best cafe he could find, and they had a nice meal before she had to leave for work.<br /> <br /> Regan's plan was to head up to the Old Man's court, in the hopes that he would be willing to teach her a new Gift. She wanted to get a good present for him, and they ended up at the local music store shopping for some tapes he would like. Staring at the shelves, the two of them came up with a brilliant idea. Aaron was champing at the bit to go after the Scream of Slaughter, and they'd seen how noise affected the <i>Suthilu</i> hybrid during their last fight. Aed started buying all the amplifiers he could get his hands on, and Regan sought out the loudest and angriest music she could find. They then realised that Aed's car didn't physically have the space for everything they'd purchased, so he had to leave Regan minding the gear while he ducked inside to use the store's phone to call Lizzie and get her to bring her band and their van around to transport everything. On the drive back to Aed's house, careful negotiations between him and Lizzie lead to the Harbingers securing (probably) the use of the van when they needed it to move the equipment to go on the attack.<br /> <br /> With evening approaching, they gathered Aaron and slipped into the Shadow through the mirror locus. The Shadow, brighter in the weeks since the New Year's Eve party, seemed quiet and at ease as they headed up the mountain slopes. The mountain's eye stood open, allowing them to pass through the passage of chains and into the Old Man's court. There were fewer Broken around than they recalled, but the Old Man himself seemed healthier and without the fever-like heat he'd exuded in previous encounters. He greeted Regan warmly, and she presented him with a boombox playing some of the music she'd selected. He took the device and hugged it close to his chest, allowing the vibrations to channel straight into his torso. He stood there, eyes closed, until the first song stopped. Afterwards, he listened to Regan's request that he gift her with mastery of the elements. Only a token <i>gathra</i> was required. The tribute was paid, and he had her turn around and remove her shirt. Then, lifting her in one hand, he carved a mangled wound into her back. The new channels left Regan breathless, but it was like she could feel the entire world feeding into her through that wound. The air itself seemed to respond to her as she let the wound knit itself close.<br /> <br /> While they were there, Aaron asked the Old Man if he had sensed any Wounds opening up in their territory. The Old Man was curt with Aaron, clearly still remembering the smell of the Wolf in Golden Chains upon him, and told him that he had no knowledge of any such happenings.<br /> <br /> When they returned to Aed's home, just past dinnertime, the pack agreed to bring down the Scream of Slaughter as fast as they could. Regan sent a message, using her rite to dispatch a minor spirit, off to their new contact, Wade. The pack had the smart idea to ask him if he had noticed any particular congregations of bats underground. Then they pulled out the explosives from the attack on the New Year's party and tried to work out what they could about where the <i>Suthilu</i> hybrid could possibly have got them from. The device was clearly homemade, but it was Regan's keen nose that noted that there was the barest trace of something sweet undercut with something fouler, like flowers gone to rot.<br /> <br /> The meeting with Wade also went smoothly. In return for a dinner with Aed in Andersonstown, the sewer wolf told them that bats had been unusually nesting in the sewers beneath Belfast. They were all focussed in Andersonstown, but at their strongest near a cluster of backstreets over in the eastern part of town owned by the Silent Sons. By this point it was past ten in the evening, but Aaron put in a call to Iule to get permission to cross into the Silent Sons' territory. <br /> <br /> Iule answered the call sounding faintly out of breath and wanting to know what the emergency was, and was a little impatient when Aaron explained the situation to her. She felt her pack would have no problem reciprocating the access the Harbingers had already offered to them for a hunt, and promised to talk to the rest of her pack in the morning. Aaron wanted to keep her on the line to find out what she, as a Hunter in Darkness herself, knew about the Bat Hosts, but Iule fobbed him off saying she might remember something in the morning. Aaron hung up the phone. Only then, seeing the smirk Regan was wearing after overhearing the conversation, did it slowly dawn on him what had been going on down the other end of the line.<br /> <br /> The Harbingers decided to take the night to scout out their prey. They invoked the <i>Siskur-Dah</i>, and then worked with Hope and Regan to bind their totem into the Irraka. Regan would head into Andersonstown on her own, to see if she could locate the Scream's lair. She shifted into <i>Urhan</i> and slipped away into the darkness.<br /> <br /> Calling on all the Gifts at her disposal, Regan moved like a shadow. Unseen by the humans still on the street, she closed in on the blocks Wade had indicated as the best to search. The local pub, the Crossed Staves, was doing a roaring trade into the night. She was trying to find a scent amidst the rubbish out the back when she heard booted feet on the street beyond. Shifting back into <i>Hishu</i>, and invoking her gifts to shift her gender and obfuscate her identity a little, Regan entered the pub. He could see soldiers passing out the front of the building, an army patrol sweeping the streets. His gaze was drawn to the second level of the building though, when the faintest trace of a scent he recognised crossed his nose. Moving up, Regan could see a small man in a dirty green jacket sitting by the pub's window. He was wiping condensation away from the window, and the armpits and neckline of his jacket were soaked with sweat. He had a small clicker in his hand, and appeared to be counting the soldiers as they passed. Most importantly of all, however, Regan could smell that faintly rotten floral scent about him, stronger than what he'd detected on the explosives. Much stronger, in fact, it could only be hours old.<br /> <br /> Rather than confront the man, Regan waited for the soldiers to pass and then slipped out of the pub. He returned to <i>Urhan</i> and sought out the scent of the man in the green jacket, and tried to retrace his steps. It wasn't difficult, not with the senses at Regan's disposal. The smell led to a small backstreet called Orchardville Crescent. Only four houses actually stood on the street, with a butcher's place on the corner. The second house, with white walls and a lavender roof, was the source of the smell. Regan tried to see if he could hear anything coming from inside, but there were no sounds from the front. He circled around the side to get to the back, and listened again. This time he could hear a sound uncomfortably like a blade striking meat, and then heavy footfalls coming towards the backdoor. Regan froze, paralysed by indecision, and was still sitting there when the door opened and a grossly overweight woman came into view.<br /> <br /> There had to be something supernatural about this woman. She was so fat, so incredibly burdened with an excess of flesh, that there was no way her bones should have been able to support her body. Yet she moved with speed and grace, easily maneuvering her titanic form through the door as she smiled down at Regan. Uncomfortably, Regan saw that the woman's pupils had spilled and run like ink, forming a cloudy stain across her irises and whites.<br /> <br /> &quot;Come on in, pet,&quot; the woman said, and returned inside.<br /> <br /> Regan shifted back into <i>Hishu</i>. He took a few careful steps forward, peering in through the door. He could see a large room, a combined kitchen and dining room. There were large mirrors dominating every wall, and small ones right down to the size of hand mirrors propped up in the corners of the room so that there was nowhere to stand without casting a reflection. He could see himself in those mirrors, looking a decade older and female with a nasty scar running from the right side of his lip and down his neck, pulling his features into a frown. The woman he'd followed was speaking beneath her breath, and he could hear that she was crooning to something unseen and saying, &quot;Don't worry, I don't think they want to hurt you. You are not their prey.&quot;<br /> <br /> Regan asked the woman who she was talking to, and she asked the Voices to come forward. Spirits emerged from hiding places around the room, crawling out from behind the mirrors, from the pantry and corners of the kitchen, and from beneath the dining table. Looking at that table more closely, Regan could see that it was covered in a lace table cloth and with an odd veil set up around one end, so that someone could sit behind the veil without being seen by whoever else was seated at the table. A pile of crystals sat just in front of the veil itself.<br /> <br /> The spirits the woman had beckoned forth looked almost human, but thin and stretched and drawn out. The woman called them Voices, and said they were manifestations of despair that the Harbingers themselves had created by shutting down the mine. But then, rather than take responsibility for their creations, they had started to starve the poor dear by fighting back against the community's hopelessness. She'd taken them little ones in before they starved, just as she had done with lesser spirits before them.<br /> <br /> Regan was startled to find out that the woman knew who he was. When he asked, the woman looked at him, smiled, and said that the spirits she had reared often went on to do great things. She liked to keep an eye on those who left her side, just in case, and she was so proud of Hope to see her making something of herself. If Hope and Regan weren't sharing a body right at that instant, Regan would very much have liked to give the Totem a glare.<br /> <br /> Feeling both a bit creeped out and a bit more confident that the woman wasn't a threat, however, Regan explained that he was here hunting the Scream of Slaughter. The woman said she recognised the name, and by her expression and tone it was clear that she was not a fan. Regan dug a little, and was told, &quot;He took a friend from my Tommy. Nasty little brutes, these hosts.&quot; Emboldened, Regan asked for help locating the hybrid. The woman considered him for a long time, and then approached him and placed a heavy arm around his thin shoulders.<br /> <br /> Leading the werewolf out to the backyard, the woman approached a large tree bare of leaves for the winter. She placed her other hand against the trunk, and whispered in Regan's ear. &quot;Now, don't tell the others about this. Your lot don't always take kindly to <i>duguthim</i>.&quot; As she spoke, small black flowers began to bud all over the tree's branches. They grew and grew, petals spreading out and sprouting many smaller petals of their own. The stem lengthened and hardened, and the shape of the flowers flowed. Regan realised then that he was staring at a flock of crows, grown out of the tree, and a sinking feeling took hold in his gut as he realised just how powerful this Claimed must be.<br /> <br /> The crows took flight. Telling Regan that the flock would be their eyes, the woman led Regan back into the house, and closed the door against the cold behind her.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-01-20T18:58:06-08:00","id":1281855,"title":"","content":"Boy that session was long and dense, introducing two new significant NPCs in Wade and the woman on Orchardville Crescent. There were a lot of great roleplaying moments that I really liked. Aed winning Wade's help by offering him a hot meal did a great job of highlighting just how sad the Ghost Wolf's plight must be, while Old Man Mountain and Regan bonding over music was just cute, and the Iule-Aaron phone call made for a good laugh. I feel like we got to really cut loose and have a lot of fun with a pretty broad selection of the NPCs, new and established, this game.<br /> <br /> I'm out of town this weekend sadly, so the rest of Regan's night with his new and unnerving friend will be a fortnight away.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-02-04T20:14:55-08:00","id":1284881,"title":"","content":"Session 19<br /> <br /> In a small home in the dead of winter, a Werewolf named Regan and a Claimed named Adele drank tea and ate biscuits while they waited for a score of hunting crows to find their prey. Regan learned many new things about his new friend. She worked as a fortune teller, a fact which grew more ominous as he came to understand the nature of the creature sitting across from him. Her essence had long since seeped into the mirrors among which she made her home. The visions seen in them, of an older Regan scarred and haunted, were a manifestation of that essence. His eager questioning revealed slowly that Adele's spirit-half had once been a mighty spirit of ill fortune, an oracle whose hands plucked at the darkest and most rotten strands of the future's web of fate. So when she suggested that Regan stay in school, Regan certainly listened... albeit not sure whether the advice was for his benefit or Adele's.<br /> <br /> At last, Adele's eyes began to glaze. Her crows had found the hosts squatting in an old clubhouse not far from where they were sitting. She relayed the information to Regan, who took the opportunity to excuse himself from the rather nerve-wracking confines of the powerful Claimed's home. He slipped into the darkness and took off, shifting into <i>Urhan</i> as he drew as close to the clubhouse as he dared. The low structure was boarded up, the door sealed, and there wasn't a single light on inside. In fact, scanning the street, Regan noted that every street lamp had been smashed, and that bats clung to the poles. None of the creatures appeared to take notice to the <i>Irraka</i> in their midst, but it was clear that Adele was not lying about this being the source of the <i>Suthilu</i>. Slinking as close as he dared, Regan noted a large hole in the roof facing away from the main street, and a heavy tarp bundled to one side that could be used to close off the rooftop exit.<br /> <br /> Regan returned to his packmates to share what he'd learned. They disbanded for the night (with Regan using his gifts to return to being a girl), and returned the next morning to lay their plans. Aaron was meticulous, pouring over every public record he could get about the clubhouse and its surrounding area. They mapped out the sewers, worked out where the <i>Suthilu</i> had most likely breached into the sewers (because, thanks to Wade, they were certain there would be a sewer exit as well), and took the daylight hours to scope out the streets.<br /> <br /> Sunday morning was when they decided to make their play. It would be daylight outside, but there would be fewer people in the streets while mass was taking place. They loaded the van borrowed from Lizzie's band with the amplifiers and boomboxes Aed had purchased. When they reached the clubhouse, they made sure the surrounding area was clear before Aed hoisted the massive, weighty amps onto his shoulders and launched himself into the air. He landed on the roof with a muffled <i>thump</i>, and placed the equipment there. Regan followed in his wake, carrying the rest of the equipment on her back, but without Aed's unnatural strength she had to scale the side of the building by hand. All seemed quiet inside, and she reached the roof and took up her perch beside the tarp. Aed had bought her, and Aaron, digital watches to synchronise so that everyone's part in the plan could be coordinated to perfection, so she settled down to wait.<br /> <br /> The two older men then descended into the sewers. They had to retreat a few streets over to find the first manhole cover, but they slipped underground without being seen. They didn't have to retrace their steps far before they started to encounter the sleeping bats. Step by step they crept until they were directly beneath the clubhouse, and sure enough they found a crude hole torn through the basement to allow the <i>Suthilu</i> to move underground unseen during the day. And there they settled in to wait, letting the last few minutes tick past. Bats carpeted the floor and ceiling around them, the air reeked of guano with a sharper, rotten stench coming from above, but they held their position until the appointed time.<br /> <br /> Crooned, garbled voices drifted down to them while they waited. The words were in the first tongue, but Aaron could have almost believed it was a hymn. The voices themselves were coarse and slurred the words badly at times, as though speaking with mouths ill-equipped for language. The service, or whatever it was, was just beginning to wind down when three digital watches began to beep.<br /> <br /> Music roared to life, flooding down the hole in the roof and echoing throughout the entire building as Regan kicked every part of the assembled equipment into overdrive. The bats in the sewers beneath stirred and woke, squealing in confusion. The confusion turned to fear a second later as Aaron and Aed lit up the flares they'd carried down with them. They pushed forward, not wanting to get distracted by the <i>Suthilu</i>'s attendants, leaving a burning flare behind them to cover their backs. They stepped up into the basement and froze when they spotted three crude, homemade bombs sitting alongside duffel bags not unlike those the Scream of Slaughter had used during its last attack. Aaron opened his mouth, ready to suggest they leave the flare behind rather than carry the sparking stick past the bombs, but Aed pushed forward regardless. By pure luck, and certainly no skill, Aed kept any sparks from touching the bombs.<br /> <br /> (OOC: Rolled a chance die, got a 10. We all laughed. Everyone tried not to think about how close they'd just come to blowing themselves up.)<br /> <br /> And then they were through. The main room of the building was completely dark, save for the light of their flares. They could make out five larger figures in the room, turning at their approach. Three were immense <i>Suthilu</i> hybrids and one, the smallest, they recognised by its colouration and scars as the Scream of Slaughter. A larger hybrid had it pinned to the wall and was snarling in its face. Alongside them were two men, nails and lips torn and bitten to shreds, who were standing over a pile of bite-ridden bodies. And clinging to those bodies, fur matted with blood and bloated from its gorging, was a single <i>Xikaba</i> host. The familiar stench of the Herald reached Aed's nose.<br /> <br /> The Scream reacted first, and acted true to its name. A high-pitched cry split the air, making Aaron's head pound and his ears bleed. Deafened, he was voiced to shift into <i>Garou</i> immediately to keep on his feet. He charged straight for one large hybrid still standing on its own, and the two great beasts slammed into one another with a heavy thud. Claws and fangs ripped and tore into each other's flesh as they each sought to overwhelm the other.<br /> <br /> The Herald took the opportunity to take wing. The light from the Harbingers' flares only lit about half the room, and the <i>Suthilu</i> took its opportunity to escape. Its flight was erratic, weighed down by its feast and and disorientated by the cacophony of noise filtering down from the roof, but when it went beyond the reach of the flare's light it vanished into the darkness. The larger hybrid holding Scream seemed to react to the presence of <i>Garou</i> wolves and acted on instinct, fleeing in the wake of the Herald in the same manner.<br /> <br /> The two human hosts tried to break past Aed, one rushing him and trying to barge him out of the way. The <i>Cahalith</i> stood his ground, shifting into <i>Urshul</i> and seizing the running man's thigh in his jaws. His screams built to fever pitch as Aed tore him open, but the bat within the ribcage struggled free and fled. Aed reared back and howled, letting loose a devastating war cry that indicated Regan's turn to at last enter the fight. The second man vaulted over the bloody remains of his fellow host and raced into the darkness, and Aed was poised to follow.<br /> <br /> The Scream made its move then. Its larger fellow hybrid was falling back, torn and bloodied by the clash with the <i>Garou</i> form Aaron. As it vanished into the shadows, the Scream took its place. The creature fought like a beast possessed, hooking its claws into Aaron's chest and peeling him open. As snarls turned to whimpers, Scream's muzzle plunged deep into the open wound and took merciless bites from Aaron's organs. His heart was in reach, the Scream's jaws gnashing closer and closer, when Regan struck.<br /> <br /> Quite how the young <i>Irraka</i> made it down from the rooftop was unclear, the tarp covering the hole above their heads was still in place, but the shadows yielded behind the Scream and suddenly an <i>Urshul</i> wolf had the hybrid by the throat. Aed heard the cries and yelps of his packmates and abandoned the pursuit of the fleeing hosts to focus on the one they had come to hunt. He closed the distance in a moment, even as Aaron rejoined the fight. His wounds were so terrible even the regeneration of <i>Urfarah</i> himself struggled to close the wounds, but he took one of Scream's limbs in his mouth as his third packmate joined the fight. The three werewolves pulled, and the Scream of Slaughter came apart in their jaws. As its body broke, bats spilled out from the open wounds and began to fill the air, but the single <i>Suthilu</i> shard among them was clear to each of the Harbingers. They ripped it apart as a pack, jaws filled with the crunching of bat bones and the taste of bloodied fur.<br /> <br /> Unseen, unheard, a watcher in the darkness sent forth its biting silver teeth. Trapping their prey, exploiting its weaknesses, it was as clear a triumph of Cunning as it had ever seen. Though the fight had cost them dearly, they would feel the bite of new Renown in their flesh even after their wounds had healed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-02-05T03:57:27-08:00","id":1284914,"title":"","content":"Nice. I like how you are also narrating the Renown gain.<br /> <br /> So, was the enclosure busy, or near critical areas? Why didn't they just light and Chuck the bombs toward the Suthilu to soften them up? Explosions are great for taking out Swarms and why look a gift bomb in the mouth?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-02-05T05:58:11-08:00","id":1284929,"title":"","content":"The place was surrounded by townhouses and there were concerns the fire would spread.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4968-mrbreaksit","name":"MrBreaksIt","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"130"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-02-05T15:16:25-08:00","id":1285064,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1284914#post1284914\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nice. I like how you are also narrating the Renown gain.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, we've gone back and forth a little on how to handle Renown in the game. We actually ended up removing the experience cost for it, because early on the players were both gaining Renown at a relatively brisk trot and not gaining beats very quickly (it always seems to take my groups about 5-10 sessions to get into the groove of beat generation). That left them in so much experience debt that they didn't really feel like they had control of their characters' progression. I think Aed, who had gained a point of Glory for taking on the Herald in the underground Shadow on his own, reached a point where he was like four Experience points in debt and hadn't actually had an opportunity to spend an Experience himself all campaign.<br /> <br /> Now I think we're reaching the point where generating more Renown is becoming relatively tricky, so this side of things might start slowing down a little. But I've enjoyed coming up with the Lunes granting each Renown source and I'm hopeful they'll be able to develop a little further down the track. We'll see.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1284914#post1284914\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, was the enclosure busy, or near critical areas? Why didn't they just light and Chuck the bombs toward the Suthilu to soften them up? Explosions are great for taking out Swarms and why look a gift bomb in the mouth?</div> </div> </div> </div>This really didn't factor into the players' decisions (they originally considered just setting fire to the place during the day and waiting in the sewers for the hosts to come to them, which is when they inquired as to how close the neighbouring buildings were and then decided against it), but it's probably a good thing they didn't go for it. The music they were playing might lead to some noise complaints, but a few constables showing up looking for partying kids are going to be much easier to evade than the kind of response they'd have had from setting off bombs. They're within spitting distance of the Silver City military fort, after all...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-02-17T21:05:16-08:00","id":1289083,"title":"","content":"Well, the fortnightly pace is continuing for 2019, and since I'm out of town <i>again</i> this coming weekend it'll continue another fortnight at least. Hopefully we can get back to weekly games shortly afterwards. Anyway, after the action packed adventures of last time, we settled in for a game a bit more focussed on <i>uhhhh how do we deal with a packmate sitting on 10 Aggravated Damage?</i><br /> <br /> Session 20<br /> <br /> The Scream of Slaughter's breath was still steaming from its broken body when the Harbingers felt the approach of something new. The tarp covering the hole in the roof above their heads ignited, and light fell streaming into the chamber. Essence boiled up in its wake, a blazing presence that caused the wood to smolder, paint to bubble, and flesh to boil. Then, as if noticing the wolves staring at it, the raw heat and flame seemed to turn inwards and boiled away into the silhouette of a man. As the last essence vanished, leaving just a faintly golden glow in the air, an angel stood before the wolves. He had golden hair tumbling to his shoulders, skin smooth and unbroken across perfect muscles. He was bare-chested, though he wore greaves of mirrored steel on his forearms. Bronze wings splayed out behind him, pinions of metal flexing and turning with the rattle of gears as they shifted and folded behind him. He cast a gaze over the Harbingers and, as Aaron relinquished the war form and averted his gaze, the angel spoke.<br /> <br /> &quot;Wolves,&quot; he said. &quot;Of God, I see.&quot;<br /> <br /> He introduced himself as Michael, and said he had heard their howls during the fight. He was a hunter, here to slay the festering beasts of shadow, but it was clear that the Harbingers had come across his prey first. Aed, staring at him, asked if he was a Hellion, but Michael's response was a little ambiguous. His exact words, &quot;I follow in the Sun's wake,&quot; could have easily been interpreted as the <i>Son's</i> wake and at Aaron's insistence the other wolves held their tongues. They told Michael that the <i>Suthilu</i> had fled into the Shadow, and he departed as swiftly as he had come to continue his hunt.<br /> <br /> The pack left Regan behind, lurking in the shadows as only an Irraka could, and Aed escorted the grievously wounded Aaron to safety. Though his clothes had not felt the bite of the Scream's claws, now that he had reverted to the <i>Hishu</i> they were soaked through with gore. When the clothes were peeled back to bandage the wounds, Aed could see the pulse and throb of Aaron's heart and lungs only barely obscured by what was left of his chest. The two of them drove back to Aed's home, and then Aed returned for Regan.<br /> <br /> Regan's job was to keep an eye on the explosives to make sure that the <i>Suthilu</i> did not return for them. The response from human authorities came only minutes after the others left, however. British soldiers entered the cellar, rather than the police Regan had been expecting, and then fell back again when they found the bombs. Regan took that opportunity to slink away through the sewers, evading the military patrols with almost contemptuous ease.<br /> <br /> Aaron asked for the pack's help in returning to his Glade. Aed thought it was a good idea, he hoped that Silkenspun, the spirit of healing they'd once asked for help locating Cairnbreath, would be able to provide some assistance. They found the Shadow filled with patrols of the Court of Bloody Communion, beast-soldiers not unlike Scarred-Beast-Burning swarming through the streets of Andersonstown, but the Werewolves went unchallenged as they made their way to the Glade. They found the edges of the garden tattered, overrun by weed-like spirits with the faces of leering men, but the heart of the Glade was still ordered and carefully maintained. Aaron settled himself underneath one of the larger trees, and Aed called over Silkenspun. The healing spirit was lurking in the Glade, tending to the garden as best it could, and it agreed to do what it could to speed up Aaron's recovery if the rest of the pack did their best to seek out another and help their healing along.<br /> <br /> Regan and Aed departed. The day was still relatively young, since they'd mounted their assault on the <i>Suthilu</i> so early in the morning, so they had time to contact both Eaven and Wade to see if either had any pressing need of healers. Eaven's meeting went down first, and they found to their concern that she had brought her pack's Torture-Claimed, Num-Num, along. The fleshy, oozing creature was visibly excited to see Aed, virtually dancing on the spot, and Eaven explained that he had written Aed a letter asking for a favour. With some trepidation, Aed read the small piece of fluid-stained paper (&quot;It's probably coffee, right?&quot;), trying not to be too disconcerted by the large, blocky letters and child-like spelling of the Claimed. Num-Num claimed that the one responsible for &quot;making&quot; him was at the quarry, and asked for permission to repay the gift. Aed told Num-Num he would look into the matter and get back to him. For her part, Eaven didn't seem particularly invested in whether her pet Claimed received permission or not, and she was happy to answer the Harbingers' questions. There were no dire injuries among the Cleareyes in need of healing, but she did mention that if they wanted the injured they could look out west towards the territory of the Fulcrum. A pack of Wolf-Blooded, headed by twin Predator Kings named Bridget and Cole, they were known for their gristly hunts and for not being entirely thorough. Injured would abound wherever those two went.<br /> <br /> Although the two Blood Talons were tempted by the idea of squaring off against a pair of Predator Kings, they decided it would probably be a poor idea to dive into another conflict so soon. They met with Wade in the evening and Aed offered to follow through with the dinner plans. They took Wade back to Aed's home so that he could shower first, and then the three of them headed out onto the streets to walk to a nearby restaurant. They were en route when Regan spotted a car with tinted windows pull onto the street, driving slower than it should have. The window wound down, and she was shouting at the half-dozen humans also out and about to get down on the ground. Calling on her gifts, Regan redirected the first spray of bullets from the drive-by into the lamppost above their heads, plunging the area into darkness. She struggled to summon the magic again on the second salvo, but by that point Aed had reached the closest humans and physically put his body between them and the car. He grunted as bullets struck him from behind, knocking the breath from his lungs and almost sending him falling onto the humans he was crouched on top of, and then the car was past them and speeding away.<br /> <br /> The wolves didn't stick around to listen to the stammered thanks from those they'd saved. Wade accepted readily that the drive-by had ruined the evening, and that they'd get back together for another attempt in a few nights. Once they were safely back at Aed's home, Aed had Regan dig out the bullets with a knife and then closed the wounds with a burst of essence. They were sitting there, wondering what to do next, when they wondered if what had just transpired counted for Silkenspun's requirement that they find someone to heal. They made their way back into the Shadow and returned to the Glade, and found Silkenspun happily humming and weaving her diaphanous form around Aaron's chest. She could speed up the healing, but it would still be a full month before Aaron's final wounds would close completely.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-03T19:25:27-08:00","id":1293630,"title":"","content":"Session 21<br /> <br /> The Harbingers wound down their hunts and shifted back to simple patrols of their territory, waiting on Father Aaron's wounds before they dared push more assertively against any of their circling enemies. Their hope was that any enemies circling out there in the dark would be similarly preoccupied with regrouping. The <i>Suthilu</i> were scattered, their borders with both Pure and Forsaken neighbours alike appeared relatively secure, so only the Silver City presence at the Black Mountain Quarry was of pressing concern.<br /> <br /> Over the course of the following week, Aed and Regan managed to reorganise a night out with Wade. They swapped stories with the Ghost Wolf, with Aed sharing many of his exploits in order to open Wade up enough to get him talking. He told them how he'd lived so many years without setting foot above ground, spending as much time in the Shadow as he did in the flesh. They learned that only two packs in the city, the Symphony Cascade and the Shorerunners, had ever shown any real acknowledgement or respect towards his claim on subterranean Belfast. And he mentioned to them how, over the years, he'd often heard mournful howls echoing up from far beneath the earth. He'd always felt some level of kinship with the lonely cries, though he'd never seen their owner. The earth had been silent these last few weeks though. Aed and Regan tried to avoid looking at one another as Wade spoke. The meal passed without incident, a moment of warmth and the promise of a new friendship after the chaos and violence the pack had been battling through since mid-December.<br /> <br /> The pack met up with Iule a few days later to discuss the Silver City situation. She mentioned that her Irraka packmates had tried to breach the perimeter to get a better idea of what the soldiers were doing but somehow, impossibly, they'd failed. Though members of her pack were closer to walking shadows than beings of flesh, augmented by a totem with an affinity for stealth, they'd found themselves being shadowed by unseen creatures in the undergrowth and had glimpsed human patrols moving in coordination to block them off and trap them. They'd barely made it out without being forced into a fight. The experience had shaken the Silent Sons from the sound of it, they weren't used to being detected so easily.<br /> <br /> Thinking over the problem, the Harbingers invited Iule to come with them into the <i>Hisil</i> to see what the quarry was like Shadow-side. They found the quarry's reflection to be a churned, shifting morass of broken earth, littered with the shattered corpses of slain machine-spirits. Large chunks of the landscape, particularly the jagged spires erupting from the earth across the quarry, appeared to be somnolent Broken that they recognised from the Old Man's court. They appeared in better health and at ease enough to rest, giving the Harbingers some comfort, though the air in the quarry was hot and moist in a way that made them think unpleasantly of an open wound.<br /> <br /> After some investigation, the pack struck upon a trail. Human boots had trod the ground, though the trail was obscured in places by a curious blurring that the wolves eventually worked out must caused by a rope or tether tied around the humans' wastes. They appeared to be on the trail of a group of four, though the humans no longer appeared to be in the Shadow. They had emerged from, and then disappeared back into, a fissure in the mountainside. The Harbingers and Iule peered into the darkness of the fissure but saw no sign of where it might lead, and then tried to work out how to proceed. They discussed various options, but eventually agreed that the Silent Sons would continue their watch on the quarry Flesh-side and the Harbingers would begin a watch on the Shadow quarry for more human incursions. Aaron laid down a Boundary Ward upon the quarry, set to alert him if any humans set foot there, and they withdrew back to the Flesh.<br /> <br /> Aaron woke at 2am that night as his Boundary Ward was breached. He could sense humans emerging from the fissure. He called Aed to tell him what was happening, but as usual Aed slept right through the phone. Frustrated, Aaron realised he couldn't really do anything more than note down the time and go back to sleep.<br /> <br /> The next morning began with Regan finding that Arthur Cosgrove, the police constable she'd turned Wolfblooded, was waiting for her just out of sight of her home on her way to school. They talked as she headed for the school, with Arthur telling her that he'd managed to get some semblance of control over his life again but that he was still hearing a voice urging him to violence. He was worried that he might soon begin to lose control of his actions. Regan confessed that this was all rather outside of her area of expertise, and told him to follow up with Father Aaron again.<br /> <br /> Aaron, when Arthur turned up on his doorstop a few hours later, listened to the symptoms and finally pieced together what was happening. Arthur had been made Receptive to allow Hope to easily move him out of the way, and it was likely that this act had opened him up to other external influences after Hope had vacated the premises. Although Arthur was nearly on the verge of walking out again over this talk of &quot;spirits&quot;, he ultimately decided that he'd allow Aaron to perform an exorcism. He refused to give up his gun beforehand though, and as Aaron began to intone the carefully selected phrases to eject the spirit fettered to Arthur's soul he could see Arthur's right hand creeping towards the holster. His left hand seized his right before the fingers could make it, however, and forced it away. Moments later, Aaron felt the will opposing his own crumble. A small, wretched thing crawled out of Arthur's chest, its incorporeal body leaving its host's body untouched by the passage, and flopped to the ground.<br /> <br /> To all extents and purposes, the small spirit looked like a fledgling bird. It had a few tattered black feathers, and it was soaked head to toe in gore. Aaron didn't recognise it as a kind of spirit he'd encountered before. It seemed to struggle to do so much as raise its head, its long thin neck too weak to support its own weight, but it croaked out a rhyming ditty demanding to know why it had been called from its play. Aaron wheedled out a little bit of information from the spirit, finding that its earliest memories after coming into being were finding the delicious, appetizing flesh of Arthur nearby and crawling inside. And from there, the rest of the story fell into place.<br /> <br /> Aaron remembered that they'd first encountered Arthur when he was standing guard over the crime scene where the <i>Xikaba</i> wearing Ciara's flesh had murdered her father. When Regan inflicted Lunacy on Arthur to help them get access to the crime scene, they had left behind them the perfect host for the newborn spirit of murder born at the scene. Whether the essence-ridden flesh of the Wolfblooded had been enough to call the Murder Crow across the Gauntlet on its own, or if it had found passage some other way, Aaron wasn't sure, but it sounded like the Murder Crow had fettered itself to Arthur within minutes of their departure from the crime scene.<br /> <br /> With their Bone Shadow still off somewhere else in the country, Aaron realised he was at a bit of an impasse. He had no way of preventing any other spirits from inhabiting Arthur, who was still as open and receptive to possession and spiritual interference as the day Regan struck him down with Lunacy, and he had no way to bottle or bind the Murder Crow in its insubstantial form. In about 24 hours it would regain the ability to inhabit Arthur's flesh, assuming no stronger spirit took that spot first, and there didn't appear to be anything to hand that he could do to stop it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-04T03:54:18-08:00","id":1293691,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In about 24 hours it would regain the ability to inhabit Arthur's flesh, assuming no stronger spirit took that spot first, and there didn't appear to be anything to hand that he could do to stop it. </div> </div> </div>What about find a non-murderous Spirit, at least? Maybe one of the Healing ones from the Grove? Sure, they don't want Arthur to be Claimed regardless, but having an urge to heal is better than an urge to murder, right?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-04T15:17:36-08:00","id":1293898,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1293691#post1293691\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> What about find a non-murderous Spirit, at least? Maybe one of the Healing ones from the Grove? Sure, they don't want Arthur to be Claimed regardless, but having an urge to heal is better than an urge to murder, right?</div> </div> </div> </div>It's basically the trolley dilemma. Sure, you can get them possessed by a nicer spirit, but you're still aiding and abetting the possession of a human.<br /> <br /> (Ok, so they're already morally culpable because Regan <i>made Arthur Receptive to begin with</i>, and from memory the Condition only resolves if he experiences great danger as a consequence of his connection to the <i>Hisil</i>, but still sometimes you have to decide when to stop digging your hole.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-04T16:12:20-08:00","id":1293913,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1293898#post1293898\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's basically the trolley dilemma. Sure, you can get them possessed by a nicer spirit, but you're still aiding and abetting the possession of a human.<br /> <br /> (Ok, so they're already morally culpable because Regan <i>made Arthur Receptive to begin with</i>, and from memory the Condition only resolves if he experiences great danger as a consequence of his connection to the <i>Hisil</i>, but <b>still sometimes you have to decide when to stop digging your hole.</b>)</div> </div> </div> </div>Wait, we are talking about your PCs here, right? :-)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-06T05:37:33-08:00","id":1294372,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1293913#post1293913\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Wait, we are talking about your PCs here, right? :-)</div> </div> </div> </div>...yeah, I guess we'll see the repercussions of a Happiness Claimed run amok in about three sessions' time, hey? Probably best to ignore the crowd of grinning drones chanting &quot;Join us, be happy&quot;, that's someone else's problem by now surely...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-11T22:30:03-07:00","id":1296042,"title":"","content":"And just in the nick of time... Orla returns.<br /> <br /> Session 22<br /> <br /> Aed heard the hammering on his door as he was speaking with Aaron, listening to the priest explain the issue with being unable to contain the Murder Crow. The knocking had ceased by the time Aed had hung up and made his way to the front door. There, dressed in filth-encrusted clothes that might have once been a band tour shirt, was Orla. She was sitting slumped and virtually unconscious against the door, and nearly fell inside when he opened it. With Lizzie out of the house and Orla clearly in need of someone keeping an eye on her, Aed loaded her into the back of the van (pointedly <i>not</i> putting her in his car) and drove her to the rectory.<br /> <br /> They found that the Murder Crow had slipped away while Aaron had been on the phone, not wanting to stick around for reinforcements. Aed and Aaron discussed options with Arthur as Orla slowly returned to consciousness. They established that there wasn't really any way for the Wolf-Blooded to avoid possession in the future, but told him to get in contact as fast as possible if he experienced any missing time or heard voices like the Murder Crow's. Shaken, but without any other choice, Arthur made his exit.<br /> <br /> Regan had used her Message Rite to arrange a meeting with Old Man Mountain for that evening. When she was let out of school she joined the rest of the pack in Aed's home, and they crossed through to the Shadow together. The slim crescent face of Luna shone down on them as they made their way towards the mountain. On the slopes they were met by a grand procession of earth and nature spirits, escorting the Old Man down the slopes with some ceremony. The procession wound down the mountain face, and the wolves followed. They found a kind of rough-hewn shrine at the base, and the Old Man beckoned to follow him inside. The other spirits waited as they entered the shrine, which was either larger on the inside or else continued through into the mountain. The central room of the shrine was a large pit in the earth, surrounded by the guttering remains of smoldering fire pits. There, the Old Man gathered the Harbingers to his side. He told them that he had noticed the incursion of the mortals, and annointed Orla and Aed with his blessing. The earth seemed to wind its way around their limbs, settle into their flesh, and armour them against harm. The blessing would last until they left the mountain slopes.<br /> <br /> As the wolves made to leave, the Old Man warned them that one of his Broken had severed all ties with the court. The Broken, a spirit named Braghdar, had vanished from the <i>Hisil</i>. Recalling the strange gap in the Gauntlet they had found in the quarry, the Harbingers asked the Old Man if he knew about it. He revealed that he had once been struck with a terrible weapon, one capable of tearing the barrier between worlds, at the height of a conflict many centuries ago. The injury had never healed, and he had sought to scab over the hole with layers of earth. The humans had disturbed the site with their quarry, revealing the tear.<br /> <br /> The pack circled around the mountain to reach the quarry. There, they set about trying to work out what the humans had done when they crossed Aaron's wards the night before. The pack located several large bores, about 20cm across, in the stone near the entrance, but it was Aaron who saw with almost preternatural awareness and gift of recall that several of the somnolent Broken from their last visit were no longer at their resting place. Aaron attempted to wake one of the remaining sleeping spirits, but it proved exceptionally resilient to his efforts. <br /> <br /> They heard the chattering of teeth and turned to see a creature crawling out of one of the bored tunnels in the earth. It looked like a small, round rock with hands and feet, but its body (or head) split open into a pair of jaws. It flung itself at Aed, but no matter how hard it tried it couldn't punch through the armour bestowed by the Old Man's blessing. Finally, more bemused than anything, Aed sent it running with a low snarl. It released his leg and raced away, vanishing among the broken earth of the quarry.<br /> <br /> After retreating to a safe distance, Aed sent Regan and Aaron back to the mortal world. It was getting late, so Regan had to sneak back through a window to avoid alarming Steph. Aaron, on the other hand, was given an important task. He had to return to Aed's home and gather several old costumes, somewhere between a luchadore outfit and a superhero kit. Orla and Aed intended to remain in the Shadow, on watch, until the humans returned. Aaron was still gathering the kit together when he felt his wards trigger. He snatched up the capes and masks and rushed back to where he had left his packmates. Aed and Orla had been watching the distant torchlight from where they were camped beyond the lip of the quarry. They dressed quickly and marched up to the rise.<br /> <br /> When they crested the rise, the two <i>Uratha</i> could see four human men dressed in outfits not too dissimilar from an astronaut's gear. Mirrored visors hid their faces, and they were clearly breathing through oxygen tanks. They were tethered to cords reaching out behind them, cords that disappeared through the fissure in the rock and back, presumably, to the Flesh. Each man carried an assault rifle and was weighed down with a dizzying array of other equipment. And there, ranging ahead of them like a tracking hound, was a particularly large Broken.<br /> <br /> Confident that his identity was concealed by the mask and cape, Aed called out a challenge and began to stride down to meet the intruders.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-18T21:46:34-07:00","id":1297884,"title":"","content":"Session 23<br /> <br /> It's impossible to know, really, what the soldiers in the quarry were thinking when they saw Orla and Aed approach dressed halfway between luchadors and superheroes. The men's expressions were hidden behind their mirrored visors, and the lead soldier began to fumble for something at his waist as the Harbingers drew closer. It was Braghdar who intercepted them, the spirit visibly puzzled and incapable of recognising the werewolves in their costumes. Aed confidently demanded that the spirit cease cooperating with the soldiers and that the humans return to the world of the flesh. Braghdar, his words barely audible over the grinding, gnashing noise of his mouth moving, claimed that he owed these men a debt for clearing the quarry of the miners. And then the soldier struggling with some device around his waist succeeded in switching it on, and a tinny, artificial voice filled with static boomed out and demanded Aed and Orla raise their hands above their heads and reveal who they were.<br /> <br /> Well, Aed might be concealing his identity, but he wasn't one to hide his nature. He claimed to be a protector of the balance between worlds, a balance they were disrupting by being here. At this Braghdar showed recognition, and growled out the word, &quot;<i>Uratha</i>&quot;. It was a term the soldiers seemed to recognise. Three of the four soldiers released their rifles and went for their sidearms, raising smaller pistols trained on Aed's chest. The fourth soldier seemed to waver, his rifle dipping. There was a moment's lull in the conversation as the lead soldier and this fourth man under his command seemed to converse, and Aed demanded to know what the men were doing here. He was still standing at ease, hands nowhere near his head, and grips were tightening around the pistols aimed at his body. When the conversation between the two soldiers ceased, the original speaker claimed to be the head of an exploratory team into the &quot;parasite world&quot;. He told Aed and Orla again to raise their hands, and demanded that they move towards the fissure in the stone. They were under arrest, and would be taken back to Earth for containment.<br /> <br /> Aed flared his Renown, silver shining forth across his skin as a brilliant challenge to those who would doubt his glory and might. He sought to cow the soldiers, but they were clearly on edge and unsettled by the wolves' refusal to follow their orders. He heard a howl behind him as Orla read the situation and shifted up into Garou, a fraction of a second before the first gunshots rang out. Orla's howl turned into a frenzied snarl as cursed, biting silver tore into her flesh. The Old Man's blessing shielded her from the worst of the damage, but the hissing of boiling blood and the stench of necrotic flesh filled the air as the bullet still carved a bloody furrow across her ribs. Her mind teetered on the edge of the Death Rage as she hurled herself at the leader of the wolf pack, but he was startlingly prepared for her attack. He dodged every blow with consummate ease, never quite where her talons sought to land, ducking and weaving and letting her drive him back until she was flanked by his fellow soldiers. Bullets shattered harmlessly across her chest as the fourth soldier opened fire with his assault weapon, the bullets that punched through the stone forming across her skin simply pushed out moment's later.<br /> <br /> Behind her, Braghdar had launched himself at Aed. The large Broken seemed intent on incapacitating the Cahalith before he, too, could take the war form, but Aed was prepared for the attack. He seized the Broken's forearm and forced its away from his chest, and then unleashed a roar from the depths of his chest. Enforced by the might of his Renown, the echoes of the command cracked the stone of the quarry and sent the Harbingers' attackers staggering away. &quot;Run,&quot; he told them. &quot;Run back to your world.&quot; And the humans obeyed, Braghdar close on their heels. Orla kept herself under control, claws twitching and teeth gnashing, until the last soldier had vanished through the fissure in the stone. Then, calling on the balance in her Half-Moon soul, she relinquished the brimming rage and shifted back to the <i>Hishu</i>.<br /> <br /> The two wolves retreated back to where they had left Aaron, and relayed the story. Aaron took it as an opportunity to lay into Orla for starting the fight and for breaking the Oaths in revealing herself to humans, though Aed came to her defence. She hadn't started the fight, just reacted faster than he had when it became clear the humans were going to attack. And besides, the humans were working with a spirit and understood the term &quot;Uratha&quot;. She probably hadn't done any harm through her actions.<br /> <br /> The pack retreated back to Aed's home and the Locus there, shifting back across the Gauntlet. Aed got in contact with Regan and managed to persuade her, however reluctantly, to try and summon her grandfather Reynard so that they could ask some questions about ancient weapons capable of sundering the Gauntlet. Aaron, for his part, waited his turn to contact Iule and let her know what had happened. He tried to follow up with Arthur to see if the Wolfblooded constable had experienced any more issues with the Murder Crow or other spirits, but Arthur wasn't at home. Or, well, he didn't pick up the phone if he was.<br /> <br /> Regan's message had asked Reynard to come that evening, so the pack holed up at Aed's home to watch movies and drink. Reynard didn't show up, and when the night devolved into a drinking contest between Aed and Lizzie the others took their chance to slip away. When Aed woke the next morning, however, he woke to Reynard knocking on his door. The old man apologised for not being able to make it the previous evening, and Aed made a few quick calls to the rest of the pack to summon them. The two men chatted at the kitchen table while they waited, and Aed learned that Reynard was very proud of the recent academic achievements of a granddaughter named Maddy. Regan's half-sister, he assumed.<br /> <br /> When the entire pack had arrived and seated at the table, Aed explained what they had learned about the tear in the Gauntlet and the activities of these humans. He asked Reynard what he knew about such weapons. Reynard was happy enough to help, explaining that legends of such weapons abounded. They had most likely been made in ancient times, not long after the fall of Pangaea. The packs that would later become the Pure, those that longed for the lost world, had sought out rites and magics that could breach the new divide between worlds and let them reclaim what had been lost. The weapon they'd mentioned was surely a Fetish from that time. He didn't know where it might lie today for certain, but his research on the subject indicated that deep within the Spirit Wilds around Shankill there lay a vault of some kind. The spirits refused to speak of what it contained, but his efforts to track the old weapons had never turned up anything more promising than the vault. The Balelight Howl kept a watch in the area, and he was certain there was some kind of cache of ancient artefacts contained inside.<br /> <br /> While Aaron was interested in learning all he could about the weapon (his constant interruptions earning a short demand from Reynard that he be allowed to tell his story first), Aed was less concerned. As he pointed out, the weapon was only useful if they wanted to make more holes in the Gauntlet, and that was not their intention. He asked Reynard how to go about closing a breach in the Gauntlet like the one he described. Reynard hesitated, and then mentioned the <i>Azlu</i>. Most of the pack were unfamiliar with the Spider Hosts, so Reynard regaled them with the story of the Spinner Hag and their ability to thicken the Gauntlet. It would be a dangerous gambit, compelling an Azlu to repair the Gauntlet, but they were the only creatures Reynard could think of that might have the ability to repair the breach. He offered the help of the <i>Kallakalat</i>, claiming that Joseph was an expert in the Hosts and would be able to locate one and drive it in their direction if need be, but the offer was shot down. Aaron seemed a bit put out after Reynard snapped at him, and told him that the Harbingers didn't need the help of a Hunter in Darkness who had allowed the <i>Suthilu</i> to run rampant for so long. Regan, who had been quiet throughout most of her grandfather's visit, was delighted to see her packmates turning on him. When Aaron finished by claiming that they didn't need help from anyone, Reynard offered a polite smile, said his goodbyes, and left. As Aed was showing him out, the Wolf-blooded turned to him and asked that if they did uncover the vault he had mentioned, could they please let him know in return for his help? He was certain there were great things to discover there. Aed gave him his word, and the loremaster of the <i>Kallakalat</i> headed off into the bright Sunday morning.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-19T02:10:09-07:00","id":1297908,"title":"","content":"At the risk of misinterpreting the text again: Was Braghdar outright lied to about the miners? Did the players notice?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>~<span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u266b</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7593-teatime","name":"Teatime","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7593_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"750"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-19T05:24:08-07:00","id":1297927,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Teatime</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1297908#post1297908\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">At the risk of misinterpreting the text again: Was Braghdar outright lied to about the miners? Did the players notice?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well strictly speaking the army <i>did</i> shut down the mine. Sure, their attention was called to the mine in the first place by the pack's efforts in getting the strange disturbances mentioned in the newspaper, but even the army doesn't know that the pack was responsible for that <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-04T19:02:43-07:00","id":1302712,"title":"","content":"Oops, kept forgetting to actually do this write-up. If this looks a little short, it's because about 60% of the session gets glossed over in the first two paragraphs.<br /> <br /> Session 24<br /> <br /> The overwhelming bulk of this session consisted of the pack evaluating and discarding different approaches to sealing the hole in the Gauntlet and preventing any further incursions by Silver City. The options ranged from Aaron conceding that he saw forcing an <i>Azlu</i> to web the area as the best option, all the way to Regan suggesting that they all just dive through the hole into the middle of the quarry and let the Death Rage take them and just see if the problem was sorted when they regained their senses. Ultimately, the pack decided to aim to just cover the hole again with tons of earth and rubble. Regan suggested using a concrete mixer, but Aed wanted to reach out to the Cleareyes to see if they could help. Their Totem, from the various hints the pack had gathered in previous interactions, had something to do with the earth. They could gather more information on their Pure neighbours and resolve the issue at the same time.<br /> <br /> Through all this, Braghdar remained an unanswered, and rather thorny, problem for the pack. The pack could bury the hole in enough earth that it would take Silver City months to excavate it, but it wouldn't be much of an obstacle if the hunters could call on earth spirits to open the way for them.<br /> <br /> The Harbingers met with Eaven and Awne at the grove on the edge of their territories. Regan and Aed explained the problem, outlining that they had an issue with a camp of human hunters backed by the British military set up within their territory, and wanted the help of the Cleareyes' totem in dealing with it. Eaven thought the proposal over and agreed, bringing the pack back to the Pure's compound. She took them into the main building and led them past the main lounge room into a smaller study, where a pale-skinned, red-haired man Wolf-blooded nervously showed them into a staircase that led beneath the compound. The air inside was hot, like stepping into a boiler room, and Aed found himself face to face with the immense form of the totem <i>Matharazith</i>. The spirit was built like a rottweiler, compact, four-legged, and covered in muscle, with a blunt head that appeared to be all maw. It lay on a bed of iron nails, and twisted scraps of iron shrapnel budded and grew from its flesh with the pattering of bloody rain.<br /> <br /> The spirit seemed initially taken aback at being confronted by a Forsaken, even with its packmates lurking in the background, but Aed's words were irresistible. His words were carefully chosen, plucked from the skein of various futures to choose those most likely to sway the Totem, yet it still took every bit of his formidable charisma to persuade <i>Matharazith</i> to accept the trade. Aed would fight a single target of the Cleareyes' choosing, and in return this spirit of violence and blood-soaked earth would bury the quarry. The totem bellowed to Eaven that he wanted his &quot;sculptor&quot; to be ready as he pledged his service to the Harbinger's war.<br /> <br /> OOC: Aed swung a cool +5 to his Impression level at the start of this negotiation, enough to bring literally any impression level, even Hostile, up to Perfect. We still capped out Aed's number of rolls at the usual level as if it were an Extended Action, giving him 9 rolls to open 11 doors. He scored the second Exceptional Success he needed on the very last roll.<br /> <br /> The pack returned from the Cleareyes' turf triumphant, and decided to update their Forsaken allies on the vague plan without specifying that they were involving the Pure. Regan tried to contact Iule with the Message rite, asking for her to come to Aed's as soon as she could, but the <i>Rahu</i> hadn't shown up by the time the rest of the pack went home, nor by the time Aed went to bed. Instead he was woken at two in the morning by a hammering at the door, and he found Iule there when he opened it. She was a bit put out to discover that there was not, in fact, an emergency that demanded her immediate attention, she'd come straight from her shift monitoring the quarry. Aed explained that the plan the pack had was that they would bury the gap in the Gauntlet under tons of rock, hoping that the collapse would push back the Silver City forces. He asked Iule if she knew what kind of resources the Werewolf hunters had at their disposal, since presumably the entire British military wasn't openly working to combat the supernatural, but she could only tell him that they appeared to have pull and connections with the rest of the army but that the Silent Sons had never tested the limits. Both wolves agreed that destroying Braghdar, and any other Broken the Silver City may have persuaded to work for them, was to be their top priority before they collapsed the quarry.<br /> <br /> Aaron and Aed met Regan a few blocks from her home the next morning to nail down the final details of the plan as she walked to school. They had the support of two other packs, unbeknownst to either, and the backing of a powerful spirit. They needed to isolate Braghdar first, and Aaron noted that his wards around the Gauntlet breach hadn't been triggered since their last encounter with Silver City.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-14T16:33:34-07:00","id":1304913,"title":"","content":"Just a heads up for anyone wondering about the lack of updates, a few players have been snowed under and unable to make it the past few weekends. The coming weekend will probably also be a no-go, but we expect to be back in two weeks.<br /> <br /> On the plus side, this gives the players plenty of time to work out what they actually want to do about Braghdar and Silver City.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-28T20:50:17-07:00","id":1308587,"title":"","content":"Session 25<br /> <br /> OOC: No Orla this session unfortunately.<br /> <br /> Without a clear plan on how to extract Braghdar from the quarry, the Harbingers spent the next few days patrolling their territory and laying the groundwork for their next attempt at warding their territory through Aaron's rites. They were returning to their cars, parked near the grove on the edge of the Cleareyes' territory, when they saw the storm rolling in. The strange sound of thunder accompanied the advancing snow, leading to worried looks between the packmates. It could be a natural phenomenon, but privately the wolves suspected that they weren't that lucky.<br /> <br /> The snow had begun to fall by the time they reached the grove. They were almost back to their cars when Aaron detected a new scent on the wind, not unlike a wet dog. He snapped to attention, telling Regan to circle around unseen, while he and Aed approached from the front. They reached the cars to find a slender figure perched cross-legged on the hood of Aed's vehicle, their face hidden by the jacket hood drawn up to protect against the weather. The figure took a moment sizing up the two middle-aged men, then jumped to the ground and addressed them.<br /> <br /> &quot;Well, I'm betting neither of you are my sister.&quot;<br /> <br /> The figure pulled back the jacket hood to reveal a shock of short, blonde hair. It was clear from her features that this newcomer had some kind of family connection to Regan, but there were stranger elements to her appearance. Slit pupils, near-pointed ears, and the delicate curve of a fang biting into her bottom lip almost made her look like she had shifted to <i>Dalu</i>, but she lacked the size and muscle mass for that. She introduced herself as Maddie, and asked where she could find Regan. Aaron had just finished saying, &quot;You'll see her when she wants you to,&quot; when Regan tapped Maddie on the shoulder.<br /> <br /> Maddie's enthusiasm with meeting her sister was met with a rather more reserved response from Regan. She explained to the Harbingers that Reynard hadn't told the family about meeting Regan, in fact neither Maddie nor her sister Eva had actually known Regan existed. However, in Maddie's words, &quot;It's very hard to keep secrets from me.&quot; She'd discovered Regan's existence after Reynard's most recent visit to the pack. Regan spoke up, at last, to ask if Maddie was involved in &quot;Wolfing&quot;, a turn of phrase that made Maddie chuckle before she confirmed that yes, she was one of the People. <br /> <br /> With snow coming down, no one really wanted to hang around outside. They agreed to drive back to Aed's place, and Maddie insisted on going with Regan in Aed's car in spite of the cramped conditions. The two girls chatted with one another as they drove, though Maddie largely dominated the conversation. She was about a year older than Regan, a grade ahead of her in school. After a few minutes Maddie asked Regan about her mother. &quot;My mother's dead,&quot; was the short response. A brief pause later, Maddie said just about the only thing that could have actually endeared her to Regan. &quot;Mine too.&quot;<br /> <br /> Back at Aed's home, while waiting for Aaron to join them, the pack asked Maddie if she could use her ability to turn up secrets to help them dislodge the Silver City group at the quarry. She told them that she'd need to see Braghdar in person, or at least see a reasonably fresh corpse who had seen Braghdar just before it died, to work out what the spirit's Ban or Bane might be. Aed floated the idea of Num-Num's creator. Maddie's, &quot;What's a Num-Num?&quot; query was cut off half-finished when something seemed to let her know, and her eyes went wide with horror. Nevertheless, she agreed to see what she could find out. By the time Aaron arrived, she'd turned up a few details about the man working with Silver City responsible for the Claimed's creation. She couldn't divine his name, but she did tell them that his routine involved work at both the quarry and the Andersonstown police station. He didn't appear to know he was responsible for Num-Num, in fact he hadn't ever seen the <i>duguthim</i>, their connection appeared to be that his actions had spawned the Torture Spirit that went on to become one half of the Claimed they all knew.<br /> <br /> The flurry of snow had lightened to just a light snowfall, and Maddie told them all that she needed to head off to catch the bus home if she wanted to avoid being out too late. Aed, not wanting a rogue Werewolf floating around their territory, insisted on calling and paying for a cab to take her home. Maddie and Regan went outside to wait for it together. They showed off their powers to one another, each revealing that they had some mastery of the elements, with Maddie's affinity for water and Regan's control of the air allowing them to play with the snow around them in different ways. Maddie pulled up her jacket and shirt to show Regan the eye-shaped scars down her left side, the markings from when she had been granted the Gift of Insight from the spirit Sliverbug. Just before the taxi arrived, the two girls swapped phone numbers. Regan gave Maddie the phone number for Aed's home, while Maddie gave Regan her home phone number. She told Regan to just ask for her if either their father or her other sister answered the phone, pointing out that neither Joseph nor Eva would recognise Regan's voice anyway. She didn't notice the hurt that flashed in Regan's eyes at that comment.<br /> <br /> That night, the pack set off through the snow to walk the borders of their territory and invoke the warding rites Aaron had prepared. The rites went off without a hitch, but they were returning through the Andersonstown streets when they stumbled across a group of British soldiers erecting a temporary checkpoint. Most of the soldiers were occupied with getting a barrier in place to stop cars from passing, but two of them approached the wolves and told them that they'd be searching for weapons. Regan had, thankfully, left her stolen gun hidden at home, so they saw no reason not to acquiesce. As Aed was being searched, however, Aaron spotted a flash of something silver being palmed by the soldier carrying out the search. He tried to catch the soldier's attention and ask if it was a crucifix, but the man just shook his head. Aed felt a searing pain moments later as silver contacted his skin just above his ankle, and felt the rise of the Death Rage inside him.<br /> <br /> The screaming started seconds later. Aed shifted to <i>Dalu</i>, sending the soldier with the silver object fleeing in screaming terror as moon-wrought delusions infected his mind. The soldier's companion staggered back, trying to get his rifle ready to fire, but Regan ran in and wrestled for control of the rifle with him. Aaron shifted to <i>Dalu</i> as well. The soldiers occupied with the barrier construction joined the fight, one of them racing towards Aaron. He seemed consumed by a frenzy, and drove a knife repeatedly into Aaron's abdomen. The other soldiers tried to stay as far clear of the monsters as they possibly could, opening fire on the one clear target they had in Aed. To the despair of the soldiers, the Werewolf's inhuman reflexes allowed him to dodge nearly every shot, and the one salvo that landed barely seemed to bother him at all.<br /> <br /> The wolves, even Aed, were able to focus their attention on disarming the soldiers rather than killing them. Claws were stained in blood as they ripped open arms and near-severed wrists, sending rifles clattering to the ground. The soldier wrestling Regan managed to get his knife out even as Regan vanished from view with his rifle, only to reappear in <i>Urshul</i> form. Her jaws closed around a second soldier's wrist, almost biting his hand clean off, and his gun fell to the ground. The soldier she'd been grappling drew his knife, but this time it was Aaron taking to <i>Urshul</i> to rake open his arm. Undeterred, the man went for his pistol with his one good hand, but this time Aaron's jaws closed around the gun and bit it in two.<br /> <br /> A salvo of bullets slammed into Aed's chest as he advanced on the last unscathed soldier. He edges of his vision, already tinged red in his rage, began to turn black. He could feel his organs and the slivers of metal filling his inside, but still he resisted the urge to shift to <i>Garou</i> or succumb to the Death Rage entirely. The sight of him, bloodied, snarling, and flexing his claws, was enough to sending the rest of the wounded soldiers fleeing.<br /> <br /> Regan found herself squaring off against the frenzied soldier. The man was driven wild, his eyes unblinking, froth forming at the corners of his mouth. He lunged at her, again and gain, each time she barely corkscrewed her body out of the way of the slashing knife. She barged into him, knocking him back several places, and then shifted back to human as he tried to recover. She swept out his legs with a kick, and there was a sharp crack as his head hit a patch of ice on the ground. His eyes rolled back into his head and he lay still, but still breathing.<br /> <br /> Aaron's opponent, with just one good hand and no firearms left, steeled his nerves and drew out a grenade. Eyes wide with fear, he backed up as far as he could and threw it, hands shaking, towards Aaron. His aim was off though, and Regan was able to call on her own Gifts to divert the grenade even further, and it landed somewhere behind Aaron where he was only cost on the edge of the blast. After the stab wounds he had suffered, however, he found himself only barely conscious as flame and steel tore at his back. And then that final stalwart soldier, bereft of any weapons at all, was running from the bloodied Werewolves.<br /> <br /> The entire exchange had taken only seconds, but as their hearing returned in the explosion's wake they could hear sirens wailing in the distance. Aed took a few moments to centre himself, clawing control back as his packmates helped soothe the rage of the beast inside. It took a supreme effort of will, and even buried Aed was sure the monster within was closer to the surface than it ever had been before, but he managed to fight off <i>Wasu-Im</i>. He and Aaron began to force their most grievous wounds to heal by spending essence, and then the pack shifted into <i>Urhan</i> and began to run.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-26T21:59:51-07:00","id":1314652,"title":"","content":"Oops, forgot to update this following the last two sessions. Just quickly to get us up to date:<br /> <br /> Sessions 26 and 27<br /> <br /> The pack managed to evade the hunting soldiers without much difficulty. Reagan used her gifts to will her packmates as silent and stealthy as she was, and they slipped through streets and undergrowth until they were able to circle back to Aed's home. Aaron stopped off at the church, but the rest of the pack holed up at Aed's place. Reagan didn't want to risk the streets with the army out in force, but she was able to persuade Steph in a phone call that she and Aed had been working with Father Aaron and that, quite truthfully, she'd rather sleep over at Aed's place than brave the streets right now. She slept through the night on Lizzie's bed, a fact Lizzie herself didn't seem to notice when she came home in the wee hours of the morning and passed out almost on top of Reagan.<br /> <br /> The pack left Aaron behind to recover from his injuries. They waited until Reagan had finished her schooling for the day, then passed into the Shadow to seek an audience with the Old Man of the Mountain. The snow, falling gently all day, had stirred into a full blown storm in the Shadow. Aed wanted to find out who commanded the Court of Bloody Communion locally, having some ideas on how to deal with the issue of the quarry, and asked the butterfly-like snowflake spirits who commanded in this area. They only gave him nonsense answers, but he worked out on his own that, based on the elements he'd seen aligned with the Bloody Communion, the Old Man himself was likely in charge in spite of his seeming neutrality in the war.<br /> <br /> The long climb to the Old Man's court led the pack through an ever-intensifying storm. Visibility dropped to almost nothing as they approached the peak. They sensed movement in the storm, and humanoid figures began to form in the swirling snow and rain. Two ahead and two behind, blocking their path forward and back, and an immense figure as tall as a house to their right just floating beyond the edge of the cliff.<br /> <br /> With a voice like the crack of thunder and the sound of shattering ice, the immense spirit asked the <i>Uratha</i> which court they owed their allegiance to. It seemed baffled when Aed pointed out that they were Werewolves, that they had no such tie to either court. The sky titan tried to rephrase its question, asking if they would join its attack on the heart of Belfast, where the Union still ruled. Again, Aed refused, telling the spirit that they could not cross so many territories without permission. As much as it seemed to be struggling to understand the idea of unaligned beings, even <i>Uratha</i>, in the context of the war, the great spirit did understand the idea of needing to obey strange and arcane laws. The titan moved like lightning, seemingly frozen in place only to snap to a new position with impossible speed. It led the pack through the Eye of the Mountain to the Old Man's court, its body of wind, lightning, and snow stretching and twisting to fit through the tunnel and setting the chains therein rattling with its passage.<br /> <br /> Within the Old Man's court, the Harbingers witnessed the titan asking for volunteers to join its army. They watched Broken come shuffling from beneath the eaves of the Old Man's Willow Wardens, prostrating themselves at the great spirit's feet. The Old Man sat on his throne, unmoved, until the sight of so many Broken gathering seemed to stir him to action. He rose from his throne and addressed the sky titan as Lightning-Strikes-Ice, and reminded the spirit of the terms of his allegiance to the Court. &quot;No more than one in three,&quot; he said, and the last handful of Broken who had made their way to the titan's side crawled back towards their master's throne a little sheepishly.<br /> <br /> Once Lightning-Strikes-Ice had departed, Aed approached the Old Man. He asked the Mountain if there was anything he could tell the pack about why Braghdar had gone rogue. The Old Man answered that Braghdar credited the human army for shutting down the quarry since they had, after all, been the ones who had actually physically closed it. Braghdar, and a few of his fellow Broken, believed they owed the humans a debt greater than his former fealty to the Old Man. The pack decided that they should try and convince Braghdar that the army were just the pack's pawns, and that he should owe that favour to them instead. They performed Reagan's Message Rite to call Braghdar to a parley in the quarry on the Shadow-side, promising no harm would come to him and offering a way to resolve the conflict, but asking that he leave the soldiers behind and only come with his fellow Broken.<br /> <br /> They waited at the quarry into the evening. Luna had risen in her swollen, gibbous form, though the clouds left by Lightning-Strikes-Ice's passage obscured her mostly from view. They waited for the Broken's arrival, but it was Orla who noticed they had come. She could see the Broken creeping through the rubble around them, flanking the pack and preparing an ambush. She pointed them out to her packmates, and Aed called out to the Broken. Invoking the Voice of Glory and hunting through his prophetic dreams for the right words, Aed persuaded them not to fight but to listen to what the pack had to say. The Broken they recognised as Braghdar hesitated, and from there it was almost a formality as Aed brought the full force of his charisma to bear. He told the Broken that the soldiers had been manipulated by the pack into shutting down the quarry, that the idea belonged to the Harbingers and that they had only used the human soldiers as proxies. One by one the Broken seemed to waver on hearing that, until even Braghdar was looking at the wolves in admiration. Aed offered to resettle the Broken somewhere safe, so long as they used their influence with the army to convince the mortals that the quarry was unsafe. He told them that he intended to collapse the quarry entirely, and that if the soldiers didn't vacate soon they would be caught in the collapse. The Broken, hearing that the quarry could soon be destroyed entirely, readily agreed to lend their help.<br /> <br /> The Harbingers wanted to make sure expectations that something would go wrong at the quarry were properly in place before they invoked <i>Matharazith</i>'s power. They spent the remainder of the week promoting a food drive for the quarry workers who had been stood down, and made sure to invite Martin Covoney, the local journalist they'd worked with previously, to the function. They wanted to sow rumours that, without proper maintenance, the quarry had probably become unsafe. They also arranged a meeting with Eaven, convincing her to give sanctuary to the rebel Broken in the further reaches of her territory where Belfast's war didn't typically reach. The nature of the Pure's Locus basically guaranteed there would be essence within the Pure's territory for the earth spirits, and Eaven seemed happy enough to take them in.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-02T21:13:11-07:00","id":1316189,"title":"","content":"Session 28<br /> <br /> OOC: This is a big one, and I have a few things I'd like to dig into and analyse in a later post. It may take a day or two for me to get my thoughts in order though, in part because I'm still processing everything that went down. If nothing else though, this was an absolute cracker of a session.<br /> <br /> Sunday rolled around, and with it came the charity drive the Harbingers had arranged for the out-of-work quarry employees. The morning started slow, with the local gossip Gloria being the first through the door, but by the time Martin Covoney showed up the church was humming with most of the regular members of the congregation along with a fair few others Aaron didn't normally see for his Sunday services. Martin sequestered himself in a corner of the room at first, taking pictures. The pack had cleared most of the pews to one side, and set up tables loaded with the offerings of the people of Andersonstown. Orla caused a small scene trying to get herself into Martin's pictures, when she accidentally backed right over Gloria and knocked her to the ground. Gloria played the event to the hilt, retiring to a chair with a trembling hand held to the light graze on her forehead, while her friends clustered around her with a first aid kit chattering about concussions and pausing every thirty seconds or so to shoot glares at Orla from across the room.<br /> <br /> A slightly foul smell cutting through the crowd told the pack that Wade had arrived to the proceedings as well. The Ghost Wolf lurked at the threshold of the church until Reagan invited him inside. The adults of the pack were busy keeping the event humming along, and Aed was in the midst of a discussion with Martin and several workers of the quarry that he was pointedly pushing towards a discussion of how unsafe the quarry would be without its workers and their regular maintenance, so the other Werewolf was left entirely in Reagan's hands. Wade confided quietly that he had picked up traces of intruders on his turf, and he was hoping the Harbingers would lend their help in hunting them. He didn't know what he was up against, and would like to have back-up in case they turned out to be something formidable. Reagan agreed happily enough, but mentioned that her pack was currently on a hunt. Once they had wrapped up their current business, they would seek Wade out. In the meantime, she told him to grab what food he could get from the tables. Just before Wade hurried off to scoop up armfuls of donated food, Reagan noticed that there was a reddish tinge to his irises. It looked as if they might be changing colour.<br /> <br /> The drive turned out to be quite the success. Sure enough, it made the paper on Monday morning. Not front page news, but in the respectably prominent first few pages. More importantly, Martin had made mention of the concerns that the quarry could become unsafe without the attention of its workers. Aed spent his day going around the bars, where he found many of the displaced workers were whiling away the hours outside of their respective homes, and did what he could to keep the rumours that the quarry was a danger circulating. Reagan did something similar in the schoolyard, starting rumours that, in the nature of rumours, were soon circulating back to her amplified a thousandfold. To hear the schoolyard tell it, the quarry was going to just erupt in the next few days.<br /> <br /> After school, the pack convened and decided they wanted to make sure the military was getting the message. They wanted to get in contact with Braghdar to find out who he had reported to when he worked with the army, but of course he was now safely ensconced in Eaven's territory. They gathered around their mirror locus and invoked Reagan's Message Rite, asking Eaven to meet them as soon as possible at the usual spot. Then they piled into their cars and drove out to the grove to wait. And wait. And wait. Orla passed out in the back seat of Aed's car. Reagan started to worry about getting home before Steph freaked out. Finally, after almost four hours, at past 9 in the evening, they saw movement approaching. Aine, Eaven's smaller packmate, rode up to the grove on a blue pushbike. She hurried forwards to greet them, but fixed most of her attention on Aed. The pack asked for permission to cross the border and it was clear that Aine really wanted to say yes, but she really couldn't agree. She just didn't have that authority. Eaven was the only one who could agree to that, and she was back in the compound with the others after their long excursion into the deep Shadow. Aine let slip that she was the only member of the pack uninjured right now, the others all having been hurt on their hunt. Aed praised her for being able to avoid harm when the others couldn't, and Aine blushed and admitted that it was only because Eaven didn't like to let her fight. Aed mused that it seemed like she didn't really like to let <i>anyone</i> in her pack do any fighting, before he recalled the strange emaciated male of the pack. Getting a bit distracted, he asked Aine about him, and managed to tease a reluctant Aine into telling him the story.<br /> <br /> The man was named Daithi, and he had been leader of the Cleareyes when Eaven went through her First Change. He and his wife, Alison, the middle-aged woman Aed and Aaron had met on previous visits to the Cleareyes, they ruled the back with brutal and casual violence. When it became apparent that Eaven was strong, perhaps even dangerously strong, Daithi and Alison felt threatened. They challenged Eaven to a leadership contest, hoping to preempt any aspirations she might have, when she was still practically fresh from the change. Eaven had seen the challenge coming, however. She had found <i>Matharazith</i> and forged a pact with him, and on the day of the challenge she arranged to ferry him across the Gauntlet and have him devour the Cleareyes' old totem. As the pack reeled from the loss, she struck at Daithi with a magic granted to her by her benefactor. The attack left him physically unharmed, but ripped out his soul and hollowed out his mind. Eaven claimed leadership of the pack, winning Renown for being able to do so without costing the pack the strength of its former leader, and ensured that her position would be all the more stable through reducing Daithi into a servant rather than having him plotting behind her back. Alison, for her part, was so cowed by what had been done to her husband that she had caused very little trouble of her own in the years since.<br /> <br /> When Aine finished telling her story, the pack asked if she could relay a message to Braghdar for them. She agreed, hesitatingly, and said she would be three or four hours. Aed agreed to wait, with the sleeping Orla in his car, while Aaron dropped Reagan off at home and then returned to wait for the Fire-Touched to come back. She returned in the early hours of the morning, having to knock on the window of Aed's car to wake him up. He'd been snoozing inside, keeping the car running to get the heater going and stave off the winter's chill outside. It wasn't particularly good news. Braghdar, it transpired, had a hard time distinguishing humans from one another. He'd mostly been operating on the principle of &quot;Army uniforms good, no uniforms bad&quot;. He had remembered the name McIntire though, as a human he'd spent some time talking to.<br /> <br /> Just before they went home to finally get a night's sleep, Aine mentioned that Eaven would really prefer it if they stopped dispatching Lunes to talk to her through the Message Rite. The packs agreed to meet instead at the grove each week to discuss potential problems, and they swapped phone numbers. The Pure gave the Harbingers one of their Wolf-Bloodeds' home numbers, while the Harbingers gave the Cleareyes Aed's number. Then Aine jumped back on her bicycle for one last long ride of the night, and the Harbingers went home.<br /> <br /> The following day, once Reagan was out of school, the pack decided they had waited long enough. They needed to send a clear message to the Silver City forces to vacate the quarry, and start the 48 hour countdown to when they could invoke <i>Matharazith</i> and collapse the quarry completely. The method they chose to go with was to have Reagan shift genders and then, in his male form, borrow a bunch of coins from Aed and call the quarry office from a payphone. He tried to get past the soldier manning the phone to someone of actual rank, but the soldier instead seemed determined to find out everything he could about the &quot;threat&quot; Reagan alluded to, as well as Reagan's own details. Eventually, even invoking his Irraka Auspice to worm his way into the soldier's confidences proved not to be enough, and Reagan felt the phone line cut out when the soldier determined he had gotten everything he could from the call. Determined to deliver his message, Reagan called back and yelled down the line, &quot;You're all going to die if you don't leave. You have 48 hours.&quot;<br /> <br /> Within an hour of that exchange, Aaron sensed his wards in the Shadow begin to trigger. He was sitting at Aed's table, helping brainstorm alternative approaches, when the sensation struck. The Harbingers reacted immediately. They seized masks from Aed's collection to help protect their identities, save for Aaron who wound bandages around his head to mask everything but his one good eye, and shifted into a variety of <i>Urshul</i> and <i>Dalu</i> forms. The pack rushed into the Shadow through their Locus, following the increasingly well-trod path to the quarry to confront the humans breaking the taboo.<br /> <br /> Reagan slipped ahead of the pack, making her way through the shadows and darkness. The pack spotted the humans staking out an ambush up ahead, and Reagan circled, looking for the weakest prey, intent on setting an ambush of her own. There were eight humans taking cover in and around the crevice through which the world was torn. Four of them were easy enough to spot, clad in the astronaut-like environment suits and wearing tethers that linked them back through the portal to the world of the flesh. The other four men had eschewed such outfits. They wore full combat gear, heavily armed and armoured, with a mix of unusual weaponry. One man, closest to the portal, lurked away from the others and watched the approaches through the scope of an immense rifle. Another soldier carried an assault weapon and was hunkered down in one of the cracks in the quarry's ground in a makeshift bunker. To his right was a man carrying a large, weighted net, and to his left a soldier carrying, of all things, a spear with a gleaming silver point.<br /> <br /> Reagan picked her target, the man with the net. Teeth and fur launched from the shadows, bowling the man over and ripping into his arm. The net fell to the ground, and seconds later the shouting and shooting began.<br /> <br /> The Harbingers rushed forward to help their packmate before she could be surrounded. They had a glimpse of the bunkered soldier reach for something at his waist, and then the ground itself erupted beneath them. Reagan was too far away, and Aed was carried forward by his Berserker Gifts that allowed him to shrug off something as trivial as an explosion, but the others were battered as earth and fire consumed them. Aaron shifted straight to <i>Gauru</i> and charged the gunline, rushing for the man responsible for the explosion. His claws tore clear through the man's body armour, shredding it and cutting deep into his chest. To Aaron's right, Reagan went for the net-armed soldier's other arm to put him out of the fight, but underestimated her own strength as she tore the arm clear from his body. Orla rushed to grapple with the soldiers, while Aed followed more slowly in his packmates' wake and let loose a howl that reverberated through the Shadow.<br /> <br /> &quot;You should not have returned. I warned you!&quot; His war cry sunk into the soldiers, slowing their movements as the fear and Lunacy began to spread.<br /> <br /> The soldiers held their ground, and the battlefield descended into a chaos of claws and teeth and bullets. Guns were turned towards Aaron as desperate soldiers sought to put down the immense beast in their midst. A couple of the soldiers went for their sidearms, which the wolves knew from grim experience would contain silver bullets, and one of the men in the environment suits went to the aid of the dying man at Reagan's Feat. A heavy crack sounded as the rifleman at the rear of the group tried to put an immense silver round through Aaron's skull, and he was saved only by Reagan invoking her Gifts to manipulate the chaos and confusion of the battlefield. As essence roared through the channels carved into her body, the bullet's flight twisted and slammed instead into the back of the man in the bunker still staggering away from Aaron's rending claws. The wolves all had an impressive view of the large calibre silver round as it flattened itself against the soldier's body armour. It didn't break through, but the impact almost floored him and knocked all the air from his lungs.<br /> <br /> In a single oasis of calm as the wolves and soldiers fought, Aed saw one of the humans ignore the fighting and stride towards him at an unhurried pace. The spear-wielding soldier shrugged out of his jacket, revealing a bare upper torso criss-crossed with thick scar tissue in the clear shape of wolf claws. He tossed his jacket to the ground and grinned up at Aed. &quot;Wouldn't want to get this dirty,&quot; he said, and lunged for Aed with his spear. It struck and sank deep into the tissue of his thigh, burning all the way, and left Aed pinned to the ground as the man danced back out of reach and drew a gleaming silver knife from his belt.<br /> <br /> With Orla and one of the soldiers tied together in a twisted grapple, Aed and his opponent occupied behind him, and as the only wolf on the field to have taken the war form, Aaron found himself the focus of most of the remaining guns. Bullets thudded into his chest faster than he could heal, and the wounded man before him leapt from his makeshift bunker and unloaded the entire clip from his rifle into Aaron's chest. Aaron felt organs rip and tear, some of them blasted almost to pieces, beyond his body's ability to heal on the spot. Even in the red rage of <i>Garou</i>, he could feel the darkness at the edges of his vision beginning to win and close in. He had no choice if he were to survive. He shifted down to <i>Urshul</i>, sensing instinctively that to resume <i>Hishu</i> or <i>Urhan</i> would kill him, and fled the field. He leapt and twisted amidst the jagged, broken ground of the quarry, using the spike-like rock formations to hide himself from the guns of the humans.<br /> <br /> Those guns, in turn, shifted towards the only wolf running clear of the melee. Reagan found herself in the sights, and set her Essence racing through her Gifts. Speed found her legs, and she raced ahead of the gunfire to take shelter behind a rocky outcropping. She waited for a pause in the gunfire, a moment's distraction as Aed roared a challenge, and in that fraction of an instant when no eyes were upon her she moved to a new hiding spot beyond the means of the humans to see her.<br /> <br /> Aed snapped the spear from his leg without breaking eye contact from the human in front of him. His Berserker's Might shielded him from most of the pain, but the Death Rage was rising from the smallest touch of silver that had crept through. The wound in his leg throbbed and burned and froze in a streaming cacophony of pain, but he didn't flinch as he closed the distance. <br /> <br /> &quot;You have a lot of scars,&quot; he told the still-grinning hunter in front of him. &quot;Let me add to them.&quot;<br /> <br /> And, for the first time any of his packmates could recall, Aed shifted into <i>Garou</i>. His already large frame filled out rapidly, furred muscle expanding as he took the Favoured Form he had so long denied. Claws moved like lightning, in and out of the soldier's guard, leaving red ruin in their wake. The bare, muscled chest of the man tore into ribbons of gore and glimpses of gleaming white bone. But somehow, impossibly, the man stayed on his feet.<br /> <br /> &quot;My,&quot; he managed to gasp out, &quot;what big teeth you have.&quot;<br /> <br /> He struck out with his knife, but Aed moved like one who knew where the blow would land. The visions of the Cahalith hummed through his mind as he saw the knife coming just as he had in dreams long before this day, and he battered it out of the way. The soldier turned the strike into a roll, sliding out of the way, and at that moment Aed realised every gun on the battlefield was pointed at him. An explosion of gunfire ripped into him, bullets hammering away, and again Reagan saved a packmate by twisting the sickly silver bullet from the massive rifle into the ground.<br /> <br /> Beyond his target, Aed could just make out the crevice these humans had used to enter the <i>Hisil</i>. His heart fell when he saw movement among the rocks, a man in a soldier's uniform came into view. For all his personal confidence, Aed knew his pack was on the cusp of defeat. Aaron was gone, running with what strength remained in his limbs, buoyed up as much by Hope's blessings than anything. Reagan remained unscathed, dodging bullets with supernatural ease, but she was sure to have near burned through all her Essence to do so and now hid among the rocks waiting for a chance to rejoin the battle. And Orla had staggered out of view, avoiding the line of soldiers and their wicked silver bullets. He was the only one left standing, and now there were <i>more</i> humans comi-<br /> <br /> Wait. A second soldier appeared behind the first, and a third behind him, and Aed realised there was something wrong with these men. The first wore a uniform deeply stained with dry blood. The second was missing a fairly large chunk of his skull. The third's entrails slipped and slopped from his stomach, dragging through the dirt behind him. And then the fourth came through, without any legs. For a moment Aed wondered what horrors these humans had been experimenting with, to call up such allies, when a large, skeletal hand reached over the edge of the cliff above the crevice. A second appeared moments later, and Cairnbreath hauled himself into view.<br /> <br /> &quot;A chance to repay my debt, I see.&quot; The ancient death god's dry hiss resounded over the battlefield. The Silver City men turned in horror to see the dead at their backs and a figure out of nightmare descending down the cliff above them. A thick mist began to rise about their feet, a miasma that caused the environmental suits many of the men wore to disintegrate, sloughing off and taking large chunks of flesh with it. Reagan and Aed could see men weeping blood, muscles come apart beneath increasingly translucent skin, the crumbling of exposed bone.<br /> <br /> Something in the soldiers refused to give, however. As one, they remained on their feet. One man, his armour torn to ribbons by Aaron's claws, seized the fallen body of the man Riley had dismembered at the start of the fight and hauled him towards the portal. He roared out &quot;Clear a path!&quot; and his fellow men turned their weapons on the zombies. The walking dead jerked under the focused fire, most of them crumbling on the spot, and the dying men staggered through. The rifleman turned to follow, falling to his knees when Reagan bore down on his back from behind tearing deep gouges in his flesh, but he regained his footing and gifted her with a parting shot that burned the last dregs of her essence to avoid. The bare chested man looked at Aed and down to his knife with something that could almost be disappointment in spite of his horrific injuries, and he ran to leap through the portal in the wake of his comrades.<br /> <br /> Cairnbreath crawled down to the base of the cliff to nurse his fallen zombies, and one by one they began to twitch and return to life. The shocking damage done to their bodies seemed to matter little as Cairnbreath caressed the fallen, his long tongue flicking out to touch them as he whispered in their ears. At last, in a louder voice, the grim spirit directed his creations back towards the crevice. &quot;Make sure they don't return.&quot;<br /> <br /> The Harbingers had regrouped, but Aed was still struggling with the rising rage from his contact with silver. Cairnbreath seemed to size up the situation, and without a parting word he followed his zombies through the crevice. The wolves could hear the distant sound of gunfire and screaming, a sound that grew dramatically louder when Cairnbreath vanished from their sight.<br /> <br /> Orla called on the Half-Moon's heritage to restrain Aed's Death Rage, and the four of them stood a moment on the battlefield trying to take stock of what had happened. Then, as one, they turned and ran.<br /> <br /> With a wounded Aaron to help, the return trip to their Locus took over an hour. When they stepped through into Aed's living room, they could hear distant, sporadic gunfire from the direction of the quarry. Andersonstown rang with the sound of alarms and sirens, and Riley rushed to the phone to make sure Steph knew she was alright. Outside, police were telling anyone who ventured outside that they needed to return to their homes. By the time she finished talking to her Aunt, the gunfire had petered out. Aed helped lift Aaron, now in <i>Dalu</i>, up onto the kitchen table to assess the worst of the damage, when he spotted Iule and Bull from the Silent Sons standing outside through his kitchen window. He rushed to the door to usher them in, noting that Bull had a mark on his face like someone had dragged their nails down his cheek.<br /> <br /> The two packs swapped stories. Aed explained that they had been making preparations to collapse the quarry, and mentioned how they had tried to threaten the Silver City troops into leaving. He described how that had triggered the incursion into the Shadow, and how the Harbingers had responded and fought with the Werewolf hunters until the intervention of Cairnbreath. The Silent Sons, for their part, were baffled that a spirit of such strength could exist in Belfast without the Forsaken knowing of it, but Aed only revealed that it owed the Harbingers a favour and had come to repay the debt.<br /> <br /> The Sons, then, described the scene they had witnessed from their overlook monitoring the quarry. They had seen it flit in and out of corporeality, appearing out of nowhere in front of soldiers and, in some cases, literally melting the faces from those men. It had sewn merry ruin throughout the quarry for several minutes before vanishing. They couldn't be sure whether it had returned across the Gauntlet or not, but it had certainly left a great many of the walking dead behind. A few had even spilled out of the camp and wandered into the countryside or up the mountain before the army managed to get the situation under control. After some discussion with the Harbingers, Iule decided to ask the Sons' Totem, Farraige, the Silence in the Mist, to scour this side of the Gauntlet for any trace of Cairnbreath. The Harbingers caught a glimpse of a pair of dark eyes peering at them from a shadowy corner of the room as she spoke her Totem's name, before it vanished again from sight. The Harbingers promised to clean up the remaining mess, and told the Sons to return to their own territory.<br /> <br /> The next two days passed in a grey fog for the Harbingers. They did their duty, scouring the countryside for any of the living dead that had escaped the battle, picking them off with relative ease. Aaron sequestered himself in his Glade in the Shadow, unable to appear anywhere he might be seen until he healed enough to be capable of taking human form once again. The pack kept an eye on the news, watching as the official story became that of an insurgent attack on the quarry involving possible chemical weapons, with no group coming forward to take responsibility. Silver City mostly pulled out of the quarry the day after, but the pack noted there was always at least one patrol of soldiers inside even if the buildings were no longer manned.<br /> <br /> At last, two days after their phone call to the quarry, the pack met Aine and the hulking <i>Matharazith</i> atop the flanks of the Old Man, at the overlook spot in the Flesh they had so often used to observe the quarry. Aed noted that Aine seemed nervous around her Totem, while it in turn always moved to keep her close. Her stiff posture and effort to twist away spelled out clearly what she thought of that proximity. When <i>Matharazith</i> rumbled out, &quot;It is time,&quot; Aine reached out with one trembling hand to touch the spirit's flank. Earth spiralled up from the ground, merging with the spirit's body, rendering it corporeal as sweat began to run down Aine's face even in the freezing weather. One massive, muscled leg rose, before slamming into the ground. <i>Matharazith</i> repeated this action once, twice, three times.<br /> <br /> The first tremor struck far, far beneath their feet, the rumble only barely perceptible. It was enough to have the military patrol below them, wandering the quarry on high alert, freeze in place. Then the second tremor hit, far more forceful, and the pack watched the distant soldiers break into a run as they fled. The third tremor struck and never seemed to end. The walls of the quarry shivered and shook and began to collapse. The mountainside itself, far below them, slipped and poured like water towards the wound in its flank. A pack of wolves watched as the hole in the mountain vanished beneath a wave of earth and a cloud of dust.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-03T17:52:07-07:00","id":1316340,"title":"","content":"For some reason in the last two write-ups I started spelling Regan's name wrong again <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/confused.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Confused\" smilieid=\"10\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Now on to the ramble!<br /> <br /> A few members of the game are about to go overseas for 4-5 weeks, so we were aiming this week or next to hit a natural end point before the break. I'd say we successfully managed that with this session, ending with a final resolution to the events that began this campaign. Odds seem good that the quarry itself isn't going to be the biggest issue for the Harbingers moving forward... With the game's one year anniversary taking place during the break, it sure makes that session feel like a mighty appropriate season finale.<br /> <br /> Speaking of how long we've been playing this game, that sparked the first thing I wanted to dive into. After a year of play, averaging a game ~every fortnight, we've seen Harmony shift exactly twice. Aaron moving towards the Flesh with an unlucky dice roll, and Orla moving towards the Flesh because I started her at Harmony 3 and we decided over one of the time skips that rather tediously roll out every single Harmony check she'd have to make for spending a day in the Flesh we'd just bump her up to Harmony 4. The pack can go multiple sessions without taking a Harmony check, and have decent dice pools with Touchstones available when they do. It's reduced Harmony to barely even feeling like a part of the game. So, taking inspiration from Orla, I've decided to try out removing dice rolls from the mechanic. From now on, if my players hit a Harmony breaking point, they break in that direction. We'll see if that swings things too far in the other direction. We implemented the idea with this session, and I also ruled that whether it was a regular breaking point or not the destruction of the quarry was a pretty big symbol of the pack choosing the Spirit over the Flesh, so they've all crept a bit closer to balance (except Orla, but she'll be back where she was originally in a day or so).<br /> <br /> Second, I would like to dig into that fight, because it gave me some food for thought. Poor Aaron continues to be on the receiving end of all the punishment in the world. He's been caught in a terrible cycle where his large amount of aggravated damage and inability, compared to his packmates, to just avoid or absorb damage means that he feels pressured to shift into <i>Gauru</i> reasonably early in each fight. And for a lot of enemies, and human soldiers particularly, their reaction to seeing the war form is to identify it as the biggest threat. That leads to him being focussed on, which can often wrap around to do more aggravated damage. At this point he has so much he's forced to stay in <i>Dalu</i> or <i>Urshul</i> to survive with one health, so we're definitely going to need to break into a reasonably lengthy time skip when the game returns. It's a harsh loop to be caught in, even if as a Rahu he should be able to take a bit more punishment than the rest of the group.<br /> <br /> I was impressed with how the Silver City hunters and their back-up performed in the fight though. Designing humans capable of taking the fight to Werewolves is a tough time, but even with losing one of the specialists before the fight even began they were winning that fight and loading the players up with Aggravated damage until Cairnbreath intervened. That intervention is something I've been sitting on for a while, I originally intended for it to happen during the conflict with the <i>Suthilu</i> where the pack killed the Scream but just... plain forgot <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/redface.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Embarrassment\" smilieid=\"2\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> The pack turned out to have that one in hand though, and luckily this fight had all the preconditions to make it work. Aed's big howl at the start of the fight explicitly alerts spirits to his location and they start showing up three turns later, which is when Cairnbreath made his intervention. There were corpses nearby (the pack spotted body bags in the quarry from some unknown conflict back when they first spied on it) too, giving him easy access to fodder.<br /> <br /> Regan was incredible during this fight. It cost her almost all her essence, but she was just the perfect Irraka. Between Fog of War and Hit and Run she was literally untouchable and extended those benefits to her packmates. Anytime someone tried to target her, she just wasn't there. She kept the big 5-6 aggravated damage rifle from ever landing a shot too, a fact her packmates were consistently thankful for. Aed might have had the big splashy moments in the fight (and against any other human on the field would have just torn them in half in one attack on the turn he went <i>Garou</i>, dealing a cool 9 damage on his attack against a pretty high defence character designed through and through to go toe to toe with Werewolves), but she kept so much pressure off her packmates backs through her actions. None of the Harbingers are outright bad at combat, but Riley has always had the smallest combat dice pools and historically struggled at times to do damage, so it was super cool to watch her perform in this fight the way she did.<br /> <br /> I can't remember if there was anything more I wanted to talk about. If I think of something I'll make another post I guess. I'm excited to see where we go when we return in six or seven weeks though, to see what lessons I and the pack take away from this session. For myself, it really solidified a few things I'd been thinking about. Now excuse me, I need to award a few points of experience to some Werewolf Hunters and count out how many months they'll be laid up in hospital before I can reasonably have them return <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-07-07T08:17:00-07:00","id":1322787,"title":"","content":"Interlude<br /> <br /> OOC: We're still at least two weeks away from being able to resume normal play, but I got to sit down with Regan's player to run a quick little mini-adventure to celebrate Regan's birthday. The players plan to skip through a month or two of in-game time next session to allow all their aggravated damage to heal, and as the only uninjured member of the pack Regan will have a bit of free reign. The events here happen about a month into the time skip, and tie into that dangling thread of Wade asking for help in the previous session. This session was loads of fun, even if it did feature a remarkable amount of me roleplaying conversations with myself as NPCs met each other for the first time and forged new relationships.<br /> <br /> It was a tradition, on Regan's birthday, for her Aunt Stephanie to bake her a cake. It was a tradition, every bit as important, for that cake it be terrible. Steph had outdone herself this year, Regan was pretty sure she'd just cracked a tooth on an uncooked grain of rice. When the two of them finished laughing at the cake, Steph did turn a little more serious. She asked Regan about her activities at the church, trying to suss out what had brought on the change. They'd never been a particularly religious household, not since Regan's grandparents had turfed out her mother for falling pregnant, and Steph was interested in sussing out what had brought on the change in her niece. Regan, not wanting to lie about something that might change how Steph saw her, instead managed to change the subject back to safer territory by asking Steph about her job at the corner store. Steph mentioned that her shifts had changed and she'd have an extra day off work each work.<br /> <br /> As Regan mentally calculated how that might need to change her behaviour with the pack, she heard the sound of raised voices outside. One high and a little shrill, the other lower and steadier. She recognised Jo's voice as the louder of the two and Maddy's as the lower. Thinking fast, she told Steph that she had a couple of friends coming over and thought that she could hear them outside. She was out the front door moments later.<br /> <br /> Regan found Jo with her back against a lamppost, hand to her chest, staring at Maddy with the wide-eyed stare of a cornered prey animal. Maddy, for her part, was apparently oblivious to the effect she was having. She was in the middle of a story of some kind, and Regan caught the words, &quot;-and she was out of school for three weeks while her hair grew ba- hey there birthday girl!&quot;<br /> <br /> It turned out that both of them had come to wish Regan a happy birthday before school. Regan introduced them to one another, and noticed that Maddy did a very poor job of hiding her put out expression when she was introduced as just a friend. Jo, on edge in Maddy's presence and clearly struggling to focus, presented Regan with a soft, carefully wrapped package. Maddy saw the exchange and pulled something out of the handbag she was carrying on one shoulder, a pile of browning papers which she planted on top of Jo's present. At a glance Regan could see that the papers were etched with the glyphs she'd previously seen used to depict the First Tongue, a series of close-knit pictograms describing it as a rite for forging alliances. She stowed the gifts in the inner pockets of her jacket and invited the other two girls inside to meet Steph. They followed her in.<br /> <br /> Steph greeted the friends enthusiastically, only embarrassing Regan a little with her exclamation of, &quot;Your friends do exist!&quot; Regan watched Maddy size up Steph before introducing herself with a big grin, leaving her family name and relationship to Regan unsaid. Steph didn't appear to recognise Maddy, but Maddy's conversation was stilted and awkward enough that Regan was considering intervening when she heard Jo gasp.<br /> <br /> A wolf, coated in muck and gore, sat panting on the floor behind them. It could have almost passed for a natural creature, until it raised pitch black eyes to Regan's and its jaw twisted in unnatural ways to speak. &quot;The Ghost Wolf fights alone, Friend of Wade. He begs your help.&quot;<br /> <br /> If Regan had any hope that Jo and Steph may not have noticed the speaking wolf, Jo's staggered, stammered response dismissed it. Her mind raced, trying to work out how to spin it, when Maddy put a hand on her shoulder and said, &quot;That sounds serious, we'd better go.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Shut up shut up shut up Maddy.&quot; Regan didn't actually say the words out loud, but she was screaming internally at her sister for shredding whatever deniability she had left. Steph and Jo backed away from the wolf, but they kept close to the other girls. Steph asked Regan if she was in trouble, Jo wanted to know what was going on, but Regan's brain was locked in panic as her two worlds collided in front of her.<br /> <br /> She tried telling them she had to run, but the two mortals wanted to follow. She tried telling them that she would explain everything later, but they were worried and didn't want her out of their sight. At last, in desperation, she bolted from the house and tried to hide amidst the gardens beyond, but Jo spotted her and led Steph towards her. At last she shifted into <i>Urhan</i> to sprint away. Lunacy seized Steph, sending her racing back to the safety of the house and bolting the door behind her. Jo resisted, watching in shock as her friend turned into a wolf in front of her. She looked over at Maddy and the talking wolf, who had followed out into the street. The wolf made for the sewers, and Regan bolted through the hole it opened into the tunnels below. Maddy clambered down, still in <i>Hishu</i>, and Regan's companions were slow enough for Jo to keep up.<br /> <br /> When the four of them were alone in the sewers, Regan shifted back. She couldn't persuade Jo to abandon her. Finally, Regan makes Jo promise to stay in the back and to run if anything starts moving towards her. Regan shifted back into <i>Urhan</i> and the group followed the wolf deeper into the sewer.<br /> <br /> Regan wasn't sure how long they'd been moving when they heard a series of snarls and thuds. She melted into the darkness, telling the others to follow slowly, and moved around the next corner to see what was happening. She could see Wade, hulked out and in <i>Dalu</i> form. He was roaring and slashing at a fast-moving shadow, a creature flowing in and out of the flickering light of the tunnel with a predator's easy grace. Wade's blows never seemed to land, and as the shadow passed above and past him Regan could see savage claw wounds open up across his body.<br /> <br /> She acted fast, lunging from the shadows to tear into the dark figure from behind. As her claws sank in, passing through the shadowflesh without any real resistance, light blazed through the tunnel. Lines of golden sunlight rippled through the creature, shadows shrinking into a series of spots marking its flesh, and Regan glimpsed its lengthy, cat-like form in its entirety. The creature sized up the group bearing down on it and chooses to flee, racing off faster than Regan felt she would be able to match.<br /> <br /> Regan introduced Maddy and Jo to Wade. The Ghost Wolf gave them each a nod, and the muck-covered wolf returned to his side. When Regan asked about the wolf, Wade mentioned that wolf spirits had been coming to help him lately. It was almost like having a pack. He then described the situation to him. The intruder in his territory had begun stalking and attacking him over the course of the last few hours. It would lunge from the shadows, strike once or twice while it had the element of surprise, and then retreat before he could do any real damage to it.<br /> <br /> Jo, meanwhile, was waiting impatiently to ask some questions. The first and most important of which was whether Werejaguars existed, a question Regan had to concede she didn't know the answer to. Maddy explained what spirits were, and they speculated that Belfast's rather lax pet laws may have allowed someone to import a jaguar as a pet and thus birthed a spiritual reflection in the Shadow. It had clearly grown since then.<br /> <br /> Wade wasn't interested so much in what the creature was or how it had come to be as he was in how to fight it. They needed to find a way to stop the hit-and-run tactics the jaguar had developed, or else they were never going to put it down. They needed to either locate its lair or find some other means to trap it. Jo, thinking back to what she knew about jaguars, confidently told the group that in Mesoamerican cultures they were commonly perceived as creatures of two worlds. They were comfortable among trees and water, could hunt at day or at night, and lived in caves and thus became associated with the dead. Regan worked out from there that it would probably be setting up shop in a Locus, and Wade told them that he knew of a Locus that might be where it had nested. He couldn't be sure though, and the location of the Locus wasn't his place to share. Regan couldn't convince him to tell her where it was, so the group had to resort to tracking the jaguar.<br /> <br /> Before they set off, however, Regan pulled out the notes given to her by Maddy. The Alliance Rite would help them to work together as they tracked the jaguar down, and it seemed easy enough to perform. She had each of them, Jo, Maddy, and Wade, go around a circle and tell lies and boasts about their achievements. Only Jo really seemed to get into the spirit of things, the two Werewolves struggled to come up with anything inventive on the spot, but Jo's help was enough for Regan to bind them together. She also asked Maddy if she was carrying anything that might help keep Jo self. Maddy dug out a stone covered in spiderweb-like cracks, calling it a &quot;Shatterstone&quot;. It would slow and debilitate an attacker if Jo held it up against the attacker's skin. After a moment's pause, Maddy rummaged around and handed Regan another two just in case.<br /> <br /> The Werewolves in concert were able to track the jaguar unerringly back to its lair. When they returned to street level, the group found themselves looking at a house warped and subsiding into the ground from water damage. The ground in front of the house was sodden mud, and the grass drowned and dead. Regan could feel the thinning of the Gauntlet as they neared the home. Maddy took hold of Jo and brought her through the Shadow, Wade and Regan following moments later.<br /> <br /> In the Shadow, the house visibly sinking into the ground. Water poured in an unending stream from its gutters, running in streams across the windows, even though there was no rain in the sky. The front door lay yawning open and they could see the floor beyond had sunk down another foot, covered now by murky water. The small group crept over the threshold and followed the trail to the right. They entered a large room, a reflection of a den or garage perhaps, where the water sank almost to their knees.<br /> <br /> Regan scanned the room, looking for any sign of the jaguar. She couldn't see it, but there were over a dozen pale objects bobbing in the water. They looked like bodies, Regan realised. Maddy walked towards the closest one and poked it, and leapt back just in time as an arm flailed out towards her. The body rolled over, revealing itself to be the corpse of an old woman. Her fingers were bent and gnarled, and she wore a mouldy old wedding dress. Worse, she was missing parts. The left side of her head, a chunk below her ribs on the right, everything below the knee. They weren't bloody wounds, just places where her body was... missing. Glancing at the other bodies, Regan could see that they were in a similar state, but also noted that the bodies were a scattered mix of different ages and genders dressed in a variety of anachronistic clothing. Some of them wore outfits that had to be centuries out of fashion.<br /> <br /> Ghosts, Maddy pronounced. Or what was left of them. Jo's claim that the jaguar was connected to the dead had not been in error, it seemed.<br /> <br /> Regan felt the air shift behind her. She was already turning when the claws took her in the shoulder, tearing deep. She heard Jo scream and water splash as the jaguar, its form clouded and dark again, landed in the water ahead of her. Wade shifted into <i>Gauru</i>, roared, and tore open a gash along the creature's flank. Again, the light flared as sunlight streamed from its form to resolve into the spotted flanks of a true jaguar. Maddy tried to bind it in place by calling up streams of water from the floor, but the spirit dodged the weaving tendrils. Jo tried to reach out and touch it with the Shatterstone as it rushed past her, but her hand was shaking so badly she missed the creature by a good foot and almost tagged Maddy.<br /> <br /> Watching the jaguar weaving in and out amidst her friends, Regan pulled out one of the Shatterstones of her own. She lunged at the creature, and saw it recoil in pain as the stone touched its flank and shattered. The shards sank into its flesh, travelling beneath the skin to erupt again from its limbs. The creature's pace slowed, enough that Jo's second attempt touched it with the stone in her hands as well. Crippled and limping, the creature whirled and ducked beneath Wade's next attack. Regan flung herself at it, but its form blurred as it raced out of reach. At that burst of speed the ghost corpses began to stir. Pale limbs reached for the jaguar, but it dodged nimbly between the grasping hands. Jo backed up towards a corner of the room, while Maddy moved to block the spirit's escape through the entrance to the room. It lunged at her, claws rending deep, and she vanished bleeding beneath the surface of the water. Wade shifted out of <i>Garou</i> and shoved the jaguar out of the way, plunging his hands beneath the surface of the water to reach for Maddy. Regan, focussing on the prey, closed the distance again to strike. Again the spirit blurred, but this time its former prey were waiting. Hands latched on to each limb as it passed, holding it helpless and struggling in place. Regan shifted to <i>Urshul</i> and launched herself onto the spirit's back. She bore it to the ground, teeth biting into its neck, and she wrenched and tore and worked her jaws deeper until the jaguar's head came apart from its body. Its form scattered, dispersing into the shadow, and Regan felt the relief of a hunt resolved.<br /> <br /> She looked back and saw Wade hauling a bloodied, but slowly waking, Maddy to safety. Jo was unharmed, though her clothes were soaked and filthy. The group fled the Locus quickly, shifting back into the world of the flesh, and Wade was able to help each of them get home safely and unseen. Jo was a little worried about what might happen when she got home, since she'd never missed a day of school like that before and they might have called her dad.<br /> <br /> Several days later, Regan woke to find the jaguar lying on the floor of her bedroom. It nudged a bracelet towards her, woven of strands of blonde and darker hair. &quot;I took this from the heads of your friends,&quot; it told her. They were unharmed, it had only removed a few strands, for it needed a vessel to hold its form during its period of servitude. By slaying it, Regan had unintentionally invoked its Ban holding it to serve its own killer. As it spoke the jaguar slowly broke apart before Regan's eyes, its essence flowing into the bracelet. It would give her the power to call on the Resonance of Loci, until its service was complete.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-07-21T03:23:06-07:00","id":1326427,"title":"","content":"Out of curiosity, how <i>do</i> you build a human to go toe to toe with Uratha?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Measure of Hope is right about everything.&quot; - Wise Old Guru<br /> <br /> Currently running an Exalted 2.5 Abyssals game in a homebrew modern shard because I value neither my time or my sanity, and I'm loving almost every minute of it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23907-measure-of-hope","name":"Measure of Hope","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"190"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-07-21T16:10:44-07:00","id":1326552,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Measure of Hope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1231714-a-time-of-trouble-werewolf-the-forsaken-actual-play?p=1326427#post1326427\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Out of curiosity, how <i>do</i> you build a human to go toe to toe with Uratha?</div> </div> </div> </div>Heh, well, most of the Silver City hunters had the Scarred merit with Lunacy as the breaking point they were immune to, which helps to avoid some early dice penalties. The rest of it (aside from all being generally competent combat characters) was about teamwork and delaying actions. None of the hunters were meant to outright win a one-on-one fight with a werewolf, but they've got a few teamwork related merits that make them quite effective at teaming up against a single wolf and inflicting a variety of debilitating conditions. The spear-wielding hunter had an assortment of abilities like Punch Drunk (being able to spend a Willpower to keep his last hit box free of damage, at the cost of upgrading a point of damage elsewhere on his track) and the ability to impale his target, which allowed him to survive multiple rounds of combat against a very angry Aed. His job in the group was basically what he did in that fight, keeping most of the threats away from his teammates while they focus down a single <i>Uratha</i> at a time. Honestly, without Regan's Fog of War Gift keeping the massive damage from the rifle from landing each round, I think the hunters would have succeeded and wiped out the entire pack in pretty short order.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-07-21T22:53:36-07:00","id":1326621,"title":"","content":"Session 29<br /> <br /> The pack settled into a routine over the course of the next month, keeping their heads down and only checking in regularly with the Cleareyes. They did learn from Eaven that the Cleareyes were adventuring into the Deep Shadow, that the Shadow in the Pure's territory did not conform closely to the real world and had many paths winding away into ancient, dark forest. The Cleareyes were hunting a creature they called a Fell Stag there, on-and-off, with some success, though the spirit was refusing to stay in the dirt where they put it. The pack had been on one such hunt when the Harbingers had tried to contact them to get access to Braghdar before the attack on the quarry, and the stag had put up quite a fight.<br /> <br /> At the end of the month, and everyone's wounds were fully healed, the pack reached out to their other neighbours to find out what had been going on. Contact with the Blackstaff wolves informed them that though the Whispers had yet to settle on a new Totem, they had gone a long way towards restoring balance in their territory. Still too many shadow spirits, but they were largely in retreat. There had been a few attacks from manic host-ridden, it sounded like the Suthilu were still lurking around their territory, but nothing the Whispers had been unable to handle.<br /> <br /> Then Steph knocked on Aaron's door. When he opened it she burst in, jabbing a finger into his chest and yelling, &quot;Werewolf!&quot; It quickly became clear that she didn't have much of a plan beyond that, but after a half-hearted attempt to put her off Aaron caved and explained a few things about Werewolves. That they were weak to silver, that Regan had inherited it from her father, and that there were other dangerous creatures out there in the night. He peppered all of this with so many warnings about the dangers of werewolf existence, including a final line ending in, &quot;Werewolves often die young,&quot; that Steph left him mid-sentence and raced home to figure out some way to protect Regan.<br /> <br /> Regan, for her part, was tentatively checking in on Jo to see whether her friend was going to reject her. Jo had been doing some research and had a checklist she wanted to run down with Regan, but seemed to have adjusted well to the Werewolf revelation. They identified that silver was a problem, wolfsbane was not, that Werewolves had a society divided up by tribe and pack, and that Regan didn't know a whole lot about Werejaguars. Jo promised to find out what she could on the latter subject.<br /> <br /> When Regan got home she found Steph lying in wait and eager to lay down some ground rules. When Regan protested that Steph didn't understand, that Regan had to spend time with her pack and go on hunts or else her spirit would degrade, Steph hesitated and then decided to invite the rest of the pack over the next day. Steph sent Regan to bed vowing to gather up all the silver in the house and put it somewhere out of the way, and not taking too kindly to Regan's muttered, &quot;But we don't <i>have</i> any silver in the house.&quot;<br /> <br /> Steph called up Aaron the next day, a Sunday, and asked him to grab &quot;all the other Werewolves&quot; and bring them over so she could lay out some ground rules. Aed wasn't sure if Steph had worked out that he and Orla were werewolves, so he had Aaron go on his own. It turned out Steph absolutely had worked it out, and she sent Aaron away again saying she wanted to speak to everyone. The pack agreed to show up for dinner on Tuesday.<br /> <br /> Until then, Steph told Regan as she busted out the three old, battered board games in the house, Regan was most definitely grounded.<br /> <br /> OOC: This was both a shorter session, and pretty much dominated by a few lengthy conversations. The pack's finding its feet within the new status quo, which promises to be awfully inconvenient for everyone. And, well, that was kind of the point of having a teenager in the pack, so mission accomplished there.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-18T20:53:43-07:00","id":1331544,"title":"","content":"Apologies for the late posting on this write-up. Hopefully I manage to catch it all.<br /> <br /> Session 30<br /> <br /> The pack was determined to liberate Regan from the oppression of her parental figure, and each packmate formed a different plan revolving around gifts for Steph to be presented at Tuesday's dinner. Ultimately it was Aed who blew everyone out of the water, rocking up on the doorstep last but sporting the most expensive bottle of wine Steph had ever seen. Over the course of dinner and drinks Aed and Aaron went through much of the minutiae of Werewolf existence, but the point they rammed home repeatedly was that Regan would have to hunt to keep her nature in balance. They emphasised the misery and lack of control that could occur in a wolf deprived of the hunt, and managed to persuade Steph to give ground on her demands. An initial ban on school night hunts was even walked back to a 10pm curfew, though Steph told Aed in no uncertain terms that he was responsible for every scratch Regan might suffer on a hunt.<br /> <br /> Regan spent most of this meal sitting miserably on a kitchen chair and shooting off mental messages with her Pack Awareness Gifts whenever they made some kind of obvious blunder, like even mentioning the possibility of physical injury. But, by the end, she had an aunt who was both aware of her nature and seemed to have a, somewhat newborn perhaps, understanding of what Regan needed to be allowed to do in order to thrive.<br /> <br /> Later that week, the pack convened. Aed's ever-growing primal strength was driving the need for the pack to hunt more frequently, and they wanted to know what Cairnbreath had been up to. If the god of death was still floating around in the Flesh, they would have to do something about it even if none of them could work out what that could possibly be. Since a violent confrontation would probably be a bad idea, the Harbingers decided to drag along a gift. They figured that the infant Murder Crow they had drive from Arthur Cosgrave's body would allow them to, ahem, kill two birds with one stone, and decided to track it down.<br /> <br /> Regan suggested that the pack meet up with the <i>duguthim</i> she'd encountered on Orchardville Crescent, since the powerful Claimed had made a point of nurturing weak Flesh-side spirits. The pack arranged with the Silent Sons to cross over to the eastern side of Andersonstown, and made their way to Orchardville Crescent. They noted the dozens of crows sitting on lampposts and poles on the street, watching the three Werewolves in eerie silence. The door to the home swung open of its own accord as they approached, and the voice of the Claimed within sung out to them to enter.<br /> <br /> Entering the lounge room where Regan had spent her time at her last visit, the pack found the place basically unchanged from how Regan had described it. Aaron made sure to look at each of the packs' reflections in the mirrors. He saw himself with a vicious, feral cast to his features, not unlike the slightly wolfish <i>Hishu</i> form adopted by Maddy. Aed, for his part, looked younger, run down, with a visibly broken nose. Regan sported the wicked scar she'd seen in her own reflection, and Orla displayed no reflection at all.<br /> <br /> The Claimed met them there. Since the pack realised that Regan had never asked her name, they all made a point of introducing themselves. The immense, overweight woman called herself Adele, but told the wolves that they could refer to her as <i>Urkerrigan</i> if that made them more comfortable. When the pack brought up the Murder Crow, Adele was visibly uncomfortable with the idea of turning over a small spirit to them. Aed promised her that the little spirit would not come to harm, and cited the previous dealings in good faith that the pack had undertaken with Flesh-side spirits in the past. Their dealings with the Broken appeared to be what persuaded her, and Adele led them to the leafless tree in her backyard and reached into a hollow in the tree. Her hands emerged carefully cradling the Murder Crow, it blinking bleary half-panicked eyes at the sight of the waiting wolves. It didn't appear to have grown since they last set eyes on it, still appearing to be a newborn bird with a head too large to be supported by its long, weak neck. Its skin was slick with reddish fluids, with the first signs of feathers beginning to break through.<br /> <br /> The Harbingers returned to their own territory with the Murder Crow in hand, thanking Adele for her aid. The small murder spirit remained seated in Aed's cupped hands during the journey, watching passers-by with unblinking intensity. The feel of its skin, at once both slick and prickly, was unpleasant and made him eager to dispose of the spirit and wash his hands of it both literally and figuratively.<br /> <br /> The pack entered the Shadow and set off towards the quarry, the last place they had seen Cairnbreath. They found that it had been filled in Shadowside as well, but that the essence of death had pervaded the site. Where the quarry had once been was now a quagmire of dark liquids and thick, low-lying mist tinged faintly green. Humanoid silhouettes could be seen drifting in the mist, but they did not respond to Aaron's hails. He considered making his way into the fog to interact with the spirits more directly, but after two steps into the quagmire and feeling it suck at his paws he decided against it.<br /> <br /> Regan's ability to pick up a trail regardless of the passage of time stood the pack in good stead there on the edge of the quagmire, as she was able to locate the path Cairnbreath had taken after the attack on the Silver City outpost. The good news was that the death god had clearly returned to the Shadow after the attack. The bad news was that the trail headed west, further than they could go with her new curfew. The pack travelled as far as they could, drawing close to the western border of their territory, before Aed and Regan broke off to get Regan home rather than risk breaking Steph's rules at the first opportunity. The rest of the pack remained with the Murder Crow over the course of the night.<br /> <br /> The Crow decided to spend the night speaking, since it had a captive audience. It seemed to think sitting around all night was a waste of time, especially when they had passed so many potential victims back in the world of the Flesh. To Aaron's horror it was able to describe each pedestrian it had spotted from the window of the car during the drive from Adele's place to Aed's, in detail, and then regaled the wolves with detailed descriptions of how they could murder those pedestrians. A lot of the ideas were detailed, inventive, and deeply unpleasant. Neither Orla nor Aaron had an opportunity to sleep that night.<br /> <br /> The next day, when Regan was finished with school, she met up with Aed and they sped in <i>Urhan</i> form to return to the others and pick up the trail again. Cairnbreath's tracks led past the western border of the Harbingers' territory, into the realm of the deeper Shadow. To the best of their knowledge the Harbingers were entering a region no one claimed, too far north for the Cleareyes and not yet far enough west to have reached the Predator Kings of the Fulcrum. The Shadow here seemed divorced from the real world in a sense, like they were progressing further from the Flesh rather than travelling in any real world direction. Trees rose up around them, growing steadily taller until they were enshrouded in primeval forest. These trees were preposterously tall and large, a canopy hidden beyond the clouds and trunks as thick as city blocks. Luna was hidden entirely by their immensity, leaving the wolves and the Murder Crow to travel in darkness. From time to time they could hear the sound of something large moving in the darkness, and once heard something like howls far off in the distance.<br /> <br /> Cairnbreath's trail followed a small trail through the wood. From time to time they found signs that they were on the right path, whether it be a discarded rifle or a human arm lying by the side of the path, showing signs that scavengers had been at it since it had been lost by its owner. At last they could see a glimmer of moonlight up ahead. Aaron led the way, stepping out into the clearing ahead of his pack. He was greeted by the sight of an immense pile of bundled sticks. It was the size of a hill, branches as long and thick as his arm tied together into bunches by thorned vine and piled on top of one another with no clear order. British soldiers, or what was left of them, stood on guard in a ring around the clearing. They didn't seem to react to the appearance of the wolves.<br /> <br /> Looking back at the strange mound, Aaron found his eyes creating patterns and images in the mess. That pile there looked almost like a clawed arm, but when he blinked he couldn't see the shape again. There, just shy of the top, was a collection of shattered sticks that almost looked like a grinning mouth beneath two dark holes for eyes, but he couldn't see the face a second later no matter how hard he tried. As he was staring there, the billowing of a dark miasma rose amidst the top of the pile as Cairnbreath crested the mound.<br /> <br /> &quot;You've come a long way, wolves.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-29T22:50:30-07:00","id":1333222,"title":"","content":"Session 31<br /> <br /> With great ceremony, Aed greeted the titanic death spirit and beckoned Aaron to step forward with the Murder Crow. The Harbingers explained that they had sought Cairnbreath out wishing to present him with a new, founding member of his court. The great skeleton, shrouded in miasma, carefully plucked the Murder Crow from Aaron's hands. Aaron felt his skin go numb where the bony fingertips made contact with his flesh. For its part, the little murder spirit was clearly cowed in the presence of this great spirit. Cairnbreath cradled it gently, and withdrew to the great mound of sticks. Boasting, he claimed that soon even his old enemy would rise in service. They stood amidst the corpse of Bristlesticks, the ruler of Cairnbreath's rival court in the time of the Wolf In Golden Chains, and Cairnbreath turned his attention back to the mound and plunged his hands in deep.<br /> <br /> Having confirmed that Cairnbreath was, at least, on the right side of the Gauntlet, the Harbingers made to withdraw. Aaron, however, came forward in supplication. He asked Cairnbreath if he could learn the Gift of Death. The request barely drew a glance from the spirit, but its response was clear. &quot;If you can rest it from my flesh, you are welcome to it.&quot;<br /> <br /> Aaron swallowed, shifted into <i>Garou</i>, and plunged his claws into the miasma that coated Cairnbreath's form. The sickly fog was drawn to his flesh, causing it to bubble and burst and run almost as quickly as his regeneration could heal it. New scars formed amiss the mess, carving the new Gift, when a flash of movement blurred fast too quickly to track. Aaron staggered backward as he felt the new spiritual channels open, giving him the magic that he sought. The red fog of the war form receded as he shifted back, his wounds closed over, but he paused in confusion as he took in his packmates. Regan sat proudly in <i>Urhan</i> in front of them, tail wagging, and one of Cairnbreath's rib bones gripped proudly in her jaws. The spirit made no comment, and the pack retreated with their task done.<br /> <br /> Behind them, as they left, they glimpsed the great mound of broken sticks and twigs stir for the briefest of moments.<br /> <br /> That night, on a routine patrol of the territory, Aaron found the scent of a pack of wolf spirits that had crossed the Harbingers' territory on the Flesh side of the Gauntlet. It had happened recently, in the last few days, and he made note to bring Regan out to track them the next day.<br /> <br /> For his part, Aed spent his time after the hunt for Cairnbreath relaxing with Lizzie. The primal energies within him had not only rendered him immune to alcohol, but had forced the growing realisation that even downing poisons wasn't going to give him much of a buzz. It was still kind of funny watching Lizzie trying to keep up though, and she let something slip amidst the drinking. Her hunters were convinced that there was a rogue Werewolf in the area. A particularly gristly murder just north of Andersonstown had corresponded with the discovery of blood paw prints, <i>and</i> the murder had fallen on the full moon.<br /> <br /> The pack, of course, knew that the full moon element was probably irrelevant, unless the murder represented a new <i>Rahu</i> going through the First Change. It was more likely that the murder had been connected in some fashion to the wolf spirit pack that Aaron had stumbled across. They decided to track down the spirits, but Regan's nose found that the trail descended into the sewers. Regan sent a messenger Lune to ask Wade to meet them at the site in a couple of hours, rather than trespass without permission. When the time came, they entered the sewers.<br /> <br /> It was only after they reached the bottom that they realised they had left the torches in the car. Aed doubled back for them, but as he was returning the others spotted a hulking figure approaching, and red eyes shining in the dark. &quot;Why did you have to come?&quot; growled out a feral voice, and then Aaron found himself beset by a raging, roaring blur of slashing claws swathed in white fur. He shifted into <i>Garou</i> in response, already feeling wounds too deep to heal immediately opening in his body, and howled out to the others. Regan melted into the shadows, circling around to look for an opening on their attacker, just as Aed landed in the sewers with a thud and torchlight illuminated the bloody scene.<br /> <br /> Wade was locked in a grapple with Aaron, both wolves having taken the war form. Their claws tore into each other, but draped across Wade's head and shoulders was a wolf's skin of white fur that warded off Aaron's blows like steel armour. Aed saw wolf spirits begin to emerge from the walls as the fighting grew, and raced through the gauntlet of snapping teeth. He shifted into <i>Urshul</i>, barreling into Wade's and counting on his remaining packmates to keep the wolf spirits off his back.<br /> <br /> The Harbingers fought together, moving in greater harmony than they had displayed in any of their previous battles. Aaron and Aed each seized one of Wade's arms and began to pull, raking great wounds in his flesh and limbs. The strength of the two wolves should have overpowered Wade easily, but a demonic strength was in him and he found openings to rip and tear into both his assailants. The wolf spirits circled, hunting for openings, but Regan kept them distracted with lightning strikes from the darkness and Orla led others away from her packmates. As the corpus of the spirits began to disperse into the air, Aaron and Aed wore Wade down even through the <i>Garou</i>'s unnatural regeneration. As his body began to come apart, the skin draped about his shoulders tore away. There was a moment when it seemed to take form into a great, white wolf, connected to Wade's body by strings of blood and tendon, and its eyes fell upon Aaron.<br /> <br /> &quot;This will be remembered.&quot;<br /> <br /> It vanished through the wall of the tunnel. As it disappeared, Wade collapsed. His form shrunk, returning to <i>Hishu</i>, and between them the Harbingers realised that his body was coming apart at the seams. The enhanced fortitude gifted by his Claiming would rapidly reverse now that the spirit had separated from the flesh, and Wade might not survive his wounds when it did. They hauled his unconscious body back to the car, taking care not to be seen, and raced him to Aed's home and through the Gauntlet to Aaron's glade. There, they called up Silkenspun and paid the spirit a great bounty of essence to keep Wade suspended on the cusp of life and death until his body could find the time to heal its wounds naturally.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-25T06:56:36-07:00","id":1337805,"title":"","content":"Ok, so, full disclosure, the reason I haven't updated in the last few weeks is because Session 32 happened when I was pretty exhausted and sleep deprived, and then we had a kind of silly sequence of events when we were skipping over the events of an in-game weekend and the players were telling me what they were doing on that weekend but it all ended up happening in... sort of reverse chronological order. The net effect of which is that I honestly couldn't tell you exactly what happened in that session, or in what order, with any degree of certainty. It didn't help exactly that it was a session with a lot of little character roleplay moments, but not a lot of direct progress on any of the major problems facing the pack. Since then we've had another session, which helped me pool my memory with that of my players and figure out exactly what took place, but it's still going to be a bit vague and muddled initially.<br /> <br /> Sessions 32 and 33<br /> <br /> It was clear to the pack that Wade would take a few weeks to recover from his ordeal. Orla and Regan wanted to expand the pack's arsenal of spiritual weaponry, in preparation for future conflicts, while Aed and Aaron wanted to get to work draining the death essence in the quarry. For several days, the packmates each focussed on their own independent activities. Orla crafted several new jars for capturing spirits, ranging from a flimsy construction with near-faded runes to a vessel of clouded glass that she was confident could hold anything shy of an <i>Incarnae</i>. Regan, for her part, kept an eye out for signs of spiritual activity that the Harbingers could use as justification for subjecting a spirit to a period of penance as a Fetish.<br /> <br /> Aaron and Aed were each busy approaching the problem of the quarry from a different direction. Aaron took advantage of his congregation and the parish council, working to convince them that the recent violence at the quarry indicated a community sorely in need of healing. Aed sought out Martin Covoney, and spread much the same message outside the confines of the church. The two of them pressed for the establishment of a memorial site, dedicated to the memory of victims of the Troubles, to be administered jointly by the city and St Hubert's parish council.<br /> <br /> To further their agenda, Aed sought out the Weeping Tree. The <i>duguthim</i> agreed to a pact, allowing itself to be relocated into the quarry where it could aid in making the memorial a place of melancholy and memory. The pack hoped that doing so would reduce the influence of Cairnbreath's death resonance, and since the tree currently served as a memorial for the site of civilian victims of local violence it would be an appropriate addition to the memorial.<br /> <br /> Regan's quest to locate a spirit acting outside its bounds came to a head shortly after the first groundwork for the memorial had been laid. She located the tracks of a white ice spider, a manifestation of winter driven across the Gauntlet by its hunger. The arachnid spirits weren't uncommon in the area, and Orla knew that they were liable to cause problems in early spring. Traditionally, they would slumber in winter when the cold essence pervaded the world, but would rise to hunt as the ice retreated. The pack tracked the rogue spirit to the slopes of Black Mountain, just shy of the radio station that served as the Old Man's silver crown. They found that the spirit had laid an ambush, but it was no match for a pack of four werewolves. Orla did not think it strong enough to be worth the use of any of her spirit jars, so the pack tore the spider apart rather than bind it to their service and let its essence flee to reform.<br /> <br /> Having returned to town, the pack decides that with the groundwork laid it is time to declare a hunt against the death essence in their territory. No sooner had they done so, however, Aaron received a phone call from Arthur saying that he needed to meet them at a location on the edge of the Whispers' territory. Aaron agreed to head there as soon as he could, but after alerting his pack they decided to perform the Message Rite to alert the Whispers of their intentions. Though they had practiced the Rite many times, this time the Lunes seemed reluctant to answer the call. By the time they were able to make their wishes clear, half an hour had passed and the pack was running late for the meeting.<br /> <br /> The Harbingers arrived at the meeting place to find that there was no sign of Arthur. They did pick up traces of his scent nearby, however. Aaron and Regan ended up having to discreetly shift into <i>Urhan</i> in order to follow it, moving quickly and quietly adjacent to the road to avoid detection while Aed followed in their wake in his car.<br /> <br /> The trail led them to a dingy little pizza parlour called Pizza-2-Go. From outside they could see that the counter was staffed by a single bored, skinny employee, and that was a police car parked around the back of the building. The pack discussed their approach, Regan ducked around the back to confirm that Arthur's scent was on the police car alongside the smell of several others, but ultimately Aed took matters into his own hands and just walked in through the front door. The employee behind the counter failed to acknowledge his presence as Aed, and then the rest of the Harbingers, looked around the room. There was a large, heavy metal door at the back of the room that appeared to be locked. Aed glanced at the employee and told him, quite truthfully, that he was looking for a friend and wondered if he might be inside. When Aed asked for the key, the employee nodded listlessly and gestured for the cash register. Sure enough, Aed dug out a key and opened the door.<br /> <br /> The smell that assaulted them from beyond the door was foul beyond measure, only the pizza shop employee appearing unmoved by the sudden assault. Looking inside, the Harbingers could see the suspended torsos of three men dangling from the ceiling. Nothing of the men remained below the waist, entrails and thick, viscous black blood dripped onto the floor from ragged wounds at the base of their torsos, and they were held aloft by heavy chains. Thick tubes of black rubber wound in and out of the skin of the men, and the pack noted that two of them were wearing the uniform of the Ulster Constabulary. Thankfully, none of the men were Arthur.<br /> <br /> One by one, the pack entered the room as quietly as they could. Aaron slipped in the congealed matter on the floor and brushed against one of the suspended bodies, which jerked and flailed for an instant. With dawning horror, the wolves realised that they could see movement in the chests of the men. They were all alive and breathing.<br /> <br /> As quietly as they could, Aaron and Aed worked together to brace and lift one of the bodies from their imprisonment. Aed grew into <i>Dalu</i> and tore through the tubing connecting the man to the roof, and a few moments later they heard the man gurgle and saw him go still. A mercy, Aed figured, and he repeated the same process with each of the other bodies to let them pass on.<br /> <br /> From where they were, the pack could see a couple of doors leading to smaller rooms breaking off from this slaughterhouse. Aaron and Aed went to the two closest and opened them both on the count of three. A ringing alarm tore through the air as Aaron wrenched open his door to reveal a room plastered with the furred, unmoving bodies of a host of oversized bats. The noise was loud enough to leave Aaron partially deafened as he slammed the door shut, and certainly loud enough to wake the much large bat-hybrid creature that filled most of the room beyond Aed's door.<br /> <br /> The Harbingers shifted into their combat forms, a mix of <i>Urshul</i> and <i>Dalu</i>, as they piled into the room to confront the rising <i>Suthilu</i> hybrid. This creature was a little larger than the Scream of Slaughter, and its response to the incursion led it to bellow out a screech that sent the wolves reeling. Aaron's partial deafness actually spared him from the worst of the damage, and he and Aed tore into the creature. Their movements were hampered, however, as their packmates joined them and more and more large, furry bodies were crammed into a room hardly large enough to contain the hybrid they were fighting.<br /> <br /> And then, suddenly, the room had yet another occupant. Perched on the remains of an old work desk in the corner, the realised that they could see a gaunt figure apparently in the form of a human man. The new arrival called out in the First Tongue, beseeching the darkness to hold at bay the foul light of Luna, and Regan and Aaron felt a chill in the depths of their souls. In their mind's eye they could each see a dark moon rising to blot out the first, a violent assault on their spirits that severed the flow of essence to their Moon Gifts. Regan tore into the man in return, lunging in her <i>Urshul</i> form and knocking him to the ground while her packmates continued their assault on the large hybrid. Aed locked the massive beast in a grapple, overpowering the larger of the beasts with the strength and power of his own <i>Urshul</i> form, while Aed tore into its flanks.<br /> <br /> The strange human snarled at Regan, struggling to regain his feet. The darkness of the void began to spill from the wounds in his back. The shadows formed into the shape of two great wings, shaped like a butterfly's or a moth's. They spread and beat, just once, and the wolves felt darkness settle across their eyes. Between the hybrid's screams and this new attack, most of the pack was both deaf and blind. Aed was able to fight the hybrid to a standstill, neither able to make ground against the other, while his packmates quested in the darkness for the human plaguing them. Aaron was able to pierce through the spiritual veil cast across his soul and call upon his Auspice's power to ignore the blindness and deafness for a time, and could make out the man limping away from the fight. There were too many large bodies between them for him to easily reach his prey, however, so he turned on the hybrid. An instant later, he witnessed the darkness reach out to enfold the human, and he vanished from the scene.<br /> <br /> Between them, the Harbingers were then able to tear down the hybrid in reasonably short order. Only Aaron had the ability to spot and kill the <i>Suthilu</i> shard as it wriggled free from the fast-disintegrating body of its host, picking it out amidst the teaming bats that suddenly filled the small space. His Gifts sealed all the doors and entrances within the building, and he was able to destroy the <i>Suthilu</i> permanently.<br /> <br /> Then came the hammering at the door to their room. A voice declared that they were the police, but only Aed had not been deafened in the fight. He shifted into a form capable of human speech and tried to tell them that they were blind and in trouble, only for his words to be greeted by gunfire. Bullets punched through the door and into his frame, adding to the wounds sustained in the fight. He roared at them to stop firing, but the attack continued. Aed threw himself prone as the door began to come apart, and bullets began to wing his wounded packmates around him. Desperate, Aed shifted into <i>Garou</i> and threw himself, blind, at the source of the attack.<br /> <br /> Without eyesight, the next few seconds were a blur of flesh beneath claws and the sound of screaming. One of the attackers made a run for their car, but Aed's ears heard the movement and he tore through the side of the building and then the car itself in a screech of rent metal. When Aed shifted back, slowly finding his vision returning, he could make out the dismembered bodies of several more police scattered around the room and a hole in the wall leading to the car park where the ruins of the police car could be seen. Each body bore a large hole in its chest in addition to the damage done by his teeth and claws, a place where something had burst out from within the rib cage. It didn't take a great leap of logic to determine that <i>Suthilu</i> had been possessing the bodies of these constables. Each of the dead men had signs of gnawing around their lips and fingers, but any further details were obscured by the damage dealt by Aed in his blind lashing out.<br /> <br /> As the pack recovered from the encounter, they heard the door open and the Pizza-2-Go employee stepped into the room. He stared blankly at the carnage in front of him and left, only to return moments later with a mop. Without any regard for the impossibility of the task, he began to try and mop up the gore matting the room's floor.<br /> <br /> The Harbingers turned their attention back to locating Arthur. A quick sniff of the air told Regan that the alarmed room that Aaron had tried to enter was where Arthur's trail left. When they opened the door again, they found the the host of bats inside had vanished. It was possible not to make out a hatch in the roof of that room, which led into a crawlspace. Aaron leapt up and found a bound and gagged Arthur lying mostly unharmed within. Aed shifted into <i>Dalu</i> and sheered through the manacles on Arthur's wrists and ankles, and they made for the exit together.<br /> <br /> Driving away from the dilapidated pizza shop, Arthur explained that a couple of weeks back a gaunt homeless man had been brought into the station. He had been painting on walls in his own faeces and bodily fluids, ranging from gibberish and nonsense signs to the occasional snatch of discernible language. The few messages that had made sense had been variations on the phrase, &quot;The colony wakes&quot;. A colleague of Arthur noted afterwards that the man was never properly processed, the paperwork pertaining to his arrest went missing, and the man himself vanished from the cells the day he was brought in. Arthur agreed to help look into it, but they found themselves stonewalled by the arresting officers. And then, a few days ago, Arthur's colleague failed to show up to work.<br /> <br /> It was hard for the Harbingers to feel too much of a sense of victory, listening to the story. They had hurt the <i>Suthilu</i> again, but they still could not determine what the Hosts were trying to accomplish. Another powerful Hybrid lay dead, but the other Shards present had all escaped. Progress, to be sure, but too slow for their liking.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-05T21:42:42-08:00","id":1346359,"title":"","content":"Oh boy, been quite a while since the last update. For those interested, university has hit a few members of the group particularly hard this year, so we've mostly kept the game on hold for the last few weeks through exams and the end of semester. Our most recent session was quite short, so this recap isn't going to look like much, but in a nutshell:<br /> <br /> Session 34<br /> <br /> The pack found themselves caught in a bind, after returning Arthur to his place of rest. They had a <i>Suthilu</i> problem to deal with, the Wolf in Golden Chains stirring up trouble in their territory as evidenced by Wade's Claiming, and they were currently bound to a hunt that was truthfully not their highest priority. Luna's silver chains allowed only enough slack to get Arthur to safety, before turning their attention back to the problem of Death Resonance infesting the quarry. This was a hunt they had planned to take their time with, slowly reshaping the essence through actions in the material world and exerting their influence on the local community. It was going to need more drastic measures now.<br /> <br /> Orla suggested that they try and have Cairnbreath take ownership of the death spirits in the spirit-bog where the quarry had once been Shadowside. The others weren't exactly sure if that would help matters, they had nothing much to trade with and Cairnbreath might just decide to settle in a place so attuned to his nature. Regan wanted to hunt the death spirits and dispatch them, or use Orla's jars to seal them away. Aaron, however, recalled that he had ever so briefly met another who might be able to help them. The Pack knew that among the loremasters of Belfast, two names stood out. Reynard of the <i>Kallakalat</i>, Regan's grandfather, and the veteran <i>Cahalith</i> of the Symphony Cascade. Aaron had encountered him when the Hunters in Darkness had gathered for that unfortunate communal hunt that had cost Aaron his eyes, but hadn't really spoken to him. If anyone knew of a shortcut or cheat to their current woes, it would be him.<br /> <br /> Regan invoked the Message rite, dispatching a tiny Lune made of musical ringing notes. The Lune's message was simple, containing Aaron's phone number and an entreaty to please call them at the earliest convenience. Aaron returned home to wait by the phone. When it rang, the speaker on the other end introduced himself in a raspy voice as Lawrence of the Symphony Cascade. Aaron explained the situation, and after some consideration Lawrence described to him a Rite that would allow them to transform the essence with the help of a spirit opposed to the nature of the resonance. They would need to strike some kind of bargain for it be fulfilled. Aaron then hung up the phone, the Harbingers having gained a new debt but having the tool they needed.<br /> <br /> The obvious spirit of choice was Silkenspun. With Wade beginning to stir and the healing spirit's days of vigilance over him nearly at an end, it didn't take much more than a token offering of essence from the ever-persuasive Aed to convince Silkenspun to help them in their endeavour. The Pack waded into the swamp, the spectral shadows of the area's death spirits kept at bay by the imposing figures of Hope and Aed, as the Rite began. Silkenspun's nature was woven into the fabric of the quarry, causing the marshy ground to bubble and boil as the death essence dissolved. As the death spirits scattered, unwilling to fight the <i>Uratha</i>, the pack felt the distant approval of their unearthly mother. The hunt resolved, albeit with a price still to be paid down the line, the pack could at last turn their attention to the more pressing problems at hand.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-03T21:17:22-08:00","id":1372611,"title":"","content":"Ok, this has been a long, long time coming. Work and studies, holidays and an increasingly busy schedule, and a few other personal things have all made getting the group together really, really hard. But we've changed the venue we play at, moved the game to a fortnightly one rather than a weekly one (let's be honest, it's been more consistently a fortnightly game even when it was going pretty regularly than it ever was a weekly one), and back to playing Werewolf again! Fingers crossed that we can keep this schedule going a little more consistently, because there's some good, juicy stuff on the horizon for this campaign. Without further ado:<br /> <br /> Session 35<br /> <br /> Their territory healed and their borders reinforced, our wolves had a moment of respite from the trials they had been facing. The pack was tense, wary, waiting to see if Cairnbreath reacted to their cleansing of his essence, but no retaliation came. Instead, the brief peace was disturbed by the stirring of another. Wade had recovered from his ordeal, and they visited him Shadowside in Aaron's Glade to listen to his story. There was a brief squabble within the pack on the cusp of the Glade, with Aaron refusing to allow Aed into the Glade so long as the Blood Talon was accompanied by his usual entourage of brawling spirits of violence. The little creatures were clearly annoyed to be told to remain outside of the Glade and turned on one another in their frustration. The pack entered the Glade to the sound of an escalating brawl behind them.<br /> <br /> Wade was disorientated, confused, and deeply apologetic when the pack revealed that he had turned on him. His recollection of the events leading up to the fight was scattershot, but together they pieced together the chronology of events. Wade had long kept company with the many wolf spirits dwelling in Belfast's underground, they had been his main source of allies as a Ghost Wolf claiming territory in the city's sewers. In recent weeks a particularly strong spirit had approached him, offering to join its strength to his to protect him. The spirit in question, a white wolf covered in golden bands, had previously seemed confined to the lower depths of the earth, but had begun to move about above ground and suggested that it might give Wade the strength to do so too. Wade had accepted the offer, presumably, though he couldn't actually recall the moment when he had done so, and his memories ever since were filled with growing anger, protracted blackouts, and the sensation of strength.<br /> <br /> It seemed clear to the pack that the spirit of the Wolf in Golden Chains had attempted to Claim Wade, only separating when they had been on the verge of killing him. They tried to help Wade piece together his memories and track his movements while he had been Claimed, and it became clear that the Wolf had been tracking the <i>Suthilu</i> through Belfast's tunnels. The Bat Hosts had not been his target, however, for the Wolf had avoided direct confrontation and instead kept watch for another drawn to the same target. He had been waiting for an angel.<br /> <br /> The pack's collective minds recalled their encounter with the creature named Michael that had come to them during their raid on the <i>Suthilu</i> nest in Andersonstown. They asked Wade what the Wolf had wanted with the angel, but the Ghost Wolf could only recall hate and the memory of the angel vanishing before the Wolf could enact its plan. The pack decided that they had best reach out to Michael, and see if he could shed any light on the situation.<br /> <br /> As they were leaving, Wade in tow, he told them that he could remember meeting with several other Werewolves while he had been Claimed. Other friends of the Wolf in Golden Chains, he believed. He couldn't recall the faces of the others, though he remembered that a pair, one male and one female, had stood out as the others had been met with singly. He was worried that this meant that the Wolf had other allies out there in Belfast, allies who might themselves prove suitable hosts for the Wolf's spirit.<br /> <br /> The pack return to the mortal world to gather supplies. The following day was Sunday, and that seemed like the best time to summon an angel whether he be of God or Helios. They returned to the Shadow to try and summon Michael after Aaron had finished delivering Mass and had foisted his remaining duties onto his Deacon. The tricky part was that they weren't really clear what kind of entity Michael was. If he were a true angel, then Aaron felt that they could only pray and hope that he responded. He could be a spirit, perhaps an agent of Helios, in which case it was possible that they could entice him with a rite involving the appropriate essence. Or perhaps Regan could reach out with her Message Rite and see if Michael, whether Angel of God, Spirit, or something else, could be reached by the Lunes at her beck and call. <br /> <br /> Naturally, the pack decided to go for all three. Regan and Orla prepared the Message Rite, while Aed carefully arranged a large bronze sun sculpture that he'd picked up as memorabilia... somewhere in his travels. Aaron attempted to lead the pack in prayer, kneeling at the altar while Helios bore down on them all through the shattered stained glass window of the Shadow Church. A few mirrors they'd been able to grab helped fill the space with sunlight as the different chants in Latin and the First Tongue mingled together.<br /> <br /> The response was swift. Blazing light filled the entrance of the church, causing the paint to bubble and peel and wisps of smoke to rise from the wooden supports. The light turned inwards, boiling away into the shape of a man, at last resolving into a beautiful being of pale skin, sculpted muscles, and golden hair. Bronze wings spread from his back, and the pack could hear the whir of gears and cogs as the pinions splayed out. The angel Michael turned his attention to the wolves, and asked why they had called him.<br /> <br /> Aed took the lead, explaining Wade's plight and presenting the Ghost Wolf to the angel. He explained that the Wolf in Golden Chains was trying to break free, and was clearly an enemy of Michael's. When asked if he would help them track the Wolf, however, the angel refused. His mission was to destroy the <i>Suthilu</i>, and he intended to see his duty completed without bothering with such distractions. The Bat Hosts were on the run, having been beaten back in Blackstaff, Andersonstown, and the northern shores, and now they fled back to their warren to attempt to waken their sleeping brethren with as great a display of bloodshed as they could muster. Michael bade the Werewolves gather all the strength they could for a crusade against the vile creatures. At Aed's prodding, Wade offered his own services to Michael directly, and the angel accepted. The two departed, with Michael pronouncing that he would call on the others for the final assault soon.<br /> <br /> The pack returned to the material world and held a quick discussion before they departed to bed down for the night. Aed was disinclined to summon any help at all for the battle with the bats, arguing that the pack would gain more Glory if they were to end the threat themselves. Regan argued that it seemed <i>more</i> glorious to fight at the head of an army led by an angel, and wanted to summon every wolf they could. Aaron and Orla sided with Regan, for varying reasons, and Aed acquiesced. They began to reach out to the other packs they knew to prepare them for the need for battle.<br /> <br /> Regan needed to head home early to meet Steph's curfew. It was as she was approaching the house, however, that a snake-like coil of moonlight reached out for her. The Lune, in Maddy's voice, whispered a panicked message that the <i>Kallakalat</i> were approaching the Harbinger's territory. Halfway through her speech she seemed to hear someone approach and switched to a more formal speech, requesting permission of behalf of her pack to enter the territory in pursuit of their prey. Panicking herself at the prospect of her father and other extended relatives running around Andersonstown, Regan agreed and then doubled back as quickly as she could to Aed's house.<br /> <br /> Once there, she explained to her packmates what had happened, and called Steph to ask permission to spend the night at Aed's. Steph reluctantly agreed, just the once. Unfortunately, both beds and couches were all occupied between Orla, Aed, Lizzie, and even a makeshift bed set aside for Wade as a promise, but Aed dug out an old dog bed and had Regan sleep in <i>Urhan</i> to save space.<br /> <br /> Aaron was the only member of the pack to leave the house that evening. On his return home, he smelled smoke and caught the sound of running feet. He followed the sound, stumbling across the pursuit in action. A mortal, near invisible smoke spirits clinging to his limbs and streaming out behind him, raced through the streets. Maddy and a girl who could only be her sister followed on foot. The unfamiliar girl's blouse was covered in red, though she bore no immediately apparent wounds. Aaron watched them go without intervening directly, and returned quickly to his church to check that the manifestation of Michael spirit-side had not caused any undue damage. To the contrary, the quality of light within seemed a little brighter than normal, but no damage had been done to the structure.<br /> <br /> And with that, the final member of the Harbingers settled down to rest before their crusade.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}